B7. (a) \(10^{-10} - \tfrac12 10^{-20} = 9.9999999995\times10^{-11}\) (the next Taylor term, \(x^3/3\), is \(3\times10^{-31}\), far too small to matter). (b) The stored \(1 + x\) can be off by up to half a gap, \(1.1\times10^{-16}\). Relative to \(x = 10^{-10}\) that is \(1.1\times10^{-6}\), so only about 6–7 significant digits of \(x\) (and so of \(\log(1+x)\)) survive. R gives log(1 + 1e-10) \(= 1.000000082690371\times10^{-10}\): correct to 7 digits. (c) \(10^{-17} &lt; \varepsilon/2\), so \(1 + 10^{-17}\) rounds to exactly 1 and log(1) returns 0: 100% error. log1p(1e-17) returns 1e-17. (d) \(1 - (1 - \mu + \mu^2/2) = \mu - \mu^2/2 = 10^{-9} - 5\times10^{-19} = 9.999999995\times10^{-10}\). This is what -expm1(-mu) returns on the slide; the naive 1 - exp(-mu) gives \(9.99999971718069\times10^{-10}\), wrong from the 8th digit.
B8. (a) Not as an R integer: \(3.1\times10^9 &gt; 2^{31} - 1 \approx 2.147\times10^9\). As a double, yes: every whole number up to \(9\times10^{15}\) is exact. (b) \(20\,000 \times 100\,000 = 2\times10^9 &lt; 2.147\times10^9\): it fits, barely. \(30\,000 \times 100\,000 = 3\times10^9\) does not. This is why large sparse-matrix libraries switched to 64-bit indices. (c) No: the gap at \(2^{53}\) is 2, so \(2^{53} + 1\) is a tie and rounds to \(2^{53}\) (round half to even). 2^53 + 1 - 2^53 returns 0.
