Down to \(h = 10^{-6}\) the error is \(h/2\), exactly as B5 predicts (truncation, order 1). Below \(h \approx 10^{-8}\) the error grows again: \(e^h\) and 1 agree in almost all their 16 digits, and the subtraction leaves only rounding noise, which is then divided by a tiny \(h\). The best possible accuracy is about 8 digits. Convergence and cost explains why the optimum is near \(\sqrt{10^{-16}} = 10^{-8}\).









