In R, lm and qr.solve use Householder QR. In Python, np.linalg.qr is Householder QR and np.linalg.lstsq uses the SVD. In Rust, nalgebra’s QR is Householder QR: q_tr_mul applies \(Q^T\) to a vector without forming \(Q\), and solve_upper_triangular does the back substitution. nalgebra’s QR::solve only accepts square matrices, so the least-squares solve is written out in two lines.

Solutions






Solution





B1. (a) \(A = \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 1\\ 1 & 2\\ 1 & 3\\ 1 & 4\end{pmatrix}\), \(\mathbf b = (34.6, 31.2, 28.0, 24.6)\). (b) \(A^TA = \begin{pmatrix} 4 & 10\\ 10 & 30\end{pmatrix}\); \(A^T\mathbf b = (\sum b_i, \sum t_ib_i) = (118.4,\; 34.6 + 62.4 + 84.0 + 98.4) = (118.4, 279.4)\). (c) \(D = 4\cdot 30 - 10^2 = 20\). \(x_1 = (30\cdot118.4 - 10\cdot279.4)/20 = (3552 - 2794)/20 = 37.9\). \(x_2 = (4\cdot279.4 - 10\cdot118.4)/20 = (1117.6 - 1184)/20 = -3.32\). (d) Fitted values \(37.9 - 3.32\,t\): \(34.58, 31.26, 27.94, 24.62\). Residuals \(\mathbf r = (0.02, -0.06, 0.06, -0.02)\), \(\sum r_i^2 = 0.0004 + 0.0036 + 0.0036 + 0.0004 = 0.008\). Check: \(\sum r_i = 0\) and \(\sum t_ir_i = 0.02 - 0.12 + 0.18 - 0.08 = 0\). (e) \(E = 10^{1/3.32} - 1 = 10^{0.3012} - 1 = 2.001 - 1 \approx 100\,\%\).
B2. (a) \(\|A\mathbf v\|^2 = v_1^2 + \delta^2v_2^2\), largest 1 (at \(\mathbf v = \mathbf e_1\)), smallest \(\delta\) (at \(\mathbf e_2\)). \(\kappa(A) = 1/\delta = 10^3\). \(A^TA = \begin{pmatrix}1 & 0\\ 0 & \delta^2\end{pmatrix}\), \(\kappa(A^TA) = 1/\delta^2 = 10^6\): the square. (b) \(A^TA = \begin{pmatrix} 1 + \delta^2 & 1\\ 1 & 1 + \delta^2\end{pmatrix}\). (c) \(\delta^2 = 10^{-16} &lt; \varepsilon/2\), so \(1 + \delta^2\) is stored as exactly \(1\). The computed \(A^TA = \begin{pmatrix}1 & 1\\1 & 1\end{pmatrix}\) is singular. But \(A\) has rank 2: rows 2 and 3, \((\delta, 0)\) and \((0, \delta)\), are independent. All the information that separates the two predictors was in \(\delta^2\), and it was rounded away when \(A^TA\) was formed. (d) A good method loses about \(\log_{10}(1.4\times10^8) \approx 8\) digits, so about 8 of 16 remain. The normal equations would lose about 16, i.e. all of them; here they do not even get an answer.
B3. (a) \(r_{11} = \sqrt3\), \(\mathbf q_1 = (1,1,1)/\sqrt3\). \(r_{12} = \mathbf q_1^T\mathbf a_2 = 9/\sqrt3 = 3\sqrt3 \approx 5.196\). \(\mathbf v = \mathbf a_2 - 3\sqrt3\,\mathbf q_1 = (1,3,5) - (3,3,3) = (-2, 0, 2)\). \(r_{22} = \sqrt8 = 2\sqrt2 \approx 2.828\), \(\mathbf q_2 = (-1, 0, 1)/\sqrt2\). \[R = \begin{pmatrix}\sqrt3 & 3\sqrt3\\ 0 & 2\sqrt2\end{pmatrix} \approx \begin{pmatrix} 1.732 & 5.196\\ 0 & 2.828\end{pmatrix}\] (b) \(\mathbf q_1^T\mathbf q_2 = (-1 + 0 + 1)/\sqrt6 = 0\). \(3\sqrt3\,\mathbf q_1 + 2\sqrt2\,\mathbf q_2 = (3,3,3) + (-2, 0, 2) = (1, 3, 5)\). (c) \(Q^T\mathbf b = \big((35 + 28.4 + 21.6)/\sqrt3,\; (21.6 - 35)/\sqrt2\big) = (85/\sqrt3,\; -13.4/\sqrt2)\). Back substitution: \(x_2 = (-13.4/\sqrt2)/(2\sqrt2) = -13.4/4 = -3.35\). \(x_1 = (85/\sqrt3 - 3\sqrt3\cdot(-3.35))/\sqrt3 = 85/3 + 3\cdot3.35 = 28.333 + 10.05 = 38.383\). (d) \(\mathbf v = \mathbf a_2 - \bar a_2\): the predictor centred at its mean (3). The slope is \(x_2 = \mathbf q_2^T\mathbf b / r_{22} = \dfrac{\sum_i (t_i - \bar t)\,b_i}{\sum_i (t_i - \bar t)^2}\), the familiar regression formula.
B4. (a) \(\|\mathbf a\| = \sqrt{4 + 1 + 4} = 3\). \(\mathbf v = (2 + 3, 1, 2) = (5, 1, 2)\), \(\mathbf v^T\mathbf v = 30\), \(\mathbf v^T\mathbf a = 10 + 1 + 4 = 15\). \(H\mathbf a = \mathbf a - 2\cdot\tfrac{15}{30}\mathbf v = \mathbf a - \mathbf v = (-3, 0, 0)\). (b) An orthogonal \(H\) keeps lengths, so the result has length 3 and all of it is in the first component: two zeros were created without changing the size of anything. A non-orthogonal transformation (like an elimination step) can change lengths, and with them the size of the rounding errors already present. (c) \(\|\mathbf a\| = \sqrt{1 + 10^{-10}} = 1.00000000005\). With the sign in fact 6, \(a_1 + \|\mathbf a\| = 2.00000000005\): no cancellation. With the other sign, \(a_1 - \|\mathbf a\| = -5\times10^{-11}\): the difference of two numbers that agree in 10 digits. The stored \(\|\mathbf a\|\) has an absolute error up to about \(10^{-16}\), which relative to \(5\times10^{-11}\) is about \(2\times10^{-6}\): some 10 of the 16 digits of \(v_1\) are lost (catastrophic cancellation, as in Error, conditioning and stability).
B5. (a) µg/mL: \(A = \begin{pmatrix}1 & 0 & 0\\ 1 & 1000 & 10^6\\ 1 & 2000 & 4\times10^6\end{pmatrix}\). mg/mL: \(A = \begin{pmatrix}1 & 0 & 0\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\ 1 & 2 & 4\end{pmatrix}\). (b) Column lengths in µg/mL: \(\sqrt3 = 1.73\), \(\sqrt{5\times10^6} = 2236\), \(\sqrt{17\times10^{12}} = 4.12\times10^6\). So \(\kappa(A) \ge 4.12\times10^6/1.73 = 2.4\times10^6\), and \(\kappa(A^TA) = \kappa(A)^2 \ge 5.7\times10^{12}\): the normal equations lose at least 13 of 16 digits. (c) mg/mL: \(1.73\), \(2.24\), \(4.12\), bound \(\kappa \ge 2.4\). True but useless: the columns have similar length, so the bound says nothing; the actual \(\kappa\) here comes from the columns pointing in similar directions. (d) \(c_0 + c_1 s + c_2 s^2\) with \(s = x/1000\) equals \(c_0 + (c_1/1000)\,x + (c_2/10^6)\,x^2\). The curves are identical; only the numbers that describe them change.
B6. (a) \(\mathbf q_1 = (1,1,1)/\sqrt3\). Column 2: \(r_{12} = 3000/\sqrt3\), \(\mathbf v = (0, 1000, 2000) - (1000, 1000, 1000) = (-1000, 0, 1000)\), \(r_{22} = 1000\sqrt2\), \(\mathbf q_2 = (-1, 0, 1)/\sqrt2\). Column 3: \(r_{13} = 3/\sqrt3 = \sqrt3\), after removing it \(\mathbf v = (-1, 0, 1)\); \(r_{23} = \mathbf q_2^T\mathbf a_3 = 2/\sqrt2 = \sqrt2\), \(\mathbf v = (-1, 0, 1) - \sqrt2\cdot(-1,0,1)/\sqrt2 = \mathbf 0\). So \(r_{33} = 0\), and \(\mathbf q_3 = \mathbf v / r_{33}\) is \(0/0\): the algorithm breaks. Back substitution would also divide by \(r_{33} = 0\). (b) Rounding leaves a tiny nonzero \(\mathbf v\), so \(r_{33} \approx 10^{-16}\) (relative to \(r_{11}\)) instead of 0. Dividing by it gives garbage of size \(10^{16}\), not an error. Software therefore compares each \(|r_{jj}|\) with a tolerance relative to the size of the column and declares the column dependent when it falls below. lm (tolerance \(10^{-7}\)) drops the column and reports NA for its coefficient (C7).
C1.

