Background
A root of \(f\) is a number \(x^*\) with \(f(x^*) = 0\). A fixed point of \(g\) is a number \(x^*\) with \(g(x^*) = x^*\); it is a root of \(f(x) = g(x) - x\).
Facts used below:

Bracket. If \(f\) is continuous on \([a, b]\) and \(f(a)\), \(f(b)\) have opposite signs, \([a, b]\) contains a root. No sign change does not mean no root (two roots, a double root); a sign change across a pole is not a root.
Bisection. Evaluate \(f\) at the midpoint \(m = (a+b)/2\) and keep the half with the sign change. After \(k\) steps the bracket has width \((b - a)/2^k\), so reaching width tol takes \(\lceil \log_2((b-a)/\text{tol}) \rceil\) steps. Linear convergence with rate \(\tfrac12\).
Stopping. Stop when the bracket (or the step) is below \(\text{tol}\cdot\max(1, |x|)\), and always after a maximum number of iterations. Near \(x\), neighbouring doubles are about \(\varepsilon|x|\) apart (\(\varepsilon = 2.2\times10^{-16}\)), so no bracket gets narrower than that.
Fixed-point iteration. With \(e_k = x_k - x^*\), Taylor gives \(e_{k+1} \approx g'(x^*)\,e_k\). If \(|g'(x^*)| &lt; 1\) the iteration converges linearly with rate \(|g'(x^*)|\) (attracting, or stable, fixed point); if \(|g'(x^*)| &gt; 1\) it moves away (repelling, unstable). \(g' &gt; 0\): monotone; \(g' &lt; 0\): alternating.
Checking an iteration. With steps \(d_k = x_{k+1} - x_k\), the ratio \(d_{k+1}/d_k \to g'(x^*) = L\), and the remaining error is about \(|d_k|\,L/(1 - L)\).
Population models. Beverton–Holt \(N_{t+1} = \dfrac{R N_t}{1 + (R-1)N_t/K}\) has \(g'(K) = 1/R\). Ricker \(N_{t+1} = N_t\,e^{r(1 - N_t/K)}\) has \(g'(K) = 1 - r\): stable for \(0 &lt; r &lt; 2\), cycles and chaos beyond.
The harvested stock of the lecture: \(g(N) = N e^{r(1-N/K)} - H\) with \(r = 0.5\), \(K = 100\), \(H = 10\) (thousands of fish). Its steady states are \(20.482\) (unstable, \(g' = 1.336\)) and \(74.952\) (stable, \(g' = 0.709\)).

