B2. (a) Sign changes between 20 and 30 (\(-0.16\) to \(2.57\)) and between 70 and 80 (\(1.33\) to \(-1.59\)): brackets \([20, 30]\) and \([70, 80]\). Between the roots \(f &gt; 0\): the stock grows. Below 20.5 and above 75 it shrinks. (b) \(\lceil \log_2(10/10^{-6}) \rceil = \lceil 23.25 \rceil = 24\) steps for each. (c) No. The table only shows values every 10 units; a pair of roots closer together than that, or a double root where \(f\) only touches 0, would be missed. Plot \(f\) on a fine grid, or find the maximum of \(f\): here the maximum of \(f\) for \(H = 15\) is about \(-0.74\) (at \(N \approx 47\)), so there is no root. For the fish: no steady state at all, the stock shrinks every year and collapses whatever its size. The largest sustainable catch is the maximum of the surplus \(N(e^{r(1-N/K)} - 1)\), about 14.26. (d) \(f(0) = f(100) = -10\): same sign, so bisection cannot start. An even number of roots inside gives no sign change.
B3. (a) Near \(3\times10^8\) the gap between doubles is \(2^{28-52} = 2^{-24} \approx 6\times10^{-8}\). The bracket can never be narrower than one gap, so \(b - a &lt; 10^{-8}\) is never true: without a maximum number of iterations the loop runs forever. With the relative rule, the target width is \(10^{-10}\times3\times10^8 = 0.03\) cells, and \(\lceil\log_2(10^9/0.03)\rceil = 35\) steps. (b) The rule is effectively absolute (\(\max(1, |m|) = 1\)): \(\lceil\log_2(10^{-6}/10^{-8})\rceil = 7\) steps, leaving a bracket of width \(7.8\times10^{-9}\) M, about four times the root itself. No digit is correct. Fixes: use a purely relative tolerance, \(b - a &lt; \text{tol}\cdot|m|\); or work in units where the root is of order 1 (nM instead of M). The \(\max(1, |x|)\) rule assumes the problem is scaled so that \(x\) is not tiny.
B4. (a) \(N_1 = 150/(1 + 1) = 75\); \(N_2 = 225/(1 + 1.5) = 90\); \(N_3 = 270/2.8 = 96.43\); \(N_4 = 289.29/2.9286 = 98.78\). (b) Errors \(50, 25, 10, 3.57, 1.22\); ratios \(0.5, 0.4, 0.357, 0.341\), approaching \(\tfrac13\). (c) With \(c = (R-1)/K\): \(g' = \dfrac{R(1 + cN) - RN\,c}{(1 + cN)^2} = \dfrac{R}{(1 + cN)^2}\). At \(N = K\), \(1 + cK = R\), so \(g'(K) = R/R^2 = 1/R = \tfrac13\): the limit of the ratios. (d) \(u_{t+1} = \dfrac{1 + cN_t}{R N_t} = \dfrac{1}{R}\dfrac1{N_t} + \dfrac{c}{R} = \dfrac{u_t}{R} + \dfrac{R-1}{RK}\). The fixed point is \(u^* = 1/K\), and \(u_{t+1} - u^* = (u_t - u^*)/R\) exactly. Check: \(1/50 - 1/100 = 0.01\), \(1/75 - 1/100 = 0.00333\), \(1/90 - 1/100 = 0.00111\): divided by 3 each year. (In the variable \(u = 1/N\) the map is a straight line; that is why the Beverton–Holt model has an exact solution.)
