For \(r = 1.5\) the two formulas stay within one or two rounding gaps of each other forever: the attracting steady state pulls both back to \(K\). For \(r = 3\) a first difference of \(6\times10^{-14}\) (rounding) grows by a factor of about 1000 every 20 years, and after about 80 years the two trajectories are unrelated. The same happens between languages, whose exp functions can differ in the last bit: that is why the \(r = 3\) values in C5 differ between R and Python. A long chaotic simulation is not reproducible digit by digit; its statistics (range, mean, how often the stock falls below a level) are. The steady state itself is still computed accurately by a root finder.
