Background
Spectral unmixing. A cell carries unknown amounts \(\mathbf x\) of several dyes. Detector \(i\) records \(b_i = \sum_j A_{ij} x_j\), where \(A_{ij}\) is the signal of one unit of dye \(j\) in detector \(i\) (measured from single-stained controls). Unmixing one cell means solving \(A\mathbf x = \mathbf b\). Our running example is two dyes with detectors at their two peaks and overlap \(c\):
\[A = \begin{pmatrix}1 & c\\ c & 1\end{pmatrix}, \qquad c = 0.95 \text{ (peaks about 10 nm apart)}.\]
Facts used below:

Elimination (from Gaussian elimination as LU) subtracts \(m = a_{ik}/a_{kk}\) times row \(k\) from row \(i\). The pivot \(a_{kk}\) is divided by twice: in the multiplier and in back substitution. A tiny pivot gives huge multipliers, and the rows below are swamped by rounding.
Partial pivoting: before eliminating column \(k\), swap rows so that the entry of largest absolute value in column \(k\) (on or below the diagonal) is the pivot. Then \(|m| \le 1\). The swaps are collected in a permutation matrix \(P\): \(PA = LU\).
Vector norms: \(\|\mathbf v\|_1 = \sum_i |v_i|\), \(\|\mathbf v\|_2 = \sqrt{\sum_i v_i^2}\), \(\|\mathbf v\|_\infty = \max_i |v_i|\). Relative error of a vector: \(\|\hat{\mathbf x} - \mathbf x\|/\|\mathbf x\|\).
Matrix norms: \(\|A\| = \max_{\mathbf v\ne 0}\|A\mathbf v\|/\|\mathbf v\|\), the largest stretch. \(\|A\|_1\) = largest absolute column sum, \(\|A\|_\infty\) = largest absolute row sum, \(\|A\|_2 = \sigma_{\max}\), the largest singular value (for a symmetric matrix: the largest \(|\lambda|\)).
Condition number \(\kappa(A) = \|A\|\,\|A^{-1}\|\); in the 2-norm \(\kappa = \sigma_{\max}/\sigma_{\min}\). If \(A\mathbf x = \mathbf b\) and \(A(\mathbf x + \Delta\mathbf x) = \mathbf b + \Delta\mathbf b\), then \[\frac{\|\Delta\mathbf x\|}{\|\mathbf x\|} \le \kappa(A)\,\frac{\|\Delta\mathbf b\|}{\|\mathbf b\|}, \qquad \text{digits lost} \approx \log_{10}\kappa.\]
Residual vs error. The residual \(\mathbf r = \mathbf b - A\hat{\mathbf x}\) can be computed; the error \(\hat{\mathbf x} - \mathbf x\) cannot. \(\|\hat{\mathbf x} - \mathbf x\|/\|\mathbf x\| \le \kappa(A)\,\|\mathbf r\|/\|\mathbf b\|\). A backward-stable solver (LU with partial pivoting) always returns a residual near rounding level, whatever \(\kappa\) is.
Inverse of a \(2\times2\): \(\begin{pmatrix}a & b\\ c & d\end{pmatrix}^{-1} = \dfrac{1}{ad - bc}\begin{pmatrix}d & -b\\ -c & a\end{pmatrix}\).

