B1. (a) \(m = 1/0.0001 = 10^4\). Row 2: \(1 - 10^4\cdot 1 = -9999 \to -1.00\times10^4\); right side \(2 - 10^4\cdot 1 = -9998 \to -1.00\times10^4\). So \(x_2 = 1.00\), and \(x_1 = (1 - 1.00)/0.0001 = 0\). (b) Swap: \(\begin{pmatrix}1 & 1 & | & 2\\ 0.0001 & 1 & | & 1\end{pmatrix}\), \(m = 0.0001\). Row 2: \(1 - 0.0001 = 0.9999 \to 1.00\); right side \(1 - 0.0002 = 0.9998 \to 1.00\). So \(x_2 = 1.00\), \(x_1 = 2 - 1.00 = 1.00\). (c) (b) is right to 3 digits; (a) has \(x_1 = 0\), 100 % wrong. The information was lost when \(1 - 10^4\) and \(2 - 10^4\) were rounded: the 1 and the 2 of the second equation fell below the third digit of \(10^4\). Then \(x_1 = (1 - x_2)/0.0001\) is a cancellation divided by a tiny pivot. Doubles behave the same way with \(\delta\) below about \(10^{-16}\) (C1).
B2. (a) Largest \(|a_{i1}|\) is 8 (row 3): swap rows 1 and 3. Multipliers \(4/8 = 0.5\) (row with 4) and \(2/8 = 0.25\) (row with 2): \[\begin{pmatrix}8 & 7 & 9\\ 4 & 3 & 3\\ 2 & 1 & 1\end{pmatrix} \to \begin{pmatrix}8 & 7 & 9\\ 0 & -0.5 & -1.5\\ 0 & -0.75 & -1.25\end{pmatrix}.\] (b) In column 2, \(|-0.75| &gt; |-0.5|\): swap rows 2 and 3 (with their multipliers). Multiplier \(-0.5/-0.75 = 2/3\); new \((3,3)\) entry \(-1.5 - \tfrac23(-1.25) = -\tfrac23\). (c) Pivot order: original rows 3, 1, 2. \[P = \begin{pmatrix}0 & 0 & 1\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\ 0 & 1 & 0\end{pmatrix},\quad L = \begin{pmatrix}1 & 0 & 0\\ 0.25 & 1 & 0\\ 0.5 & \tfrac23 & 1\end{pmatrix},\quad U = \begin{pmatrix}8 & 7 & 9\\ 0 & -0.75 & -1.25\\ 0 & 0 & -\tfrac23\end{pmatrix}.\] Check row 3 of \(LU\): \(0.5\cdot(8, 7, 9) + \tfrac23(0, -0.75, -1.25) + (0, 0, -\tfrac23) = (4, 3.5 - 0.5, 4.5 - 0.8333 - 0.6667) = (4, 3, 3)\), which is row 3 of \(PA\) (original row 2). All multipliers have \(|m| \le 1\). (d) \(P\mathbf b = (24, 4, 10)\). Forward: \(y_1 = 24\), \(y_2 = 4 - 0.25\cdot 24 = -2\), \(y_3 = 10 - 0.5\cdot 24 - \tfrac23(-2) = -\tfrac23\). Back: \(x_3 = (-\tfrac23)/(-\tfrac23) = 1\), \(x_2 = (-2 + 1.25\cdot 1)/(-0.75) = 1\), \(x_1 = (24 - 7 - 9)/8 = 1\). So \(\mathbf x = (1, 1, 1)\).
B3. (a) \(\mathbf v\): \(19\), \(13\), \(12\). \(\mathbf w\): \(4\), \(2\), \(1\). (For \(\mathbf w\) the three norms differ by the largest possible factors, \(n = 4\) and \(\sqrt n = 2\).) (b) \(\hat{\mathbf x} - \mathbf x = (0.2, -0.2)\). 1-norm: \(0.4/2 = 0.2\). 2-norm: \(0.2\sqrt2/\sqrt2 = 0.2\). \(\infty\)-norm: \(0.2/1 = 0.2\). A 20 % error in every norm (because all components have the same size). (c) Diamond (1-norm), circle (2-norm), square (\(\infty\)-norm). \(\|\mathbf v\|_1 = \|\mathbf v\|_\infty\) exactly when at most one component is nonzero: the corners of the diamond, where it touches the square.
B4. (a) Column sums and row sums are all \(1.95\): \(\|A\|_1 = \|A\|_\infty = 1.95\). (b) \(\det A = 1 - 0.9025 = 0.0975\). \[A^{-1} = \frac{1}{0.0975}\begin{pmatrix}1 & -0.95\\ -0.95 & 1\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}10.256 & -9.744\\ -9.744 & 10.256\end{pmatrix}.\] \(\|A^{-1}\|_\infty = 1.95/0.0975 = 20\), so \(\kappa_\infty = 1.95 \times 20 = 39\). (c) \(\sigma_{\max} = 1.95\), \(\sigma_{\min} = 0.05\), \(\kappa_2 = 39\). (Here all three norms give the same \(\kappa\); in general they differ by a modest factor.) (d) Eigenvalues \(1 \pm c\), both positive for \(0 \le c &lt; 1\), so \(\kappa_2 = (1 + c)/(1 - c)\): \(c = 0.5\): 3; \(c = 0.9\): 19; \(c = 0.99\): 199. (e) Digits left \(\approx 3 - \log_{10}\kappa\): \(c = 0.5\): \(3 - 0.5 = 2.5\); \(c = 0.95\): \(3 - 1.6 = 1.4\); \(c = 0.99\): \(3 - 2.3 = 0.7\), less than one digit. Rounding errors of the computer (\(16 - 2.3\) digits left) are irrelevant; the 3-digit data decide.
B5. (a) \(\Delta\mathbf x = A^{-1}(0.01, -0.01) = \frac{0.01}{0.0975}(1 + 0.95, -0.95 - 1) = (0.2, -0.2)\). Relative changes: \(\|\Delta\mathbf x\|/\|\mathbf x\| = 0.2\) and \(\|\Delta\mathbf b\|/\|\mathbf b\| = 0.01/1.95 = 0.005128\). Amplification \(0.2/0.005128 = 39 = \kappa\). (b) \(\Delta\mathbf x = \frac{0.01}{0.0975}(1 - 0.95, -0.95 + 1) = (0.005128, 0.005128)\). Relative changes \(0.005128\) and \(0.005128\): amplification 1. (c) \(\mathbf b = (1.95, 1.95)\) points along the eigenvector \((1, 1)\) with eigenvalue \(1.95\) (stretched): \(\|\mathbf x\| = \|\mathbf b\|/1.95\) is as small as it can be relative to \(\|\mathbf b\|\). A \(\Delta\mathbf b\) along \((1, -1)\) is divided by the eigenvalue \(0.05\): \(\|\Delta\mathbf x\| = \|\Delta\mathbf b\|/0.05\). Together \(1.95/0.05 = 39\), the worst case. A \(\Delta\mathbf b\) along \((1, 1)\) is divided by \(1.95\), like \(\mathbf b\) itself: no amplification. Anti-correlated detector errors (one detector reads high, the other low) are dangerous; a common gain error in both detectors is harmless.
B6. (a) \(A\hat{\mathbf x}_a = (1.2 + 0.76, 1.14 + 0.8) = (1.96, 1.94)\), \(\mathbf r_a = (-0.01, 0.01)\), relative residual \(0.01/1.95 = 0.0051\). \(A\hat{\mathbf x}_b = (1.9695, 1.9695)\), \(\mathbf r_b = (-0.0195, -0.0195)\), relative residual \(0.0195/1.95 = 0.01\). (b) Errors: \(\hat{\mathbf x}_a\): \(0.2\) (20 %); \(\hat{\mathbf x}_b\): \(0.01\) (1 %). (c) By residual you would pick \(\hat{\mathbf x}_a\), which is 20 times worse. Bound: \(\kappa_\infty \times 0.005128 = 39 \times 0.005128 = 0.2\), exactly the error of \(\hat{\mathbf x}_a\): it is the worst case. A small residual means a small error only when \(\kappa\) is small.
B7. (a) The multiplier is \(1/0\): division by zero, and the method stops (or produces Inf/NaN). (b) Swap: \(\begin{pmatrix}1 & 0.3 & | & 1.3\\ 0 & 1 & | & 1\end{pmatrix}\), already triangular. \(x_2 = 1\), \(x_1 = 1.3 - 0.3 = 1\). (c) \(\det A = -1\), \(A^{-1} = \begin{pmatrix}-0.3 & 1\\ 1 & 0\end{pmatrix}\). \(\|A\|_\infty = 1.3\), \(\|A^{-1}\|_\infty = 1.3\), \(\kappa_\infty = 1.69\). A very easy problem: the failure in (a) was entirely the algorithm’s fault (the order of the equations). Pivoting removes it.
