The random numbers differ between languages, so the measured standard deviations differ by a few per cent; the predictions are the same. With overlap 0.5 the noise in the unmixed dye is \(1.5\times\) the detector noise; with overlap 0.95 it is \(14\times\): a 1 % detector noise becomes a 14 % spread in the unmixed abundance. The factor \(\|\text{row 1 of } A^{-1}\|_2\) (\(1.49\) and \(14.1\)) is below \(\kappa\) (\(3\) and \(39\)): \(\kappa\) is the worst case over all directions of \(\mathbf b\) and \(\Delta\mathbf b\), while independent noise in both detectors falls only partly in the worst direction. The spread is along \((1, -1)\), dye 1 against dye 2 (lecture figure): this is what cytometrists call spillover spreading. No solver can remove it; only better-separated dyes (smaller \(c\)) or more detectors (Part 4) can.

Background
The toggle switch. Two genes repress each other. With concentrations \(u\), \(v\) (in units of the repression threshold) and time in units of the protein lifetime,
\[\frac{du}{dt} = \frac{\alpha}{1 + v^{n}} - u, \qquad \frac{dv}{dt} = \frac{\alpha}{1 + u^{n}} - v,\]
with promoter strength \(\alpha = 3\) and Hill coefficient \(n = 2\) unless stated otherwise. A steady state is a root of \(\mathbf F(\mathbf x) = \mathbf 0\), with \(\mathbf x = (u, v)\) and
\[F_1(u, v) = \frac{\alpha}{1 + v^{n}} - u, \qquad F_2(u, v) = \frac{\alpha}{1 + u^{n}} - v.\]
Facts used below:

Partial derivative. \(\partial F_i / \partial x_j\) is the ordinary derivative of \(F_i\) with respect to \(x_j\), with every other variable held fixed at its value. Check: \(\big(F_i(\mathbf x + h\,\mathbf e_j) - F_i(\mathbf x)\big)/h\) for small \(h\), where \(\mathbf e_j\) moves only \(x_j\).
Jacobian. \(J_{ij} = \partial F_i / \partial x_j\): row = equation, column = variable. For the toggle switch \[J(u, v) = \begin{pmatrix} -1 & -\dfrac{\alpha n v^{n-1}}{(1+v^n)^2} \\[6pt] -\dfrac{\alpha n u^{n-1}}{(1+u^n)^2} & -1 \end{pmatrix}.\]
Linear approximation. \(\mathbf F(\mathbf x + \boldsymbol\delta) \approx \mathbf F(\mathbf x) + J(\mathbf x)\,\boldsymbol\delta\), with an error of order \(\|\boldsymbol\delta\|^2\).
Newton step. Solve \(J(\mathbf x_k)\,\boldsymbol\delta = -\mathbf F(\mathbf x_k)\), then \(\mathbf x_{k+1} = \mathbf x_k + \boldsymbol\delta\). Near a root with a non-singular Jacobian, convergence is quadratic: \(e_{k+1} \approx C e_k^2\).
Length of a vector (Euclidean norm): \(\|\mathbf a\| = \sqrt{a_1^2 + a_2^2}\). Used for the residual \(\|\mathbf F\|\), the step \(\|\boldsymbol\delta\|\) and the error \(\|\mathbf x_k - \mathbf x^*\|\).
Finite-difference Jacobian. Column \(j\) is \(\big(\mathbf F(\mathbf x + h_j \mathbf e_j) - \mathbf F(\mathbf x)\big)/h_j\) with \(h_j = \sqrt{\varepsilon}\,\max(|x_j|, 1)\), \(\sqrt\varepsilon \approx 1.5\times10^{-8}\). About 8 correct digits; \(n + 1\) evaluations of \(\mathbf F\).
Library routines. R: rootSolve::multiroot (Newton with a finite-difference Jacobian unless you pass jacfunc). Python: scipy.optimize.root (default method "hybr", Powell’s hybrid method from MINPACK) or the older scipy.optimize.fsolve. Rust: none in our crate list, so we hand-write Newton with nalgebra’s LU solve.

The model, its Jacobian and one Newton step in each language (R: solve; Python: np.linalg.solve):

