Solutions






Solution — by hand





B1. (a) \(\partial g/\partial u = 2uv\) (treat \(v\) as a constant), \(\partial g/\partial v = u^2 + 3\) (treat \(u\) as a constant). At \((1, 2)\): \(\partial g/\partial u = 4\), \(\partial g/\partial v = 4\). (b) \(\partial r/\partial E = \dfrac{kS}{K + S} = \dfrac{2\cdot1}{2} = 1\): one more unit of enzyme raises the rate by 1 unit (the rate is proportional to \(E\)). \(\partial r/\partial S = kE\,\dfrac{K}{(K+S)^2} = 2\cdot0.5\cdot\dfrac{1}{4} = 0.25\): at \(S = K\) the enzyme is half saturated, and one more unit of substrate raises the rate by only 0.25. (c) \(r(1.01, 0.5) = 2\cdot0.5\cdot1.01/2.01 = 0.502488\), \(r(1, 0.5) = 0.5\), so the quotient is \(0.002488/0.01 = 0.2488\), close to \(0.25\) (the forward difference has an error of order \(h\)).
B2. (a) \(\partial F_1/\partial u = -1\), \(\partial F_1/\partial v = \alpha\cdot\big(-(1+v^n)^{-2}\big)\cdot n v^{n-1} = -\dfrac{\alpha n v^{n-1}}{(1+v^n)^2}\); by symmetry \(\partial F_2/\partial u = -\dfrac{\alpha n u^{n-1}}{(1+u^n)^2}\) and \(\partial F_2/\partial v = -1\). (b) At \((1, 2)\): \(\partial F_1/\partial v = -\dfrac{6\cdot2}{5^2} = -0.48\), \(\partial F_2/\partial u = -\dfrac{6\cdot1}{2^2} = -1.5\). So \(J = \begin{pmatrix} -1 & -0.48\\ -1.5 & -1\end{pmatrix}\). (c) The diagonal \(-1\) is degradation/dilution: each protein lowers its own rate of change. The off-diagonal entries are repression. At \((1, 2)\), \(u = 1\) sits exactly at the repression threshold, the steepest part of the Hill curve, so a change in U has a strong effect on V’s production (\(-1.5\)). \(v = 2\) is above threshold, where the Hill curve flattens, so V’s effect on U is weaker (\(-0.48\)). (d) At \(u = v = 1.2134\): off-diagonals \(-\dfrac{6\cdot1.2134}{(1 + 1.4724)^2} = -\dfrac{7.2805}{6.1128} = -1.1911\), so \(J = \begin{pmatrix} -1 & -1.1911\\ -1.1911 & -1\end{pmatrix}\) and \(\det J = 1 - 1.1911^2 = -0.4186\). Not singular, so Newton converges quadratically there too.
B3. \(\mathbf F(1, 2) = (3/5 - 1,\; 3/2 - 2) = (-0.4, -0.5)\). With \(\boldsymbol\delta = (0.1, -0.1)\): \(J\boldsymbol\delta = (-0.1 + 0.048,\; -0.15 + 0.1) = (-0.052, -0.05)\), so the prediction is \((-0.452, -0.550)\). True: \(\mathbf F(1.1, 1.9) = (3/4.61 - 1.1,\; 3/2.21 - 1.9) = (-0.4492, -0.5425)\). The prediction is off by about \(0.003\) and \(0.007\), of the order of \(\|\boldsymbol\delta\|^2 = 0.02\), as Fact 3 says.
B4. (a) \(\mathbf F(3, 0) = (3/1 - 3,\; 3/10 - 0) = (0, 0.3)\). \(J(3, 0) = \begin{pmatrix} -1 & 0\\ -0.18 & -1\end{pmatrix}\): at \(v = 0\) the Hill curve is flat (\(\partial F_1/\partial v = 0\)), so \(J\) is triangular. (b) Row 1: \(-\delta_u = 0\), so \(\delta_u = 0\). Row 2: \(-0.18\cdot0 - \delta_v = -0.3\), so \(\delta_v = 0.3\). \(\mathbf x_1 = (3, 0.3)\). (c) \(\mathbf F(3, 0.3) = (3/1.09 - 3,\; 0.3 - 0.3) = (-0.24771, 0)\). \(\partial F_1/\partial v = -6\cdot0.3/1.09^2 = -1.51502\), \(\partial F_2/\partial u = -0.18\). Solve \(\begin{pmatrix} -1 & -1.51502\\ -0.18 & -1\end{pmatrix}\boldsymbol\delta = \begin{pmatrix} 0.24771\\ 0\end{pmatrix}\). Row 2 gives \(\delta_v = -0.18\,\delta_u\); row 1 then gives \((-1 + 0.27270)\,\delta_u = 0.24771\), so \(\delta_u = -0.34058\), \(\delta_v = 0.06131\). \(\mathbf x_2 = (2.6594, 0.3613)\), error about \(0.046\) (C2 confirms these digits).
B5. (a) \(u + uv^2 - v - vu^2 = (u - v) - uv(u - v) = (u - v)(1 - uv) = 0\). (b) \(u = v\) in \(u(1 + u^2) = \alpha\) gives \(u^3 + u - \alpha = 0\). Newton with \(f(u) = u^3 + u - 3\), \(f'(u) = 3u^2 + 1\): \(u_1 = 1 - (-1)/4 = 1.25\); \(u_2 = 1.25 - 0.203125/5.6875 = 1.21429\). (The root is \(1.213412\).) (c) With \(uv = 1\): \(u(1 + v^2) = u + v(uv) = u + v = \alpha\). So \(u, v\) are the roots of \(t^2 - 3t + 1 = 0\): \(t = (3 \pm \sqrt5)/2\), i.e. \((u, v) = (2.618034, 0.381966)\) or \((0.381966, 2.618034)\). (These are \(\varphi^2\) and \(1/\varphi^2\), with \(\varphi\) the golden ratio.) (d) \(t^2 - \alpha t + 1 = 0\) has real roots when \(\alpha^2 - 4 \ge 0\), i.e. \(\alpha \ge 2\) (both positive, since their product is 1 and their sum \(\alpha &gt; 0\)). For \(\alpha &lt; 2\) only the symmetric state exists: the circuit is not a switch. For \(\alpha &gt; 2\) there are three steady states: bistable. At \(\alpha = 2\) all three merge at \(u = v = 1\).
B6. \(J = A\), constant. From any \(\mathbf x_0\): \(A\boldsymbol\delta = -(A\mathbf x_0 - \mathbf b)\), so \(\mathbf x_1 = \mathbf x_0 + \boldsymbol\delta = \mathbf x_0 - \mathbf x_0 + A^{-1}\mathbf b = A^{-1}\mathbf b\), the exact root. The linear approximation is exact for a linear function, so one step suffices. (Newton is “solve the linear problem, repeat”; for a linear problem there is nothing to repeat.)
B7. (a) \(\mathbf F(3, 0.51) = (3/1.2601 - 3,\; 0.3 - 0.51) = (-0.619237, -0.21)\), \(\mathbf F(3, 0.5) = (-0.6, -0.2)\). Column 2 \(= (-0.019237, -0.01)/0.01 = (-1.9237, -1)\). Exact: \((-1.92, -1)\). The error \(0.0037\) is about \(h\,|\partial^2 F_1/\partial v^2|/2\), as for any forward difference. (b) \(\mathbf F(3.01, 0.5) = (2.4 - 3.01,\; 3/10.0601 - 0.5) = (-0.61, -0.201792)\). Column 1 \(= (-0.01, -0.001792)/0.01 = (-1, -0.1792)\). Exact: \((-1, -0.18)\). (c) 3 evaluations: \(\mathbf F(\mathbf x)\) once, plus one per column. For 100 genes: 101 evaluations.
B8. (a) With \(u = v\): \(F_1 = \alpha/(1 + u^n) - u = F_2\). The off-diagonal entries are the same function evaluated at \(v\) and at \(u\), so they are equal (\(= b\), say), and both diagonal entries are \(-1\). The system \(\begin{pmatrix} -1 & b\\ b & -1\end{pmatrix}\boldsymbol\delta = -\begin{pmatrix} F_1\\ F_1\end{pmatrix}\) is unchanged when we swap \(\delta_u\) and \(\delta_v\), so its unique solution has \(\delta_u = \delta_v\). Every iterate stays on the diagonal, so Newton can only find the middle state \((1.2134, 1.2134)\). (b) \(n = 2\): about 5 operations, nanoseconds. \(n = 1000\): \(6.7\times10^8\) operations, 0.67 s. \(n = 20\,000\): \(5.3\times10^{12}\), about 1.5 hours per step. Building the Jacobian costs \((n + 1)\cdot10n \approx 10n^2\) operations: \(10^7\) for \(n = 1000\) and \(4\times10^9\) for \(n = 20\,000\), small compared with the solve. For large dense problems the linear solve dominates; for cheap \(\mathbf F\) and sparse \(J\) the balance changes (Part 3).









