At \((1.9, 0.5)\) the Jacobian is nearly singular (\(\det J = -0.03\)), the first step has length 8 and goes to \(u = -5.1\), and the run ends in the middle state. A start only \(0.1\) away, also with small \(\det J\), ends in “U on” after a detour through \(v = -0.89\). Near the curve \(\det J = 0\) the outcome is unpredictable.
With \(n = 2.5\) the first step gives \(v = -3.25\), and \((-3.25)^{2.5}\) is NaN; every later iterate is NaN. (Python also prints a RuntimeWarning: invalid value encountered in scalar power; R and Rust are silent.)
The damped Newton halves the first step three times (\(t = 0.125\)) to stay non-negative, then takes full steps and converges quadratically to the “U on” state of the \(n = 2.5\) switch, \((2.955096, 0.187363)\). Library routines use similar safeguards: scipy’s hybr uses a trust region, and multiroot(F, c(2, 0.5), positive = TRUE) keeps the iterates non-negative and also finds \((2.955096, 0.187363)\) (without positive = TRUE it warns “steady-state not reached”).

