The SVD matches B1 exactly (up to rounding). For \(A\) the eigenvalue route is harmless, but for \(B\) the true small singular value is \(1.0\times10^{-9}\) (what svd returns), while the square root of the eigenvalue of \(B^TB\) gives \(2.1\times10^{-8}\) in R and Python and \(0\) in Rust: all wrong. The small eigenvalue \(10^{-18}\) of \(B^TB\) is below the rounding error of its entries (about \(3\times2.2\times10^{-16}\)), so it is lost when \(B^TB\) is formed (we take abs because it can even come out negative).
