B6. (a) \(n = 3\): 13 operations to factorise (\(\tfrac23 n^3 = 18\)), 15 to solve (\(2n^2 = 18\)). For \(n = 3\) the lower-order terms still matter; the formulas are for large \(n\). (b) Factorise \(6.7\times10^8\) (about 0.7 s); solve \(2\times10^6\) (about 2 ms). Ratio \(n/3 \approx 333\). (c) Refactorising: \(1000 \times 8.3\times10^{10} = 8.3\times10^{13}\) operations, \(8.3\times10^4\) s \(\approx\) 23 hours. Factor once: \(8.3\times10^{10} + 1000 \times 5\times10^7 = 1.3\times10^{11}\), about 2 minutes. (The real diffusion matrix is banded, which makes both far cheaper: Structure: symmetric, banded, sparse.) (d) No: factorising a 3×3 is 13 operations, solving 15. Even \(10^4\) refactorisations are \(3\times10^5\) operations, a fraction of a millisecond. For small \(n\) the cost that matters in R or Python is the overhead per function call (microseconds each), which is why solve(A, B) with all consumers as columns is still the right call.
B7. (a) (i) \(63/7 = 9.00\), exact. (ii) \(1/7 = 0.142857\ldots\) rounds to \(0.143\); \(0.143 \times 63 = 9.009\) rounds to \(9.01\), wrong in the last digit. The inverse adds a rounding step (storing \(1/7\)), and the product magnifies its error by \(63\). The same happens with matrices, with more steps. (b) Factorise \(\tfrac23 n^3\), plus \(n\) columns at \(2n^2\) each \(= 2n^3\): about \(\tfrac83 n^3\), or 4 times one factorisation. (Using the zeros in \(\mathbf e_j\) brings it down to \(2n^3\), 3 times; LAPACK does this.) (c) Inverse: \(2n^3 + m \cdot 2n^2\). Factor once: \(\tfrac23 n^3 + m \cdot 2n^2\). The per-\(\mathbf b\) costs are equal, so the inverse is always behind by \(\tfrac43 n^3\) and never wins on cost; it also loses on accuracy (C6).
