The residual of the LU solve is at rounding level (\(\le 10^{-15}\)) for every \(d\), in every language. The inverse’s residual is usually orders of magnitude larger, growing as \(d\) shrinks (up to \(10^{-8}\)), though by luck it is sometimes small. The error grows as \(d\) shrinks for both methods: two nearly identical sources make the problem ill-conditioned, and no algorithm fixes that (next unit). The inverse’s error is mostly somewhat larger, up to 10 times. The consistent difference is in the residual: solve returns the exact answer to a system within rounding of yours (it is backward stable, Error, conditioning and stability), the inverse route does not. The individual numbers differ between languages because R, numpy and nalgebra round differently inside their routines.
