Solution





B1. (a) Quotient rule: \(\dfrac{2c(4 + c^2) - 2c^3}{(4 + c^2)^2} = \dfrac{8c}{(4 + c^2)^2}\). (b) \(c_0 = 1\): \(f = \tfrac15 - \tfrac12 = -0.3\), \(f' = 8/25 = 0.32\), \(c_1 = 1 + 0.3/0.32 = 1.9375\). \(c_1 = 1.9375\): \(f = 3.75391/7.75391 - 0.5 = -0.015869\), \(f' = 15.5/60.123 = 0.257805\), \(c_2 = 1.9375 + 0.061554 = 1.999054\). Errors: \(e_0 = 1\), \(e_1 = 0.0625\), \(e_2 = 0.000946\). (c) \(e_1/e_0^2 = 0.0625\), \(e_2/e_1^2 = 0.000946/0.003906 = 0.242\). The next one (in code) is \(2.2\times10^{-7}/(9.46\times10^{-4})^2 = 0.250\). The ratio approaches \(|f''/(2f')| = 0.25\) as the iterates approach the root: quadratic convergence. (d) \(g' = 2c\), so \(c - (c^2 - 4)/(2c) = \tfrac12(c + 4/c)\). From \(c_0 = 1\): \(2.5\), \(2.05\), \(2.000610\). Different \(f\) for the same root give different Newton iterations; both converge quadratically here. (e) \(f(5) = 25/29 - 0.5 = 0.36207\), \(f'(5) = 40/841 = 0.047562\), \(c_1 = 5 - 7.6125 = -2.6125\): a negative dose. The curve is flat at \(c = 5\), so the step is huge. Since \(f(-c) = f(c)\), \(f\) also has a root at \(c = -2\), and Newton converges to it: \(-1.8809\), \(-1.9967\), \(-2.0000\). The answer has the right size and the wrong sign; a range check (\(c &gt; 0\)) catches it.
B2. (a) \(g(1) = -3\), \(g(3) = 5\): \(c_2 = 3 - 5\cdot\dfrac{3 - 1}{5 - (-3)} = 3 - 1.25 = 1.75\). \(g(1.75) = -0.9375\): \(c_3 = 1.75 - (-0.9375)\cdot\dfrac{1.75 - 3}{-0.9375 - 5} = 1.75 + 0.19737 = 1.94737\). (Next: \(2.00356\), \(1.99995\).) (b) Two secant steps: 4 evaluations of \(g\) (two for the starting values, one per step); after that, each step costs one more. Two Newton steps: 4 evaluations as well (\(g\) and \(g'\) at \(c_0\) and \(c_1\)), and each further step costs two.
B3. (a) \(f' = -1/x^2\), so \(x - \dfrac{1/x - a}{-1/x^2} = x + x - a x^2 = x(2 - a x)\). (Early computers divided this way: a division turned into multiplications.) (b) \((1/a + e)(1 - a e) = 1/a - e + e - a e^2 = 1/a - a e^2\). So \(e_{k+1} = -a\,e_k^2\): exactly quadratic, with \(C = a\). (c) \(a = 0.5\), \(x^* = 2\). From 1: \(1\cdot 1.5 = 1.5\), \(1.5\cdot 1.25 = 1.875\), \(1.875\cdot 1.0625 = 1.9921875\). From 5: \(5(2 - 2.5) = -2.5\), \(-2.5(2 + 1.25) = -8.125\), and it runs off to \(-\infty\). (d) If \(x_k \le 0\) or \(x_k \ge 2/a\), then \(x_{k+1} \le 0\), and from there \(x\) only gets more negative. From \(0 &lt; x_0 &lt; 2/a\), \(|a\,e_0| &lt; 1\), and (b) shows the error shrinks. So Newton converges exactly for \(0 &lt; x_0 &lt; 2/a\): twice the root is the limit.
B4. (a) (i) Digits \(1, 1.3, 1.6, 1.9\): \(+0.3\) per step, \(e_{k+1}/e_k = \tfrac12\). Linear, \(p = 1\) (bisection-like). (ii) Digits \(1, 2, 4, 8\): doubling. Quadratic, \(p = 2\) (Newton-like, \(C = 1\)). (iii) Digits \(1, 1.6, 2.6, 4.2, 6.8\): multiplied by about \(1.6\) each step. \(p \approx 1.6\) (secant-like). With the formula: \(\log(0.1)/\log(0.25) = 1.66\), \(\log(0.024)/\log(0.1) = 1.62\), \(\log(0.00267)/\log(0.024) = 1.59\). (b) From 2 to 16 digits. (i) \(14/0.301 = 46.5\), so 47 steps. (ii) \(2 \to 4 \to 8 \to 16\): 3 steps. (iii) \(2 \to 3.2 \to 5.1 \to 8.2 \to 13.1 \to 21\): 5 steps.
B5. (a) \(S(\lambda) = \sum_i x_i/\lambda - n\); \(S = 0\) gives \(\hat\lambda = \bar x\). No root finder needed. (b) \(\ell(\lambda) = \sum_i x_i\log\lambda - n\lambda - n\log(1 - e^{-\lambda}) - \sum_i\log x_i!\). Differentiating: \(S = \dfrac{\sum x_i}{\lambda} - n - \dfrac{n\,e^{-\lambda}}{1 - e^{-\lambda}} = \dfrac{\sum x_i}{\lambda} - \dfrac{n}{1 - e^{-\lambda}}\). (c) \(S(\lambda) \approx \sum x_i/\lambda - n = n\,(1/\lambda - 1/\bar x)\): the reciprocal equation of B3 with \(a = 1/\bar x\) (the factor \(n\) cancels in \(S/S'\)). B3(d) predicts that Newton fails from \(\lambda_0 \ge 2\bar x = 3.46\). The approximation is crude near the root, so the true limit is somewhat different; C2 finds it between 2.7 and 2.8. (d) \(1 - e^{-1.73} = 0.82272\). \(S = 173/1.73 - 100/0.82272 = 100 - 121.549 = -21.549\). \(S' = -173/2.9929 + 100 \cdot 0.17728/0.67687 = -57.803 + 26.192 = -31.611\). \(\lambda_1 = 1.73 - (-21.549)/(-31.611) = 1.73 - 0.6817 = 1.0483\). The step overshoots the MLE (1.218) from above to below, but from there Newton converges (C2).
B6. (a) \(\sigma' = \dfrac{e^{-u}}{(1 + e^{-u})^2} = \dfrac{1}{1 + e^{-u}}\cdot\dfrac{e^{-u}}{1 + e^{-u}} = \sigma(1 - \sigma)\). (b) \(\dfrac{\sigma - 1/2}{\sigma'} = \dfrac{(1 - e^{-u})(1 + e^{-u})}{2e^{-u}} = \dfrac{1 - e^{-2u}}{2e^{-u}} = \dfrac{e^{u} - e^{-u}}{2} = \sinh u\). (c) \(u_1 = 1 - 1.1752 = -0.1752\); \(u_2 = -0.1752 + 0.1761 = 0.0009\). Converging to 0 (\(S = K_m\)). (d) The condition is \(\sinh u^* = 2u^*\). \(\sinh 2.177 = (8.82 - 1/8.82)/2 = (8.82 - 0.113)/2 = 4.353\), and \(2 \cdot 2.177 = 4.354\). The cycle is between \(S = 8.82\,K_m\) and \(S = K_m/8.82 = 0.113\,K_m\).
B7. (a) \(-5.73\) is outside \([3, 10]\), so take the midpoint \(6.5\). \(f(6.5) &gt; 0\), the same sign as \(f(10)\), so the new bracket is \([3, 6.5]\). (b) \(1.887\) is outside \([3, 6.5]\): midpoint \(4.75\). \(f(4.75) &gt; 0\): bracket \([3, 4.75]\). (c) \(4.75 - 0.12588/0.07477 = 4.75 - 1.6836 = 3.066\). Inside \([3, 4.75]\): accepted. From here Newton converges quadratically, and every step stays inside the bracket. (d) \((x - 1)^2 \ge 0\) everywhere: there is no sign change, so there is no bracket to start from. A root where \(f\) touches zero without crossing (a double root) can only be found by methods that do not need a sign change, such as Newton (slowly, see the quiz) or by minimising \(f\).
