One bisection step (the first Newton proposal, \(-5.73\), leaves the bracket), then Newton steps: \(c_{50}\) in 6 iterations instead of a negative dose. (b) From \(u_0 = 2.3\): one bisection, then Newton converges to \(u = 0\) (\(S = K_m\)), with \(-2.8\times10^{-16}\) an error at rounding level. From the cycle start: the first Newton step (to \(-2.177\)) is inside the bracket and accepted, and the bracket shrinks to \([-2.177, 2.177]\). The second proposal would jump back to \(+2.177\), the end of the bracket, with a step no shorter than the last one, so it is replaced by the midpoint, which is (almost) the root \(u = 0\). This is the idea of Brent’s method, with Newton in place of secant and inverse quadratic interpolation (the rtsafe routine of Numerical Recipes is this method).

