\(L - I = \begin{pmatrix} -0.4 & 0.9 \\ 0.8 & -1 \end{pmatrix}\), determinant \(0.4 - 0.72 = -0.32\). \(\mathbf y = \frac{1}{-0.32}\begin{pmatrix} -1 & -0.9 \\ -0.8 & -0.4\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}0\\1\end{pmatrix} = \frac{(-0.9, -0.4)}{-0.32} = (2.8125, 1.25)\). Proportions \((0.6923, 0.3077)\): error \(0.092\), down from \(0.6\) (a factor 0.15, close to the rate 0.125). One step of power iteration gave \((1, 0)\), error \(0.4\).
\(L - 1.2I\) is singular: the system has no unique solution, and in exact arithmetic the method breaks down. In floating point the matrix is only nearly singular (rounding in \(\sigma\) and in the elimination); the solution is huge but points almost exactly along \(\mathbf v_1\), so after normalising it is an excellent eigenvector (lecture slide “Shift exactly at the eigenvalue?”). R’s solve refuses with “system is computationally singular”; a shift of \(1.2 + 10^{-12}\) avoids that.

