All three routes give \(\boldsymbol\pi = (0.20, 0.55, 0.25)\). (The Rust output shows all digits: after 100 days the simulation is still \(5\times10^{-11}\) off, as \(0.8^{100} = 2\times10^{-10}\) predicts; R and numpy print fewer digits.) The linear-system route is exact up to rounding and is the method of choice for a small chain; it needs one equation replaced because \(P^T - I\) is singular. (b) The slow chain stops after 5406 steps with a true error of \(5.0\times10^{-6}\), 500 times the tolerance. (c) The ratio of the last two changes is \(0.998 = \lambda_2\), and \(\text{change}\cdot c/(1-c)\) reproduces the true error almost exactly. This is the check to run whenever an iteration converges slowly.

