Background
We use the course’s logistic OD curve as the truth that the readings are sampled from:
\[N(t) = \frac{K}{1 + (K/N_0 - 1)e^{-rt}}, \qquad K = 1.2,\ N_0 = 0.05,\ r = 0.8 \text{ per hour}.\]
In the lab you only have the readings; here we can measure every error exactly.
Facts used below:

Existence and uniqueness. Through \(n+1\) points with distinct times \(t_0, \dots, t_n\) there is exactly one polynomial \(p\) of degree \(\le n\) with \(p(t_i) = y_i\). In the monomial basis its coefficients solve the Vandermonde system \(V\mathbf c = \mathbf y\), \(V_{ij} = t_i^{\,j}\).
Lagrange form. \(p(t) = \sum_i y_i L_i(t)\) with \(L_i(t) = \prod_{j\ne i} \dfrac{t - t_j}{t_i - t_j}\). \(L_i\) is 1 at \(t_i\) and 0 at the other nodes, and \(\sum_i L_i(t) = 1\).
Newton form. \(p(t) = a_0 + a_1(t - t_0) + a_2(t - t_0)(t - t_1) + \dots\) with \(a_k = f[t_0, \dots, t_k]\), the divided differences: \(f[t_i] = y_i\), \(f[t_i, \dots, t_{i+k}] = \big(f[t_{i+1}, \dots, t_{i+k}] - f[t_i, \dots, t_{i+k-1}]\big)/(t_{i+k} - t_i)\). Adding a node adds one term.
Error formula. \(f(t) - p(t) = \dfrac{f^{(n+1)}(\xi)}{(n+1)!}\, w(t)\), with the node polynomial \(w(t) = \prod_i (t - t_i)\) and some \(\xi\) in the interval spanned by the nodes and \(t\). For linear interpolation between readings \(h\) apart: \(|f - p| \le \tfrac{h^2}{8}\max|f''|\).
Chebyshev nodes. On \([a, b]\): \(t_k = \tfrac{a+b}{2} - \tfrac{b-a}{2}\cos\!\big(\tfrac{(2k+1)\pi}{2n+2}\big)\), \(k = 0, \dots, n\). They make \(\max|w|\) as small as possible, \(2\big(\tfrac{b-a}{4}\big)^{n+1}\).
Noise. If reading \(i\) is off by \(\delta_i\), the interpolant is off by \(\sum_i \delta_i L_i(t)\). The worst case factor \(\max_t \sum_i |L_i(t)|\) is the Lebesgue constant: 89 for 13 equally spaced nodes, 2.6 for 13 Chebyshev nodes.
Barycentric form. \(p(t) = \dfrac{\sum_j w_j y_j/(t - t_j)}{\sum_j w_j/(t - t_j)}\) with \(w_j = 1/\prod_{k\ne j}(t_j - t_k)\). Multiplying all \(w_j\) by the same constant changes nothing. For Chebyshev nodes \(w_j = (-1)^j \sin\big(\tfrac{(2j+1)\pi}{2n+2}\big)\) (up to a constant).

The basic tools in each language. R has no polynomial interpolation function in base; lm with as many poly() coefficients as points interpolates, and approx interpolates piecewise linearly. Python has numpy.polynomial.Polynomial.fit (least squares, which interpolates when deg is the number of points minus one), scipy.interpolate.BarycentricInterpolator and np.interp. In Rust everything is hand-written.

