\(\kappa(V) = 8.4\times10^{20}\), above \(1/\varepsilon \approx 4.5\times10^{15}\), so R’s solve refuses. Forced, the polynomial values still agree with the barycentric interpolant to about \(10^{-9}\): LU gives coefficients that reproduce the readings well (a small residual), even though the coefficients themselves have few correct digits. numpy and nalgebra do not refuse at all; the condition number is the only warning you get.
Up to \(n = 20\) all three ways agree. With times in hours the values become garbage from \(n \approx 30\). Rescaled to \([-1, 1]\) the monomials last until \(\kappa \approx 10^{15}\) (\(n \approx 40\)) and then stall around \(10^{-5}\), while the barycentric formula keeps converging to \(3\times10^{-10}\). The garbage columns differ between R, Python and Rust (different LU and rounding), and beyond \(\kappa \approx 10^{16}\) the computed condition number itself is meaningless: nalgebra’s SVD returns a smallest singular value of exactly 0 and \(\kappa = \infty\). The polynomial is fine; its representation in powers of \(t\) is not.

