Solutions






Solution





B1. (a) \(N(t-h) = N - hN' + \tfrac{h^2}{2}N'' - \dots\), so \(N(t) - N(t-h) = hN' - \tfrac{h^2}{2}N'' + \dots\) and \(\dfrac{N(t) - N(t-h)}{h} = N' - \tfrac{h}{2}N'' + O(h^2)\). (b) \(\tfrac12\Big(\tfrac{N(t+h) - N(t)}{h} + \tfrac{N(t) - N(t-h)}{h}\Big) = \tfrac{N(t+h) - N(t-h)}{2h}\). The errors \(+\tfrac{h}{2}N''\) and \(-\tfrac{h}{2}N''\) cancel; the next terms, \(\pm\tfrac{h^2}{6}N'''\), have the same sign in both and survive: \(\tfrac{h^2}{6}N'''\). (c) Forward: \(\tfrac{0.2}{2}\cdot 0.059212 = 0.0059\) (actual \(0.0056\); the \(h^2\) term \(-0.00028\) is not negligible at \(h = 0.2\)). Central: \(\tfrac{0.04}{6}\cdot(-0.042270) = -2.82\times10^{-4}\) (actual \(-2.82\times10^{-4}\)). Second difference: \(\tfrac{0.04}{12}\cdot(-0.061704) = -2.06\times10^{-4}\).
B2. (a) \(N\) terms: \(w_{-1} + w_0 + w_1 = 0\). \(hN'\) terms: \(-w_{-1} + w_1 = 0\). \(h^2N''\) terms: \(\tfrac12 w_{-1} + \tfrac12 w_1 = 1\). So \(w_{-1} = w_1 = 1\), \(w_0 = -2\): the \((1, -2, 1)\) stencil. (b) \(N'(t) \approx \dfrac{3N(t) - 4N(t-h) + N(t-2h)}{2h}\) (replace \(h\) by \(-h\) in \(\tfrac{-3N(t) + 4N(t+h) - N(t+2h)}{2h}\); the signs flip because the denominator \(2h\) flips). (c) Apply the stencil to a constant function \(N \equiv 1\): every derivative is 0, so \(\sum_j w_j \cdot 1\) must be 0. It is the first of the Taylor equations.
