Forward: \(10^{-7}\) to \(10^{-6}\) relative error (about half the digits). Central: \(10^{-11}\) to \(10^{-7}\). numDeriv::grad: \(10^{-12}\). approx_fprime is a forward difference with \(h = \sqrt\varepsilon\) (not scaled to \(|\theta_j|\)), so it is similar to our forward difference. R, Python and Rust differ in the second digit of the forward errors: np.arange(61) / 6 and seq(0, 10, by = 1/6) give times that differ in the last bit, and the sums are added in a different order. The forward difference sits near the bottom of its V, where such rounding differences are amplified; the central errors agree.
The exact gradient at the maximum is \((-0.004, -0.004, -0.014)\), numerically zero (the gradient at \(\theta\) in (a) was \(10^4\)–\(10^5\)). With an absolute step of \(10^{-3}\) the \(N_0\) component is 290: the step is 2 % of \(N_0 = 0.05\), far into the truncation regime of a strongly curved direction. The \(r\) component (1.2) is also off. An optimiser that sees this gradient stops before the true maximum, as on the lecture slide. A relative step \(10^{-3}|\theta_j|\) gives errors below 1, and \(6\times10^{-6}|\theta_j|\) agrees with the exact gradient to two digits even here, where the gradient is 0 up to rounding.
The \(r\) component differs by 85 %; the others agree to \(10^{-8}\)–\(10^{-11}\). A component with relative difference of order 1 (rather than \(10^{-6}\)) points straight at the wrong partial derivative. This gradient check is what to run before trusting any hand-written (or AD) gradient.

Background
Data. Two running examples. The OD curve from Convergence and cost: a logistic \(N(t) = K / \big(1 + (K/N_0 - 1)e^{-rt}\big)\) with \(K = 1.2\), \(N_0 = 0.05\), \(r = 0.8\) per hour, read by a plate reader every 15 min for 10 h (41 reads) with measurement noise of sd 0.01. Its true growth rate is \(N'(t) = rN(1 - N/K)\), at most \(rK/4 = 0.24\) OD/h at \(t = 3.92\) h. A gene time course: an immediate-early gene measured at 0, 0.5, 1, 2, 4, 8, 12 and 24 h after stimulation (C4), and a gene along pseudotime in 40 cells (C6).
Facts used below:

Piecewise linear. On \([x_i, x_{i+1}]\) with \(h = x_{i+1} - x_i\): \(p(x) = y_i + \frac{y_{i+1} - y_i}{h}(x - x_i)\), error \(|f(x) - p(x)| \le \frac{h^2}{8}\max|f''|\): order 2.
Cubic spline. A cubic on each of the \(n - 1\) intervals with \(S(x_i) = y_i\) and \(S, S', S''\) continuous: \(4n - 4\) unknowns, \(4n - 6\) conditions, so two end conditions are needed.
Second-derivative form. With \(M_i = S''(x_i)\), \(h_i = x_{i+1} - x_i\), \(s_i = (y_{i+1} - y_i)/h_i\), \(A = (x_{i+1} - x)/h_i\), \(B = (x - x_i)/h_i\): \[S(x) = A y_i + B y_{i+1} + \tfrac{h_i^2}{6}\big[(A^3 - A)M_i + (B^3 - B)M_{i+1}\big],\] \[S'(x) = s_i + \tfrac{h_i}{6}\big[-(3A^2 - 1)M_i + (3B^2 - 1)M_{i+1}\big].\]
The system. Continuity of \(S'\) at each interior \(x_i\): \[h_{i-1}M_{i-1} + 2(h_{i-1} + h_i)M_i + h_iM_{i+1} = 6(s_i - s_{i-1}).\] Tridiagonal and strictly diagonally dominant, so the Thomas algorithm (Structure: symmetric, banded, sparse; thomas(a, b, c, d) with sub-, main and super-diagonal and right-hand side, all of length \(n\), a[1] and c[n] unused) solves it in \(O(n)\) without pivoting.
End conditions. Natural: \(M_1 = M_n = 0\). Clamped (slopes \(d_1\), \(d_n\) given): \(2h_1M_1 + h_1M_2 = 6(s_1 - d_1)\) and \(h_{n-1}M_{n-1} + 2h_{n-1}M_n = 6(d_n - s_{n-1})\). Not-a-knot: \(S'''\) continuous at \(x_2\) and \(x_{n-1}\). Defaults: R splinefun/spline use "fmm" (end cubics through 4 points), Python CubicSpline uses not-a-knot. Natural ends are \(O(h^2)\) near the ends; the others \(O(h^4)\); the spline’s slope is \(O(h^3)\).
Noise. A difference quotient of data with independent noise of sd \(\sigma\): forward \((y_{i+1} - y_i)/h\) has noise sd \(\sqrt2\,\sigma/h\); central \((y_{i+1} - y_{i-1})/(2h)\) has \(\sigma/(\sqrt2\,h)\). Smaller \(h\) means more noise.
Smoothing spline. Minimise \(\sum_i (y_i - f(t_i))^2 + \lambda\int f''(t)^2\,dt\). The minimiser is a natural cubic spline with knots at the data; \(\hat{\mathbf y} = A\mathbf y\) with the hat matrix \(A\); df \(= \operatorname{tr}A\) runs from 2 (\(\lambda\to\infty\), straight line) to \(n\) (\(\lambda = 0\), interpolation).
Choosing \(\lambda\). Leave-one-out \(\text{CV}(\lambda) = \frac1n\sum_i\big((y_i - \hat y_i)/(1 - A_{ii})\big)^2\); \(\text{GCV}(\lambda) = n\,\text{RSS}/(n - \text{df})^2\). R’s smooth.spline rescales \(t\) to \([0, 1]\), so Python’s (and our Rust) lam \(=\) R’s lambda \(\times\) (range of \(t\))\(^3\).

