Solution





B1. (a) \(p(2.5) = \tfrac12(0.2126 + 0.3888) = 0.3007\). Error \(0.3007 - 0.2918 = 0.0089\) (the line lies above the curve, which bends upwards on \([2, 3]\)). (b) \(\tfrac{1^2}{8}\cdot0.0739 = 0.0092\). The bound holds and is nearly reached, because \(|N''|\) is close to its maximum on this interval. (c) \(\tfrac{h^2}{8}\cdot0.0739 \le 0.001 \Rightarrow h^2 \le 0.108 \Rightarrow h \le 0.33\) h: one read every 20 min. C1 confirms: \(h = 0.25\) gives \(5.8\times10^{-4}\). (d) \((0.3888 - 0.2126)/1 = 0.1762\): the central difference at 2.5 with step \(0.5\) (or the forward difference at 2). Close to \(0.1767\) for exact data; with noisy data it inherits all the noise of B6.
B2. (a) 40 cubics, 160 coefficients. Interpolation: 2 per piece, 80. Continuity of \(S'\) and of \(S''\) at the 39 interior points: 39 + 39. Total 158: two are missing. (b) If \(S\), \(S'\), \(S''\) and \(S'''\) all match at \(x_2\), the two neighbouring cubics have the same Taylor expansion there and so are the same cubic. Continuing along, \(S\) is one cubic, which has only 4 coefficients and cannot pass through 41 points. (Requiring it only at \(x_2\) and \(x_{n-1}\) is exactly the not-a-knot condition.)
B3. (a) \(h_1 = h_2 = 1\), \(s_1 = 1\), \(s_2 = -1\): \(1\cdot M_1 + 2(1 + 1)M_2 + 1\cdot M_3 = 6(-1 - 1)\), so \(4M_2 = -12\), \(M_2 = -3\). (b) On \([0, 1]\) at \(x = 0.5\): \(A = B = 0.5\), \(A^3 - A = B^3 - B = -0.375\). \(S(0.5) = 0.5\cdot0 + 0.5\cdot1 + \tfrac16[-0.375\cdot0 + (-0.375)(-3)] = 0.5 + 0.1875 = 0.6875\). Linear: \(0.5\). Parabola \(1 - (x - 1)^2\): \(0.75\). The spline lies between: it bends, but less than the parabola, because its curvature is forced to 0 at the ends. (c) \(S'(0)\): \(A = 1, B = 0\): \(s_1 + \tfrac16[-(3 - 1)\cdot0 + (0 - 1)(-3)] = 1 + 0.5 = 1.5\) (parabola: 2). \(S'(1)\) on \([0, 1]\): \(A = 0\), \(B = 1\): \(1 + \tfrac16[-(0 - 1)\cdot0 + (3 - 1)(-3)] = 1 - 1 = 0\). Expected: the data are symmetric about \(x = 1\), so the peak of the spline is at \(x = 1\).
B4. (a) \(s = (1, -1, 1)\), all \(h = 1\): \[\begin{pmatrix}4 & 1\\ 1 & 4\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}M_2\\ M_3\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}6(-1 - 1)\\ 6(1 + 1)\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}-12\\ 12\end{pmatrix}.\] Diagonally dominant: \(4 &gt; 1\) in both rows (always true for splines: \(2(h_{i-1} + h_i) &gt; h_{i-1} + h_i\)). (b) Forward sweep: \(w = 1/4\), \(b_2 = 4 - \tfrac14\cdot1 = 3.75\), \(d_2 = 12 - \tfrac14(-12) = 15\). Back substitution: \(M_3 = 15/3.75 = 4\), \(M_2 = (-12 - 1\cdot4)/4 = -4\). (c) \(S(1.5)\) on \([1, 2]\): \(0.5\cdot1 + 0.5\cdot0 + \tfrac16(-0.375)(-4 + 4) = 0.5\) (symmetry). \(S(0.5)\) on \([0, 1]\): \(0.5 + \tfrac16(-0.375)(0 - 4) = 0.5 + 0.25 = 0.75\). (d) \(h_1 = 1\), \(h_2 = 2\), \(s_1 = 1\), \(s_2 = -0.5\): \(2(1 + 2)M_2 = 6(-0.5 - 1) = -9\), \(M_2 = -1.5\).
B5. (a) Divided differences of \(y = 0, 1, 0, 1\) at \(x = 0, 1, 2, 3\): first \(1, -1, 1\); second \(-1, 1\); third \(2/3\). \(p(x) = x - x(x - 1) + \tfrac23x(x - 1)(x - 2)\). \(p(0.5) = 0.5 + 0.25 + \tfrac23(0.5)(-0.5)(-1.5) = 0.5 + 0.25 + 0.25 = 1.0\). Natural: \(0.75\). In the end interval the choice matters a lot for wiggly data; in C1 the end condition decides the order of convergence. (b) Natural assumes \(N''(0) = 0\), but it is 0.028: a small error (C3: \(1.3\times10^{-3}\) with \(h = 1\)). Clamped with \(S'(0) = 0\) is worse: the true slope is 0.038, and forcing 0 bends the first interval the wrong way (C3: \(6.5\times10^{-3}\)). At \(t = 10\) the culture is close to the plateau, \(N'(10) = 0.007\), so \(S'(10) = 0\) is a reasonable clamp there. A clamp is only as good as the slope you put in. (c) In the first and last one or two intervals. R’s default is "fmm", Python’s is not-a-knot; both use the data near the ends, but differently. In the interior the influence of the end condition dies out by a factor of about 3.7 per interval.
B6. (a) Forward: \(\sqrt2\cdot0.01/0.25 = 0.057\) (24 % of 0.24). Central: \(0.01/(\sqrt2\cdot0.25) = 0.028\) (12 %). (b) \(h = 1/12\): central \(0.01\cdot12/\sqrt2 = 0.085\), 35 % of the maximum rate. Three times as many reads, three times more noise in each difference. (c) \(E(h) = 0.0128\,h^2 + 0.00707/h\). \(h^3 = 3\cdot0.01/(\sqrt2\cdot0.0768) = 0.276\), \(h^* = 0.65\) h. \(E(h^*) = 0.0054 + 0.0109 = 0.016\), about 7 % of the maximum rate, even with the best step. Same V as in Convergence and cost, with the measurement noise \(\sigma = 0.01\) in the place of \(\varepsilon|N| \approx 10^{-16}\): the bottom of the V moves from \(h \approx 10^{-5}\) to \(h \approx 0.65\), and the best error from \(10^{-12}\) to \(0.016\). The smoothing spline in C5 does better (0.0067) because it uses all the reads near \(t\), not just two.
B7. (a) \(\lambda = 0\): the interpolating natural spline, df \(= n = 41\). \(\lambda\to\infty\): \(\int f''^2 = 0\) is forced, so \(f\) is a straight line, the least-squares line, df \(= 2\). (b) \(\text{GCV} = 41\,\text{RSS}/(41 - \text{df})^2\): df 6: \(41\cdot0.00472/35^2 = 1.58\times10^{-4}\); df 9.4: \(41\cdot0.00206/31.6^2 = 8.46\times10^{-5}\); df 12: \(41\cdot0.00179/29^2 = 8.73\times10^{-5}\); df 20: \(41\cdot0.00104/21^2 = 9.67\times10^{-5}\). GCV chooses df \(= 9.4\) (as smooth.spline does). Note how flat GCV is between 9 and 12. (c) RSS always decreases as \(\lambda\) decreases; its minimum is 0 at \(\lambda = 0\), the interpolating spline that fits the noise. The denominator \((n - \text{df})^2\) in GCV (and leaving points out in CV) is what penalises fitting the noise. (d) With \(t\) in minutes, \(f''\) (per minute²) is \(3600\) times smaller and \(dt\) is 60 times larger, so \(\int f''^2\,dt\) is \(60^3 = 216\,000\) times smaller. The same curve needs a \(\lambda\) that is \(216\,000\) times larger. The df, the fitted curve and the GCV choice do not change. That is why df is the number to report.
