GCV and leave-one-out CV agree closely (df 9.4 and 9.3) and both put the peak at pseudotime 0.598 with height 2.97, close to the truth (0.6, 3.0). The rough fit (df 22.5) finds a spurious higher, sharper peak (3.22): it follows a few high cells. The stiff fit (df 3.8) flattens the peak to 2.28 and moves it. The residual check flags both: the rough fit has strongly negative lag-1 correlation (\(-0.41\): residuals alternate in sign, the curve chases individual cells), the stiff fit strongly positive (\(+0.52\): long runs of residuals with the same sign, a systematic misfit, i.e. bias). The good fits have \(|r_1| &lt; 0.1\), well inside the \(\pm2/\sqrt{40} = \pm0.32\) expected for independent noise. Python searches a grid with step 0.05 in \(\log_{10}\lambda\) and lands on the same grid point for GCV and CV; R and Rust use a one-dimensional optimiser and agree to 3–4 digits. R’s df for the rough fit differs from Python and Rust in the third decimal: R’s smooth.spline computes it with a slightly different (B-spline) representation.

Background
Facts used below:

Pseudo-random generators. A generator computes a long sequence of numbers from a seed by a fixed rule. A linear congruential generator (LCG) is \(x_{n+1} = (a x_n + c) \bmod m\), \(u_n = x_n/m\). The same seed gives the same sequence; the sequence repeats after at most \(m\) steps. Default generators: R, Mersenne Twister (set.seed); numpy, PCG64 (np.random.default_rng); Rust, StdRng = ChaCha12 (rand). The three give different streams for the same seed.
Inverse transform. If \(U \sim \text{Uniform}(0,1)\) and \(F\) is a CDF, then \(X = F^{-1}(U)\) has CDF \(F\). Exponential with rate \(\lambda\): \(X = -\log(1 - U)/\lambda\) (or \(-\log(U)/\lambda\), since \(1-U\) is uniform too). Discrete: cut \([0, 1)\) at the cumulative probabilities and see where \(U\) falls.
Rejection. To draw from a density \(f\) using a proposal \(g\) with \(f \le M g\): draw \(x \sim g\) and \(v \sim \text{Uniform}(0,1)\); keep \(x\) if \(v &lt; f(x)/(M g(x))\). The acceptance rate is \(1/M\).
Monte Carlo integration. \(\int_a^b f(x)\,dx = (b-a)\,\mathrm{E}[f(U)]\) with \(U \sim \text{Uniform}(a,b)\). With \(y_i = (b-a) f(U_i)\): \[\hat I = \bar y, \qquad \text{SE} = \frac{s}{\sqrt N}, \quad s = \text{sample sd of the } y_i.\] Report \(\hat I \pm 2\,\text{SE}\). A probability is the integral of an indicator: \(\hat p = k/N\), \(\text{SE} = \sqrt{\hat p(1-\hat p)/N}\).
Error rate. The Monte Carlo error falls like \(N^{-1/2}\) in any dimension, if the variance of \(f(U)\) is finite. A grid rule of order \(p\) with \(N\) points in \(d\) dimensions has error about \(N^{-p/d}\).
Importance sampling. \(\int h(x) f(x)\,dx = \mathrm{E}_g[h(X)\,w(X)]\), \(w = f/g\), \(X \sim g\). The proposal \(g\) must be positive wherever \(h f \neq 0\) and should have heavier tails than \(|h| f\). Effective sample size \(\text{ESS} = (\sum w_i)^2 / \sum w_i^2\).

A seeded generator and a Monte Carlo mean with its standard error, for \(\int_0^1 x^2\,dx = 1/3\). The numbers differ between the three languages (different generators); the estimates agree within their standard errors.

