Solution





B1. (a) \(x\): 1, 6, 15, 12, 13, 2, 11, 8 (\(5 \cdot 1 + 1 = 6\); \(5 \cdot 6 + 1 = 31 = 16 + 15\); \(5\cdot 15 + 1 = 76 = 4\cdot16 + 12\); …). \(u\): 0.0625, 0.375, 0.9375, 0.75, 0.8125, 0.125, 0.6875, 0.5. (b) Continuing: 9, 14, 7, 4, 5, 10, 3, 0, then 1 again. Period 16: every value \(0, \dots, 15\) appears exactly once per cycle (full period). (c) 7 → 4 → 5 → 10 → …: the seed 7 is just a different starting point on the same cycle (7 follows 14 in (b)). (d) \(a = 4\): 1, 5, 5, 5, … (\(4 \cdot 5 + 1 = 21 = 16 + 5\)): stuck at a fixed point after one step. \(a = 3\), \(c = 0\): 1, 3, 9, 11, 1, …: period 4. Only special choices of \(a\), \(c\), \(m\) give a full period. (e) Neither is an error: both reruns use other random numbers, so the answers should differ by about the Monte Carlo error. It becomes a problem only if the difference is larger than the error bars, or if the first person cannot reproduce your exact numbers when checking your code (then they need your seed and your software).
B2. (a) \(1 - u = e^{-\lambda x}\), so \(x = -\log(1-u)/\lambda\). (b) \(x = -2\ln(1 - u)\): \(u = 0.3 \to -2\ln 0.7 = 0.713\) h; \(u = 0.75 \to -2\ln 0.25 = 2.773\) h; \(u = 0.95 \to -2\ln 0.05 = 5.991\) h (the arrows in the lecture figure). (c) \(u = 0.5\): \(x = 2\ln 2 = 1.386\) h, below the mean of 2 h (the exponential is skewed). (d) Cut points \(0, 0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 1\): A \(= [0, 0.3)\), C \(= [0.3, 0.5)\), G \(= [0.5, 0.7)\), T \(= [0.7, 1)\). \(u = 0.45 \to\) C, \(0.95 \to\) T, \(0.3 \to\) C.
B3. (a) \(f'(x) = 20(1-x)^2(1-4x) = 0\) at \(x = 1/4\): \(M = f(1/4) = 20 \cdot 0.25 \cdot 0.75^3 = 2.109\). Acceptance rate \(1/M = 0.474\). (b) \((0.25, 0.9)\): \(f/M = 1 &gt; 0.9\), accept. \((0.8, 0.1)\): \(f(0.8) = 20\cdot0.8\cdot0.2^3 = 0.128\), \(f/M = 0.061 &lt; 0.1\), reject. \((0.5, 0.5)\): \(f(0.5) = 1.25\), \(f/M = 0.593 &gt; 0.5\), accept. (c) About \(1000 M = 2109\) proposals. (d) Near the mode, \(f(x) &gt; 1.5\), so \(f/M &gt; 1\) and every proposal there is accepted, but not as often as it should be: the peak is cut off, and the sample comes from \(\min(f, M)\) normalised, not from \(f\). No warning: the code runs and returns wrong draws. C3(b) shows the bias in the mean.
B4. (a) \(f = 0.01, 0.16, 0.49, 0.81\); estimate \(1.47/4 = 0.3675\). Deviations \(-0.3575, -0.2075, 0.1225, 0.4425\); \(s^2 = 0.38168/3 = 0.1272\), \(s = 0.357\); SE \(= 0.357/2 = 0.178\). The error \(0.034\) is well inside \(\pm 2\,\text{SE} = \pm 0.357\) (a very wide interval: 4 draws say little). (b) \(N = (0.298/0.001)^2 \approx 89\,000\). (c) 100 times more: \(10^6\). Trapezoid: error \(\propto N^{-2}\), so \(\sqrt{10} \approx 3.2\) times more points.
B5. (a) \(10, 10^3, 10^6, 10^{10}\) evaluations: 10 µs, 1 ms, 1 s, \(10^4\) s \(\approx\) 2.8 hours. (b) \(d = 2\): \(\sqrt{10^6} = 1000\). \(d = 6\): \(10^{6/6} = 10\). \(d = 10\): \(10^{0.6} = 3.98\), so 3 points (\(4^{10} = 1\,048\,576 &gt; 10^6\)). (c) \(p/d = 1/2\): \(d = 4\) for the trapezoid rule, \(d = 8\) for Simpson. Above that, the grid’s error falls more slowly than Monte Carlo’s.
B6. (a) Overlap when \(p - 9 \le s \le p + 999\): \(1009\) start positions per promoter. There are \(G - L + 1 = 999\,991\) possible starts, so \(p_\text{hit} = 50 \cdot 1009 / 999\,991 = 0.05045\) (the promoters are far apart, so the windows do not overlap). (b) \(\mathrm{E}[K] = 200 \cdot 0.05045 = 10.09\); \(\mathrm{sd}(K) = \sqrt{200 \cdot 0.05045 \cdot 0.94955} = 3.095\). \((18 - 10.09)/3.095 = 2.56\) sd above the mean. (c) \(\hat p = 122/10\,000 = 0.0122\); SE \(= \sqrt{0.0122 \cdot 0.9878 / 10\,000} = 0.0011\); \((k+1)/(B+1) = 123/10\,001 = 0.0123\). For SE \(= 0.1 p\): \(\sqrt{p(1-p)/B} = 0.1p \Rightarrow B = (1-p)/(0.01 p) \approx 8100\). In general \(B \approx 100/p\): you need about 100 simulations as extreme as the data. (d) For example: assembly gaps and unmappable repeats (no peak can be called there); GC content and chromatin accessibility vary along the genome (motifs and peaks are not uniform); peaks cannot overlap each other; promoters may overlap or sit at the region ends. Each changes \(p_\text{hit}\) or breaks independence. A simulation only needs a way to draw placements under the null (shuffle within accessible, GC-matched regions), not a formula for the distribution of \(K\).
B7. (a) \(10^5 \cdot 2.87\times10^{-7} = 0.029\) hits expected; \(P(\text{none}) = e^{-0.029} = 0.97\). Then \(\hat p = 0\) and SE \(= 0\): the method reports “exactly zero, with no uncertainty”, which is wrong. (b) \(-\tfrac{x^2}{2} + \tfrac{(x-5)^2}{2} = -\tfrac{x^2}{2} + \tfrac{x^2 - 10x + 25}{2} = 12.5 - 5x\). \(w(5) = e^{-12.5} = 3.7\times10^{-6}\), \(w(5.5) = e^{-15} = 3.1\times10^{-7}\), \(w(6) = e^{-17.5} = 2.5\times10^{-8}\). (c) \(h w = 0\), \(e^{-13} = 2.26\times10^{-6}\), \(e^{-15.5} = 1.86\times10^{-7}\), \(e^{-19} = 5.6\times10^{-9}\); estimate \(= (0 + 2.26\times10^{-6} + 1.86\times10^{-7} + 5.6\times10^{-9})/4 = 6.1\times10^{-7}\). The right order of magnitude from only 4 draws (plain Monte Carlo would need millions to see a single hit). (d) Half (the proposal is centred at 5). Every draw above 5 contributes a small, similar weight, instead of almost all draws contributing 0 and a rare draw contributing 1.
