For \(x^{-1/4}\) the SEs agree (0.0015) and the estimates are within \(\pm 2\) SE of \(4/3\). For \(x^{-3/4}\) the SE changes by a factor of up to 6 between seeds, and several estimates miss 4 by more than 2 SE (R seed 2: \(3.75 \pm 0.079\), 3 SE off).
\(\int_0^1 x^{-3/2}\,dx = \infty\) and \(\int_0^1 x^{-1/2}\,dx = 2\). The variance of \(x^{-3/4}\) is infinite, so the CLT and the formula SE \(= s/\sqrt N\) do not apply: the rare draws very close to 0 make the estimate jump, and the sample sd is usually too small. The variance of \(x^{-1/4}\) is \(2 - (4/3)^2 = 0.22\), finite. The fix here is a change of variables \(x = v^4\), which turns the integral into \(\int_0^1 4\,dv\).

