Background
An initial value problem (IVP) is an ODE together with a start value: \[y'(t) = f(t, y), \qquad y(t_0) = y_0.\] \(y\) can be a number or a vector (a system).

Euler’s method. With step \(h\) and \(t_n = t_0 + nh\): \[y_{n+1} = y_n + h\,f(t_n, y_n).\] It comes from the Taylor step \(y(t + h) = y(t) + h\,y'(t) + \tfrac{h^2}{2}y''(\xi)\) with the last term dropped. For a system, the same formula with vectors.
Local error (one step, starting on the exact curve): \(\approx \tfrac{h^2}{2}\,y''\), so \(O(h^2)\). Global error at a fixed time \(T\) (after \(T/h\) steps): \(O(h)\). Euler is order 1: halving \(h\) halves the error, and the slope of \(\log(\text{error})\) against \(\log h\) is 1.
Step halving. For an order-1 method, \(y_h - y_{h/2}\) estimates the error of \(y_{h/2}\). No exact solution needed.
Stability. On \(y' = -ky\) (\(k &gt; 0\)) Euler gives \(y_{n+1} = (1 - kh)\,y_n\). It decays only if \(|1 - kh| &lt; 1\), that is \(h &lt; 2/k\); for \(1/k &lt; h &lt; 2/k\) it decays with sign flips. Near an equilibrium of a nonlinear ODE, the same rule holds with \(k = -f'(y^*)\).
Higher order. Write \(x^{(m)} = g(t, x, x', \dots, x^{(m-1)})\) as a system with \(y_1 = x\), \(y_2 = x'\), …, \(y_m = x^{(m-1)}\). It needs \(m\) start values.

The running examples:

Logistic growth \(N' = rN(1 - N/K)\) with \(K = 1.2\) (OD), \(N_0 = 0.05\), \(r = 0.8\) per hour. Exact solution \(N(t) = K / \bigl(1 + (K/N_0 - 1)e^{-rt}\bigr)\).
Drug clearance \(C' = -kC\), \(k = 0.8\) per hour, \(C_0 = 10\) mg/L. Exact \(C(t) = C_0 e^{-kt}\).
SIR epidemic \(S' = -\beta SI/N\), \(I' = \beta SI/N - \gamma I\), \(R' = \gamma I\) with \(N = 10\,000\), \(\beta = 0.3\) and \(\gamma = 0.1\) per day, \((S, I, R)(0) = (9990, 10, 0)\). Exact peak \(I_{\max} = S_0 + I_0 - \tfrac{N}{R_0}\bigl(1 + \ln\tfrac{R_0 S_0}{N}\bigr) = 3007.96\), with \(R_0 = \beta/\gamma = 3\).
24-hour oscillator \(x'' = -\omega^2 x\), \(\omega = 2\pi/24\) per hour, \(x(0) = 1\), \(x'(0) = 0\). Exact \(x(t) = \cos\omega t\), amplitude \(\sqrt{x^2 + (x'/\omega)^2} = 1\) for all \(t\).

