Solutions






Solution — by hand





B1. (a) \(C' = u/V - kC = 0.4 - 0.8\,C\) (mg/L per hour), \(C(0) = 0\). Steady state \(C^* = 0.4/0.8 = 0.5\) mg/L. (b) \(m' = \alpha - \delta m = 10 - 0.2\,m\) (molecules per minute), \(m(0) = 0\). Steady state \(m^* = \alpha/\delta = 50\) molecules. (c) \(A_g' = -1.5\,A_g\), \(A_b' = 1.5\,A_g - 0.8\,A_b\) (mg per hour), \((A_g, A_b)(0) = (100, 0)\). The only steady state is \((0, 0)\): everything is eventually cleared. The absorption term leaves the gut and enters the blood, so it appears with both signs. (d) \(S' = -\beta SI/N\) (out of \(S\)), \(I' = \beta SI/N - \gamma I\) (in from \(S\), out to \(R\)), \(R' = \gamma I\) (in from \(I\)). Adding: \((S + I + R)' = 0\). Every flow leaves one box and enters another, so the total stays \(N\).
B2. (a) Slopes \(0\), \(0.18\), \(0.24\), \(0.18\), \(0\), \(-0.30\) (for example \(0.8 \cdot 0.6 \cdot (1 - 0.5) = 0.24\)). The right-hand side contains no \(t\): the growth rate depends only on the current density (an autonomous ODE), so the field looks the same in every column. (b) See the direction field slide: the curve from 0.05 is an S-shape rising to 1.2; the curve from 1.5 decreases towards 1.2. (c) Steepest where the slope is largest, at \(N = K/2 = 0.6\) (slope 0.24 per hour), around \(t = 4\) h. Equilibria: \(N = 0\) and \(N = K = 1.2\). (d) At a crossing point both curves would have the same \((t, N)\) and so the same slope, and from there on the ODE determines one single future. (For the ODEs in this course the solution through each point is unique.)
B3. (a) \(N_1 = 0.05 + 0.8 \cdot 0.05 \cdot 0.95833 = 0.08833\), \(N_2 = 0.08833 + 0.8 \cdot 0.08833 \cdot 0.92639 = 0.15380\). (b) \(f(0.05) = 0.038333\), so \(N(0.5) \approx 0.05 + 0.5 \cdot 0.038333 = 0.069167\). \(f(0.069167) = 0.8 \cdot 0.069167 \cdot 0.94236 = 0.052144\), so \(N(1) \approx 0.069167 + 0.5 \cdot 0.052144 = 0.095239\). (c) Errors at \(t = 1\): \(0.08833 - 0.10587 = -0.01754\) with \(h = 1\) and \(0.09524 - 0.10587 = -0.01063\) with \(h = 0.5\). A factor 1.65. For small \(h\) expect a factor 2 (order 1); with \(h = 1\) the step is too big for the asymptotic rule. (Note that with \(h = 1\), after one step the global error equals the local error.)
B4. (a) \(C_{n+1} = C_n + h(-kC_n) = (1 - kh)C_n\); apply \(n\) times. (b) \(h = 0.5\): factor \(0.6\), \(C_2 = 3.6\) (exact \(C(1) = 4.49\)). \(h = 1\): factor \(0.2\), \(C_2 = 0.4\) (exact \(C(2) = 2.02\)). \(h = 2\): factor \(-0.6\), \(C_1 = -6\), \(C_2 = 3.6\) (exact \(C(4) = 0.41\)). \(h = 3\): factor \(-1.4\), \(C_1 = -14\), \(C_2 = 19.6\) (exact \(C(6) = 0.08\)). The first three decay (\(|1 - kh| &lt; 1\)); only \(h = 0.5\) and \(h = 1\) stay positive. (c) Decays for \(h &lt; 2/k = 2.5\) h; never changes sign for \(h \le 1/k = 1.25\) h. At \(h = 1.25\) h the factor is exactly 0: Euler says the drug is completely gone after one step. (d) \(h &lt; 2/8 = 0.25\) h. One fast rate sets the step for the whole simulation, even long after the fast drug has disappeared. Stiffness and implicit methods deals with this.
B5. (a) \(e^{0.1} - 1.1 = 0.0051709\), \(e^{0.01} - 1.01 = 0.000050167\): a factor 103, about \(100 = 10^2\). \(h^2/2 = 0.005\) and \(0.00005\): order 2 locally. (b) \(1.1^{10} = 2.593742\), error \(2.718282 - 2.593742 = 0.124539\). \(1.01^{100} = 2.704814\), error \(0.013468\). A factor 9.25, about \(10 = 10^1\): order 1 globally. (\(e\,h/2 = 0.136\) and \(0.0136\).) (c) \(\tfrac{e}{2}h = 10^{-6}\) gives \(h = 7.4\times10^{-7}\): about 1.36 million steps for six digits of \(e\).
B6. (a) Ratio 2 per halving: order 1 (Euler). (b) Ratio 4: order 2. (c) Ratio 16: order 4 (classical Runge–Kutta, next unit). (d) Ratio about 2, not 4: the method behaves as order 1. Most likely a bug, for example a slope evaluated at the wrong point or time, which leaves an \(O(h)\) error in each step.
B7. (a) \(\beta SI/N = 0.3 \cdot 9990 \cdot 10 / 10\,000 = 2.997\) and \(\gamma I = 1\). \(S_1 = 9990 - 2.997 = 9987.003\), \(I_1 = 10 + 2.997 - 1 = 11.997\), \(R_1 = 0 + 1 = 1\). Sum: \(10\,000\). (b) \(I' \approx 0.2\,I\): exponential growth with doubling time \(\ln 2 / 0.2 = 3.47\) days. (c) \(I' = I(\beta S/N - \gamma) = 0\) with \(I &gt; 0\) gives \(S = \gamma N/\beta = N/R_0 = 3333\) people. (After the peak, each infected person infects fewer than one other.) (d) \(h &lt; 2/\gamma = 20\) days. Stability is not the problem for SIR at reasonable steps; accuracy is.
B8. (a) \(y_1' = y_2\), \(y_2' = -\omega^2 y_1\). One step: \(y_1 = 1 + 1 \cdot 0 = 1\), \(y_2 = 0 - 1 \cdot 0.06854 \cdot 1 = -0.06854\). (b) Before: amplitude 1. After: \(\sqrt{1 + (0.06854/0.2618)^2} = \sqrt{1.06854} = 1.0337\). In general, \((y_1 + hy_2)^2 + (y_2 - h\omega^2y_1)^2/\omega^2 = y_1^2 + 2hy_1y_2 + h^2y_2^2 + y_2^2/\omega^2 - 2hy_1y_2 + h^2\omega^2y_1^2 = (1 + \omega^2h^2)\,(y_1^2 + y_2^2/\omega^2)\). The amplitude grows by \(\sqrt{1 + \omega^2h^2} &gt; 1\) in every step, whatever \(h\). (c) \(y_1 = x\), \(y_2 = x'\): \(y_1' = y_2\), \(y_2' = -(c\,y_2 + \kappa\,y_1)/m\). (d) \(y_1 = x\), \(y_2 = x'\), \(y_3 = x''\): \(y_1' = y_2\), \(y_2' = y_3\), \(y_3' = y_1 y_3 - y_2^2\). Three start values: \(x(0)\), \(x'(0)\), \(x''(0)\).









