Solution — by hand





B1. (a) \(F_4 = \begin{pmatrix} 1&1&1&1\\1&-i&-1&i\\1&-1&1&-1\\1&i&-1&-i\end{pmatrix}\). \(X_0 = 3 + 1 + 1 + 3 = 8\). \(X_1 = 3 + 1(-i) + 1(-1) + 3(i) = 2 + 2i\). \(X_2 = 3 - 1 + 1 - 3 = 0\). \(X_3 = 3 + 1(i) + 1(-1) + 3(-i) = 2 - 2i\). (b) \(X_0 = 8 = \sum x_j\). \(X_3 = 2 - 2i = \overline{X_1}\). Yes to both. (c) \(a_0 = 8/4 = 2\), \(a_1 = 2\cdot2/4 = 1\), \(b_1 = -2\cdot 2/4 = -1\), \(a_2 = 0/4 = 0\). So \(p(t) = 2 + \cos\tfrac{2\pi t}{24} - \sin\tfrac{2\pi t}{24}\). \(p(6) = 2 + \cos\tfrac{\pi}{2} - \sin\tfrac\pi2 = 2 + 0 - 1 = 1\). ✓ (Also \(p(0) = 3\), \(p(12) = 1\), \(p(18) = 3\).) (d) \(A = 2|X_1|/4 = 2\sqrt8/4 = \sqrt2 \approx 1.41\). \(\arg X_1 = \pi/4\), so \(\theta = -\pi/4\); peak at \(-\tfrac18 \cdot 24 = -3\) h, i.e. at 21 h. Check: \(\cos s - \sin s = \sqrt2\cos(s + \pi/4)\) is largest at \(s = -\pi/4\), i.e. \(t = -3 \equiv 21\) h, where \(p = 2 + \sqrt2 = 3.41\), above every sample. The interpolant peaks between the samples at 18 h and 0 h. (e) \(x_1 = \tfrac14(X_0 + X_1 i + X_2 i^2 + X_3 i^3) = \tfrac14(8 + (2+2i)i + 0 + (2-2i)(-i)) = \tfrac14(8 + 2i - 2 - 2i - 2) = \tfrac14 \cdot 4 = 1\). ✓
B2. (a) Even: \((x_0, x_2) = (3, 1)\), \(E = (4, 2)\). Odd: \((x_1, x_3) = (1, 3)\), \(O = (4, -2)\). (b) \(w^0 = 1\), \(w^1 = -i\). \(X_0 = E_0 + O_0 = 8\); \(X_1 = E_1 + (-i)O_1 = 2 + 2i\); \(X_2 = E_0 - O_0 = 0\); \(X_3 = E_1 - (-i)O_1 = 2 - 2i\). Same as B1. (c) One: \((-i)\cdot O_1\) (and multiplying by \(-i\) is only a swap and a sign change). Against 16 for the matrix. (d) \(n = 2^{10} = 1024\): \(n^2 \approx 10^6\), \(n\log_2 n \approx 10^4\). \(n = 2^{20} \approx 10^6\): \(n^2 \approx 1.1\times10^{12}\) (about 18 minutes at \(10^9\)/s), \(n\log_2 n \approx 2.1\times10^7\) (0.02 s). The ratio \(n/\log_2 n\) is about 50 000.
B3. (a) Index 3 is \(k = 2\): 2 cycles in 48 h, period 24 h. Python: X[2]. (b) Mean \(= 192/24 = 8\). Amplitude \(= 2 \cdot 18/24 = 1.5\). (c) \(\arg(-18i) = -\pi/2\), \(\theta = \pi/2\): a quarter of a period after \(t = 0\), i.e. 6 h. (d) Index 23 is \(k = 22 \equiv -2\): fft(x)[23] $= = $ 0+18i. (e) \(|-18| = 18\): same amplitude, 1.5. \(\arg(-18) = \pi\), \(\theta = -\pi\): half a period off, peak at \(-12 \equiv\) 12 h. A real negative coefficient is a cosine turned upside down.
B4. (a) \(f_N = 1/(2\cdot4) = 0.125\) per hour: periods down to 8 h. (b) 24 h (\(f = 0.0417\)) and 12 h (\(0.0833\)): resolved. 8 h (\(0.125\)): exactly at Nyquist, see (d). 6 h (\(0.1667\)): alias \(|0.1667 - 0.25| = 0.0833\), reported as 12 h. 4.8 h (\(0.2083\)): alias \(0.0417\), reported as 24 h (the example from the lecture). 4 h (\(0.25 = f_s\)): alias 0: sampled at the same phase every time, it looks like a constant shift of the mean. (c) With \(\Delta t = 2\) h, \(f_N = 0.25\) (periods down to 4 h): 24, 12, 8, 6 and 4.8 h are all resolved; 4 h sits exactly at Nyquist. (d) \(p = 0\): \(\cos(\pi j) = 1, -1, 1, -1, \ldots\): full amplitude. \(p = 2\): \(\cos(\pi j - \pi/2) = 0, 0, 0, \ldots\): invisible. At exactly the Nyquist frequency the amplitude you see is \(|\cos(2\pi p/8)|\), between 0 and the truth, depending on phase. Sample faster than twice the frequency, not exactly twice. (e) In theory anything below 6 h (Nyquist for 12 h is \(\Delta t &lt; 6\) h). With 6-h sampling, a 12-h rhythm is at Nyquist (phase-dependent, (d)); slightly faster than 6 h, two or three samples per cycle with noise give a very poor estimate of amplitude and phase, and anything faster than 12 h is aliased. In practice sample at least 4–6 times per cycle of interest (2–3 h for 12-h rhythms).
B5. (a) \((h * y)_0 = \tfrac12(y_0 + y_3) = 2.5\) (wrap-around), \((h*y)_1 = \tfrac12(2 + 1) = 1.5\), \((h*y)_2 = 2.5\), \((h*y)_3 = 3.5\). The first entry mixes the start with the end. (b) \(Y = (10, -2+2i, -2, -2-2i)\), \(H = (1, \tfrac12(1 - i), 0, \tfrac12(1+i))\). Products: \(10\); \(\tfrac12(1-i)(-2+2i) = \tfrac12(4i) = 2i\); \(0\); \(\tfrac12(1+i)(-2-2i) = -2i\). Inverse: \(z_0 = \tfrac14(10 + 2i - 2i) = 2.5\); \(z_1 = \tfrac14(10 + 2i\cdot i + 0 + (-2i)(-i)) = \tfrac14(10 - 2 - 2) = 1.5\); similarly \(z_2 = 2.5\), \(z_3 = 3.5\). Same as (a). (c) \(|H_1| = \tfrac12\sqrt2 = 0.707\): the \(k = 1\) wave keeps 71 % of its amplitude. \(H_2 = 0\): the Nyquist wave \((+1, -1, +1, -1)\) averages to exactly zero. (d) \((0.5, 1.5, 2.5, 3.5, 2)\). Pad both to length \(4 + 2 - 1 = 5\): then nothing wraps around.
B6. (a) \(k = 48/24 = 2\) and \(k = 48/22 = 2.18\). They are less than one bin apart: both show up as a peak at \(k = 2\) (the 22-h one with leakage). No. (b) \(1/22 - 1/24 = 2/528 = 1/264\), so \(T \ge 264\) h, 11 days. (c) Neither. Faster sampling raises the Nyquist frequency but keeps the frequency step \(1/T\). Zero-padding interpolates the spectrum on a finer grid (it helps to locate a single peak, as in C5) but the peaks keep their width \(\approx 1/T\), so two close peaks still merge.









