The three languages draw different random noise, so the digits differ; the pattern does not. (a) On the raw trace the error at the ends stays large (13 for \(k_\text{max} = 15\), twice the noise): the start (about 95) and end (about 20) do not match, and the filter rings. (b) After removing a straight line the ends are as good as the middle, and the overall error halves. (c) Around \(k_\text{max} = 10\)–\(15\) (periods down to 8–12 h): small \(k_\text{max}\) removes part of the signal (the decay at the start is fast), large \(k_\text{max}\) lets noise through (at \(k_\text{max} = 60\) the error rises again). This is the same bias–noise trade-off as for smoothing splines in Splines.









