Background
Facts used below:

Similarity. If \(A = QR\) with \(Q\) orthogonal, then \(RQ = Q^TAQ\) has the same eigenvalues as \(A\). The QR algorithm repeats \(A_k = Q_kR_k\), \(A_{k+1} = R_kQ_k\).
Convergence. For eigenvalues ordered \(|\lambda_1| &gt; |\lambda_2| &gt; \dots\), the subdiagonal entry \(a_{i+1,i}\) of \(A_k\) shrinks like \(|\lambda_{i+1}/\lambda_i|^k\). If two eigenvalues have the same modulus it does not shrink.
Shift. \(A_k - \mu I = Q_kR_k\), \(A_{k+1} = R_kQ_k + \mu I\). The rate for the last entry becomes \(|\lambda_n - \mu| / |\lambda_{n-1} - \mu|\). Shifts: \(\mu = a_{nn}\) (Rayleigh: \(a_{nn}\) is a Rayleigh quotient, so this is the Rayleigh quotient iteration of Power iteration and friends acting on the last row), or the Wilkinson shift, the eigenvalue of the trailing 2 × 2 block \(\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\ b & c \end{pmatrix}\) closer to \(c\): \[\mu = c - \frac{\operatorname{sign}(\delta)\, b^2}{|\delta| + \sqrt{\delta^2 + b^2}}, \qquad \delta = \frac{a - c}{2}, \quad \operatorname{sign}(0) = 1.\] Deflation: once \(|a_{n,n-1}|\) is below a tolerance, \(a_{nn}\) is an eigenvalue; remove the last row and column.
2 × 2 eigenvalues. \(\lambda^2 - \operatorname{tr}(A)\lambda + \det(A) = 0\). A real matrix with \(\operatorname{tr}^2 &lt; 4\det\) has a complex pair \(\lambda = a \pm bi\): in a linear ODE model, an oscillation with period \(2\pi/b\) that grows (\(a &gt; 0\)) or decays (\(a &lt; 0\)).
Sensitivity. Symmetric \(A\): a perturbation \(E\) moves each eigenvalue by at most \(\|E\|\). Nonsymmetric \(A = VDV^{-1}\): by up to \(\kappa(V)\|E\|\). For a chain of \(n\) compartments with one feedback of size \(\varepsilon\), \((\lambda + 1)^n = \varepsilon\), so the eigenvalues move by \(\varepsilon^{1/n}\).
Lanczos. For symmetric \(A\) and a unit start vector \(\mathbf q_1\) (\(\mathbf q_0 = \mathbf 0\), \(\beta_0 = 0\)): \[\mathbf w = A\mathbf q_j - \beta_{j-1}\mathbf q_{j-1}, \quad \alpha_j = \mathbf q_j^T\mathbf w, \quad \mathbf w \leftarrow \mathbf w - \alpha_j\mathbf q_j, \quad \beta_j = \|\mathbf w\|, \quad \mathbf q_{j+1} = \mathbf w/\beta_j.\] The Ritz values after \(m\) steps are the eigenvalues of the tridiagonal \(T_m\) with \(\alpha_1, \dots, \alpha_m\) on the diagonal and \(\beta_1, \dots, \beta_{m-1}\) beside it. In floating point the \(\mathbf q_j\) lose orthogonality unless we reorthogonalise: \(\mathbf w \leftarrow \mathbf w - Q_j(Q_j^T\mathbf w)\).
Implicit centring. With column means \(\boldsymbol\mu\) and \(X_c = X - \mathbf 1\boldsymbol\mu^T\): \(X_c\mathbf v = X\mathbf v - \mathbf 1(\boldsymbol\mu^T\mathbf v)\) and \(X_c^T\mathbf w = X^T\mathbf w - \boldsymbol\mu(\mathbf 1^T\mathbf w)\). PCA needs the top eigenpairs of \(X_c^TX_c\); \(\sqrt{\lambda_i}\) are the singular values of \(X_c\).

The basic calls in each language. R’s eigen does both kinds of matrix; numpy splits them into np.linalg.eigh/eigvalsh (symmetric, ascending order) and np.linalg.eig/eigvals (general); nalgebra has symmetric_eigen (unsorted) and complex_eigenvalues (values only: nalgebra has no eigenvectors for nonsymmetric matrices).

