B1. (a) \(\|\mathbf a_1\| = \sqrt5\), \(\mathbf q_1 = (2, 1)/\sqrt5\), \(r_{11} = \sqrt5\). \(r_{12} = (2 \cdot 1 + 1 \cdot 2)/\sqrt5 = 4/\sqrt5\). \(\mathbf v = (1, 2) - \tfrac45(2, 1) = (-\tfrac35, \tfrac65)\), \(r_{22} = \tfrac35\sqrt5 = 3/\sqrt5\), \(\mathbf q_2 = (-1, 2)/\sqrt5\). So \[Q = \frac{1}{\sqrt5}\begin{pmatrix} 2 & -1 \\ 1 & 2 \end{pmatrix}, \qquad R = \begin{pmatrix} \sqrt5 & 4/\sqrt5 \\ 0 & 3/\sqrt5 \end{pmatrix}.\] (b) \(RQ = \begin{pmatrix} 2 + \tfrac45 & -1 + \tfrac85 \\ \tfrac35 & \tfrac65 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 2.8 & 0.6 \\ 0.6 & 1.2 \end{pmatrix}\). Trace \(4\), determinant \(3.36 - 0.36 = 3\), as for \(A\) (\(3 + 1\) and \(3 \cdot 1\)). The diagonal has moved towards 3 and 1. (c) \(0.6 \cdot (1/3)^k &lt; 10^{-12}\) needs \(k \log_{10}3 &gt; 12 + \log_{10}0.6 = 11.78\), so \(k &gt; 24.7\): about 25 more steps, 26 in all. C2 counts exactly 26 unshifted steps for \(B\).
B2. (a) \(Q = P\), \(R = I\) (or \(Q = -P\), \(R = -I\), which is what R returns). \(RQ = P\): the iteration does not move. (b) \(+1\) and \(-1\): equal modulus, \(|\lambda_2/\lambda_1| = 1\), so the subdiagonal shrinks by a factor 1 per step, i.e. not at all. (c) \(P - 0.5I\) has eigenvalues \(0.5\) and \(-1.5\), ratio \(1/3\): the off-diagonal shrinks by about 1/3 per step (the quiz shows \(3.4\times10^{-5}\) after 10 steps). A shift close to an eigenvalue is best: \(\mu = 0.9\) gives \(0.1\) and \(-1.9\), ratio \(0.05\). With \(\mu\) exactly 1, \(P - I\) is singular and one step deflates.
B3. (a) \(\lambda^2 + 0.1\lambda + 0.5 = 0\): \(\lambda = -0.05 \pm \tfrac12\sqrt{0.01 - 2} = -0.05 \pm i\sqrt{0.4975} = -0.05 \pm 0.7053i\). (b) Period \(2\pi/0.7053 = 8.91\) time units. Per period the amplitude is multiplied by \(e^{-0.05 \cdot 8.91} = e^{-0.445} = 0.64\). (c) \((-0.05 + 0.7053i) + (-0.05 - 0.7053i) = -0.1 = \operatorname{tr}J\); \(0.05^2 + 0.7053^2 = 0.0025 + 0.4975 = 0.5 = \det J\). (d) Real orthogonal similarity transformations keep the matrix real, and a real triangular matrix has its (real) eigenvalues on the diagonal. Complex eigenvalues can only show up as a 2 × 2 real block. Also, by fact 2, the two eigenvalues have the same modulus \(\sqrt{0.5}\), so nothing makes \(a_{21}\) shrink.
B4. (a) \(\lambda = -1 \pm \sqrt\varepsilon\): \(-1 \pm 0.01\), \(-1 \pm 10^{-5}\), \(-1 \pm 10^{-8}\). A change \(\varepsilon\) in one entry moves the eigenvalues by \(\sqrt\varepsilon\), far more than \(\varepsilon\). (b) \(\lambda = -1 \pm \varepsilon\): moved by exactly \(\varepsilon\). (c) \(10^{-10/10} = 0.1\); \(10^{-16/10} = 10^{-1.6} = 0.025\). For 20 compartments: \(10^{-16/20} = 10^{-0.8} = 0.16\). Even the rounding of the matrix entries makes the individual eigenvalues uncertain by 16 %. (d) That the number is not meaningful: all 20 eigenvalues are \(-1\) in the exact model, and rounding alone spreads them over a circle of radius 0.16 around \(-1\). Describe the dynamics instead (simulate the time course, or report the mean transit time \(n/k = 20\) h), which are well conditioned.
B5. (a) \(A\mathbf e_1 = (4, 1, 0)\), \(\alpha_1 = 4\), \(\mathbf w = (0, 1, 0)\), \(\beta_1 = 1\), \(\mathbf q_2 = \mathbf e_2\). \(A\mathbf e_2 - \beta_1\mathbf q_1 = (1, 3, 1) - (1, 0, 0) = (0, 3, 1)\), \(\alpha_2 = 3\), \(\mathbf w = (0, 0, 1)\), \(\beta_2 = 1\), \(\mathbf q_3 = \mathbf e_3\), \(\alpha_3 = 2\). (b) \(T_2 = \begin{pmatrix} 4 & 1 \\ 1 & 3 \end{pmatrix}\): \(3.5 \pm \sqrt{0.25 + 1} = 3.5 \pm 1.118\), so \(4.618\) and \(2.382\). The largest Ritz value is below the largest eigenvalue 4.732, the smallest is above 1.268: Ritz values always lie inside the range of the eigenvalues and approach the ends from inside. (c) \(T_3 = A\): \(A\) is already tridiagonal and \(Q_3 = I\), so after \(n\) steps Lanczos has done a (trivial) tridiagonal reduction and the Ritz values are exact. (d) From \(\mathbf e_3\): \(\alpha_1 = 2\), \(\beta_1 = 1\), \(\mathbf q_2 = \mathbf e_2\), \(\alpha_2 = 3\); \(T_2 = \begin{pmatrix} 2 & 1 \\ 1 & 3 \end{pmatrix}\), Ritz values \(2.5 \pm 1.118 = 3.618, 1.382\). Now the smallest eigenvalue (1.268) is approximated better. The start vector matters: it should not be close to orthogonal to the wanted eigenvectors (libraries start from a random vector).
B6. (a) Dense: \(2\times10^4 \cdot 2\times10^3 \cdot 8 = 3.2\times10^8\) bytes = 320 MB. Sparse: \(0.13 \cdot 4\times10^7 = 5.2\times10^6\) non-zeros, times 12 bytes = 62 MB. (b) 100 000 cells: dense 1.6 GB, sparse 310 MB. Gene covariance: \(2000^2 \cdot 8 = 32\) MB. Cell–cell: \(10^{10} \cdot 8 = 80\) GB. (c) \(10^3 \times 1.8\) s \(= 1800\) s, half an hour (and 3.2 GB for the matrix alone). (d) \(X\mathbf v\) and \(X^T\mathbf w\) each take about \(2\,\text{nnz} \approx 10^7\) operations, plus \(O(n + p)\) for the centring: about \(2\times10^7\) per step. The dense SVD: \(4 \cdot 2\times10^4 \cdot (2\times10^3)^2 = 3.2\times10^{11}\), the work of about 15 000 Lanczos steps. We need 30–40.
