Background
The toggle switch (from Newton for systems of equations). Two genes repress each other:
\[\frac{du}{dt} = \frac{\alpha}{1 + v^{n}} - u, \qquad \frac{dv}{dt} = \frac{\alpha}{1 + u^{n}} - v, \qquad \alpha = 3,\ n = 2.\]
Steady states: U on \(= \big((3+\sqrt5)/2,\ (3-\sqrt5)/2\big) = (2.618034, 0.381966)\), middle \(= (1.213412, 1.213412)\), V on \(= (0.381966, 2.618034)\). The biased switch has a 3 % weaker promoter for V: \(\alpha_u = 3\) in the first equation, \(\alpha_v = 2.9\) in the second.
The Sel’kov model of glycolytic oscillations (\(u\) = ADP, \(v\) = fructose-6-phosphate):
\[\frac{du}{dt} = -u + a v + u^2 v, \qquad \frac{dv}{dt} = b - a v - u^2 v, \qquad a = 0.08,\ b = 0.6.\]
Facts used below:

Fixed point \(\mathbf x^*\): \(\mathbf F(\mathbf x^*) = \mathbf 0\). In one variable it is stable if \(f'(x^*) &lt; 0\) and unstable if \(f'(x^*) &gt; 0\); a small perturbation behaves like \(\delta_0 e^{f'(x^*)t}\).
Nullclines: the curve \(\dot u = 0\) (there the flow is vertical) and the curve \(\dot v = 0\) (flow horizontal). Fixed points are where they cross.
Linearisation: \(\dot{\boldsymbol\delta} \approx J(\mathbf x^*)\,\boldsymbol\delta\) with the Jacobian \(J_{ij} = \partial F_i/\partial x_j\). If \(J\mathbf v = \lambda \mathbf v\), then \(\boldsymbol\delta(t) = c\,e^{\lambda t}\mathbf v\); a general start is a sum of such terms.
Stability: stable if all eigenvalues have \(\mathrm{Re}\,\lambda &lt; 0\); unstable if any has \(\mathrm{Re}\,\lambda &gt; 0\); undecided by linearisation if the largest real part is \(0\).
\(2\times2\) shortcut: with \(\tau = \operatorname{tr} J\), \(\Delta = \det J\): \(\lambda = \big(\tau \pm \sqrt{\tau^2 - 4\Delta}\big)/2\). \(\Delta &lt; 0\): saddle. \(\Delta &gt; 0\): stable if \(\tau &lt; 0\), unstable if \(\tau &gt; 0\); a spiral if \(\tau^2 &lt; 4\Delta\), otherwise a node. \(\tau = 0 &lt; \Delta\): centre (undecided). An eigenvector for \(\lambda\) is \(\mathbf v = (J_{12},\ \lambda - J_{11})\) (if \(J_{12} \ne 0\)).
Separatrix: the stable direction of a saddle continues as a curve that separates the basins of attraction. Running the ODE backwards in time (times decreasing, or the right-hand side multiplied by \(-1\)) from \(\mathbf x^* \pm \varepsilon\,\mathbf v_{\text{stable}}\) traces it out.
Limit cycle: an isolated closed orbit. In two variables, an unstable fixed point inside a region that trajectories cannot leave forces one.
Library routines. Trajectories: R deSolve::ode (default lsoda), Python scipy.integrate.solve_ivp (default RK45), Rust ode_solvers::Dopri5; what they do inside is the topic of Runge–Kutta and adaptive step size. Eigenvalues (how they are computed: How eigen() works, and big problems): R eigen, Python np.linalg.eigvals and np.linalg.eig, Rust nalgebra’s complex_eigenvalues. For a non-symmetric matrix eigen sorts by modulus, eigvals and nalgebra do not sort at all: never read stability from the first entry.

The toggle switch as an ODE, one trajectory, and the eigenvalues at U on, in each language. We use tight tolerances (rtol = 1e-10, atol = 1e-12) on this page so that the three solvers print the same digits:

