# t =  1.0   u = 1.323446   v = 0.968978
# t =  5.0   u = 1.628535   v = 0.872293
# t = 20.0   u = 2.587075   v = 0.393849
# [-0.33333333 -1.66666667]


// rust/examples/phase_plane_background.rs  —  cargo run --example phase_plane_background
// Background: one trajectory of the toggle switch (ode_solvers, Dopri5),
// and the eigenvalues of the Jacobian at "U on" (nalgebra).
use nalgebra::Matrix2;
use ode_solvers::{Dopri5, System, Vector2};

const ALPHA: f64 = 3.0;
const N: f64 = 2.0;

struct Toggle;
impl System&lt;f64, Vector2&lt;f64&gt;&gt; for Toggle {
    // ode_solvers wants system(t, x, dx): write the derivatives into dx
    fn system(&self, _t: f64, x: &Vector2&lt;f64&gt;, dx: &mut Vector2&lt;f64&gt;) {
        let (u, v) = (x[0], x[1]);
        dx[0] = ALPHA / (1.0 + v.powf(N)) - u; // du/dt
        dx[1] = ALPHA / (1.0 + u.powf(N)) - v; // dv/dt
    }
}

// Jacobian, as in Newton for systems
fn jac(u: f64, v: f64) -&gt; Matrix2&lt;f64&gt; {
    Matrix2::new(-1.0, -ALPHA * N * v.powf(N - 1.0) / (1.0 + v.powf(N)).powi(2),
                 -ALPHA * N * u.powf(N - 1.0) / (1.0 + u.powf(N)).powi(2), -1.0)
}

fn main() {
    // Dopri5::new(system, t0, t_end, output spacing, x0, rtol, atol)
    let mut solver = Dopri5::new(Toggle, 0.0, 20.0, 1.0, Vector2::new(0.5, 0.3), 1e-10, 1e-12);
    solver.integrate().expect("integration failed");
    let (ts, xs) = (solver.x_out(), solver.y_out()); // output at t = 0, 1, 2, ..., 20
    for i in [0, 1, 5, 20] {
        println!("t = {:4.1}   u = {:.6}   v = {:.6}", ts[i], xs[i][0], xs[i][1]);
    }
    let (u_on, v_on) = ((3.0 + 5f64.sqrt()) / 2.0, (3.0 - 5f64.sqrt()) / 2.0); // "U on", exact
    println!("{:?}", jac(u_on, v_on).complex_eigenvalues().as_slice());
}

// Output:
// t =  0.0   u = 0.500000   v = 0.300000
// t =  1.0   u = 1.323446   v = 0.968978
// t =  5.0   u = 1.628535   v = 0.872293
// t = 20.0   u = 2.587075   v = 0.393849
// [Complex { re: -1.6666666666666665, im: 0.0 }, Complex { re: -0.3333333333333333, im: 0.0 }]



Each library wants the right-hand side in its own shape: deSolve func(t, x, parms) returning a list; solve_ivp fun(t, x) returning the derivatives; ode_solvers a type implementing the System trait with system(&self, t, x, dx) that writes the derivatives into dx. ode_solvers uses its own copy of nalgebra, so in Rust we keep the ODE state (ode_solvers::Vector2) and the linear algebra (nalgebra::Matrix2) apart and copy numbers between them.

Solutions






Solution — by hand





B1. (a) \(f(x) = x\left(\dfrac{x}{1+x^2} - 0.4\right) = 0\): \(x = 0\), or \(x = 0.4(1 + x^2)\), i.e. \(0.4x^2 - x + 0.4 = 0\), \(x = \dfrac{1 \pm \sqrt{1 - 0.64}}{0.8} = \dfrac{1 \pm 0.6}{0.8}\): \(x = 0.5\) and \(x = 2\). (b) \(f'(x) = \dfrac{2x}{(1+x^2)^2} - 0.4\). \(f'(0) = -0.4\): stable (gene off). \(f'(0.5) = \dfrac{1}{1.5625} - 0.4 = 0.24\): unstable (the threshold). \(f'(2) = \dfrac{4}{25} - 0.4 = -0.24\): stable (gene on). A one-gene bistable switch: positive feedback gives memory. (c) \(\delta(t) \approx 0.1\,e^{-0.24t}\); halves after \(\ln 2/0.24 = 2.9\) time units.
B2. (a) \((0, 0)\): \(\dot u = 3 &gt; 0\), \(\dot v = 3 &gt; 0\), below both nullclines. \((3, 3)\): \(\dot u = 0.3 - 3 &lt; 0\), \(\dot v &lt; 0\), above both. \((2, 0.7)\): \(\dot u = 3/1.49 - 2 = +0.013\), \(\dot v = 3/5 - 0.7 = -0.1\): the thin lens between the middle state and U on (\(u\uparrow\), \(v\downarrow\)). \((0.7, 2)\): mirror image, \(\dot u = -0.1\), \(\dot v = +0.013\): the lens towards V on. (b) At \(v = 2\): \(u = 3/5 = 0.6\), \(\dot v = 3/1.36 - 2 = +0.206\), up. At \(v = 0.5\): \(u = 3/1.25 = 2.4\), \(\dot v = 3/6.76 - 0.5 = -0.056\), down. (c) The u-nullcline passes through the middle state. Above it (at \(v = 2\)) the flow moves up along the nullcline, below it (at \(v = 0.5\)) down: on both sides the flow carries points away from the middle state. A point slightly off the middle state on this curve does not return, so the middle state is unstable.
B3. \(A_1\): \(\tau = -6\), \(\Delta = 8\), \(\tau^2 - 4\Delta = 4 &gt; 0\): stable node, \(\lambda = (-6 \pm 2)/2 = -2, -4\). \(A_2\): \(\Delta = 1 - 4 = -3 &lt; 0\): saddle; \(\lambda = (2 \pm 4)/2 = 3, -1\). \((A_2 - 3I)\mathbf v = 0\) gives \(\mathbf v = (1, 1)\), repelling; \((A_2 + I)\mathbf v = 0\) gives \((1, -1)\), attracting. \(A_3\): \(\tau = -2\), \(\Delta = 1 + 4 = 5\), \(\tau^2 - 4\Delta = -16 &lt; 0\): stable spiral, \(\lambda = -1 \pm 2i\). \(A_4\): \(\tau = 0\), \(\Delta = 4\): centre, \(\lambda = \pm 2i\). Linearisation cannot decide for a nonlinear model.
B4. (a) \(p = 6 \cdot 0.381966/(1.145898)^2 = 2.291796/1.313082 = 1.745356\), \(q = 6\cdot 2.618034/(7.854102)^2 = 15.708204/61.686918 = 0.254644\). \(J = \begin{pmatrix} -1 & -1.745356\\ -0.254644 & -1\end{pmatrix}\), \(\tau = -2\), \(\Delta = 1 - 0.444444 = 0.555556\), \(\lambda = (-2 \pm \sqrt{4 - 2.222222})/2 = -1 \pm 0.666667\): \(-0.3333\) and \(-1.6667\). Stable node. (b) \(\tau = -2\), \(\Delta = 1 - pq\), \(\lambda = -1 \pm \sqrt{1 - (1 - pq)} = -1 \pm \sqrt{pq}\). (c) \(pq = \dfrac{2\alpha v}{(1+v^2)^2}\cdot\dfrac{2\alpha u}{(1+u^2)^2} = 4\alpha^2 uv\cdot\dfrac{u^2}{\alpha^2}\cdot\dfrac{v^2}{\alpha^2} = \dfrac{4(uv)^3}{\alpha^2} = \dfrac{4}{\alpha^2}\). So \(\lambda = -1 \pm 2/\alpha\), both negative for every \(\alpha &gt; 2\): the outer states are stable whenever they exist. At \(\alpha = 2\), \(\lambda = 0\). (d) \(\mathbf v = (J_{12}, \lambda - J_{11}) = (-1.745356, -1/3 + 1) = (-1.745356, 0.666667)\); length \(\sqrt{3.046268 + 0.444444} = 1.868345\); normalised \((-0.9342, 0.3568)\), or with the sign flipped \((0.9342, -0.3568)\): more U and less V.
B5. (a) \(J(1, 1)^T = (-1 - g, -g - 1) = (-1 - g)(1, 1)\), eigenvalue \(-1 - g\). \(J(1, -1)^T = (-1 + g, -g + 1) = (-1 + g)(1, -1)\), eigenvalue \(-1 + g\). (b) \(s^2 = 1.47234\), \(g = 6\cdot1.2134/(2.47234)^2 = 7.2804/6.11246 = 1.1911\). \(\lambda = -2.1911\) along \((1, 1)\) and \(+0.1911\) along \((1, -1)\): a saddle. The growing direction \((1, -1)\), one protein up and the other down, is the cell deciding. (c) \(g = \dfrac{2 s (1 + s^2) s}{(1 + s^2)^2} = \dfrac{2s^2}{1 + s^2}\). \(g &gt; 1 \iff 2s^2 &gt; 1 + s^2 \iff s &gt; 1 \iff \alpha = s + s^3 &gt; 2\). So for \(\alpha &gt; 2\) the middle state is a saddle, for \(\alpha &lt; 2\) it is a stable node (and the only state): the bifurcation of the lecture.
B6. (a) First entries: \(0.9342(c_1 + c_2) = 0.1\); second: \(0.3568(c_1 - c_2) = 0\). So \(c_1 = c_2 = 0.1/(2\cdot0.9342) = 0.05352\). (b) \(\boldsymbol\delta(t) = 0.05352\big(e^{-5t/3}\mathbf v_1 + e^{-t/3}\mathbf v_2\big)\), i.e. \(\delta_u = 0.05\,(e^{-5t/3} + e^{-t/3})\), \(\delta_v = 0.0191\,(e^{-5t/3} - e^{-t/3})\). At \(t = 3\): \(\delta_u = 0.05\cdot0.37462 = 0.0187\), \(\delta_v = 0.0191\cdot(-0.36114) = -0.0069\). V dips although only U was pushed: the extra U represses V’s gene, then both recover. (The full nonlinear ODE gives \((0.0182, -0.0067)\); with a push of \(0.01\) instead of \(0.1\) the agreement is three digits.) (c) The fast term is gone; \(0.05\,e^{-t/3} = 0.001\) at \(t = 3\ln 50 = 11.7\) protein lifetimes.
B7. (a) The sum is \(\dot u + \dot v = -u + b\), which is zero at the fixed point: \(u^* = b = 0.6\). The second equation: \(b = (a + u^2)v\), \(v^* = 0.6/0.44 = 1.3636\). (b) Partial derivatives: \(\partial_u(-u + av + u^2 v) = -1 + 2uv\), \(\partial_v = a + u^2\); the second row is \(-2uv\) and \(-(a + u^2)\). \(\Delta = (-1 + 2uv)(-(a+u^2)) + 2uv(a + u^2) = a + u^2 = a + b^2\). For \(b = 0.6\): \(2uv = 1.6364\), \(\tau = -1 + 1.6364 - 0.44 = 0.1964\), \(\Delta = 0.44\), \(\tau^2 - 4\Delta &lt; 0\): unstable spiral, \(\lambda = 0.0982 \pm 0.6560i\). For \(b = 1\): \(v^* = 0.9259\), \(2uv = 1.8519\), \(\tau = -0.2281\), \(\Delta = 1.08\): stable spiral, \(\lambda = -0.1141 \pm 1.0330i\). (c) \(\tau = \dfrac{2b^2}{a + b^2} - 1 - a - b^2 = 0 \iff 2b^2 = (1 + a + b^2)(a + b^2) \iff b^4 + (2a - 1)b^2 + a + a^2 = 0\). With \(a = 0.08\): \(B^2 - 0.84B + 0.0864 = 0\) for \(B = b^2\), \(B = (0.84 \pm 0.6)/2 = 0.72\) or \(0.12\), so \(b = 0.849\) and \(b = 0.346\). Between these the fixed point is unstable and the model oscillates.
B8. (a) If \(u = v\) then \(\dot u = \alpha/(1 + v^2) - u = \alpha/(1 + u^2) - v = \dot v\), so \(u - v\) stays \(0\). The trajectory stays on the diagonal and can only end at the steady state there: the middle state. The diagonal is therefore the stable manifold of the saddle, i.e. the separatrix of the symmetric switch. (b) The Jacobian of \(-\mathbf F\) is \(-J\), with eigenvalues \(-\lambda\) and the same eigenvectors. The saddle’s stable direction (\(\lambda = -2.19\)) becomes unstable (\(+2.19\)) in backward time, so a backward run from \(\mathbf x^* + \varepsilon\mathbf v_{\text{stable}}\) moves away from the saddle along the separatrix. Forwards, the separatrix attracts towards the saddle only along itself; any error (even rounding) is amplified by the unstable eigenvalue and the run leaves it. (c) \(10^{-8}e^{0.19t} = 0.1\) gives \(t = \ln(10^7)/0.19 \approx 85\) time units (compare the lecture’s figure).









