classify agrees with B3 and with the lecture. The finite-difference Jacobian changes the eigenvalues by about \(10^{-8}\), the size of the finite-difference error. That is harmless when \(|\mathrm{Re}\,\lambda| \ge 0.1\), but an eigenvalue with \(|\mathrm{Re}\,\lambda| &lt; 10^{-7}\) computed this way has a sign you cannot trust (C6). Rust prints exponents without a leading zero (2.5e-9).
