The real part is positive for \(b = 0.4, 0.6, 0.8\) and negative for \(b = 0.2\) and \(1.0\), consistent with the thresholds \(0.346\) and \(0.849\) from B7(c).
Both starts end on the same cycle: \(u\) between \(0.245\) and \(1.518\), period \(9.6226\). The tighter tolerance changes the minimum only in the 4th decimal (Python’s RK45 at \(10^{-6}\) gives \(0.2452\)) and the period not at all in the shown digits.
At \(b = 1\) the range after \(t = 100\) is \(8\times10^{-6}\): a damped spiral still ringing, not a limit cycle. The “period” printed (\(6.08\) in R, \(6.01\) in Python and Rust) is the period of that dying ringing at amplitude \(10^{-5}\), and depends on the solver. Always check the amplitude before reporting a period.

