The slowest eigenvalue \(-1 + 2/\alpha\) goes to 0 as \(\alpha \to 2\), and the time to relax grows like \(1/(\alpha - 2)\): at \(\alpha = 2.01\) a simulation of 100 time units is still \(0.08\) away from the steady state (“critical slowing down”). At \(\alpha = 2\) the true \(\lambda_{\max}\) at \((1, 1)\) is exactly 0. The finite-difference Jacobian gives \(-1.5\times10^{-8}\) (“stable”) at \((1, 1)\) and \(+3.7\times10^{-9}\) (“unstable”) at a point \(10^{-6}\) away. Both are finite-difference error (\(\approx \sqrt\varepsilon\)), not information. When \(|\mathrm{Re}\,\lambda|\) is not much larger than the errors in \(J\) and in \(\mathbf x^*\), linearisation cannot decide; use exact derivatives, analyse the nonlinear terms, or simulate (for a long time).

Background
To solve \(y' = f(t, y)\), \(y(t_0) = y_0\) with step \(h\):

Euler (order 1, 1 evaluation of \(f\)): \(y_{n+1} = y_n + h k_1\), \(k_1 = f(t_n, y_n)\).
Midpoint (order 2, 2 evaluations): \(k_2 = f(t_n + \tfrac h2, y_n + \tfrac h2 k_1)\), \(y_{n+1} = y_n + h k_2\).
Heun (order 2, 2 evaluations): \(k_2 = f(t_n + h, y_n + h k_1)\), \(y_{n+1} = y_n + \tfrac h2 (k_1 + k_2)\).
RK4 (order 4, 4 evaluations): \(k_2 = f(t_n + \tfrac h2, y_n + \tfrac h2 k_1)\), \(k_3 = f(t_n + \tfrac h2, y_n + \tfrac h2 k_2)\), \(k_4 = f(t_n + h, y_n + h k_3)\), \(y_{n+1} = y_n + \tfrac h6 (k_1 + 2k_2 + 2k_3 + k_4)\).
Order. A method of order \(p\) has global error \(\approx C h^p\). Halving \(h\) divides the error by \(2^p\), so \(p \approx \log_2(\text{err}(h)/\text{err}(h/2))\), and on a log–log plot the error is a line with slope \(p\). The error of a single step is \(\propto h^{p+1}\).
Embedded pair. Two answers of orders \(q\) and \(q+1\) from the same stages (Euler and Heun; or orders 4 and 5 in Dormand–Prince). Their difference estimates the error of the step. With the allowed error \(\text{sc}_i = \texttt{atol} + \texttt{rtol}\cdot\max(|y_{n,i}|, |y_{n+1,i}|)\) per component, the scaled error is \[\text{err} = \sqrt{\tfrac1m \textstyle\sum_{i=1}^m \big(\text{est}_i / \text{sc}_i\big)^2}.\] The step is accepted if \(\text{err} \le 1\), otherwise rejected and retried. Either way the next step is \(h_{\text{new}} = h\cdot \min\!\big(5, \max(0.2,\ 0.9\, \text{err}^{-1/(q+1)})\big)\).
Library solvers. R: deSolve::ode with method = "ode45" (Dormand–Prince, defaults rtol = atol = 1e-6) or "rk4" (fixed step = spacing of times, see rk). Python: scipy.integrate.solve_ivp with method="RK45" (the default; rtol=1e-3, atol=1e-6). Rust: the ode_solvers crate with Dopri5 (adaptive) and Rk4 (fixed step); you give both tolerances.

The running examples:

Logistic OD curve \(N' = rN(1 - N/K)\), \(r = 0.8\) per hour, \(K = 1.2\), \(N_0 = 0.05\); exact \(N(t) = K / \big(1 + (K/N_0 - 1)e^{-rt}\big)\).
Drug clearance \(C' = -0.8\, C\), \(C_0 = 10\) mg/L; exact \(C(t) = 10 e^{-0.8t}\).
SIR epidemic (as in Initial value problems and Euler’s method): \(S' = -\beta SI/N\), \(I' = \beta SI/N - \gamma I\), \(R' = \gamma I\) with \(N = 10\,000\) people, \(\beta = 0.3\) and \(\gamma = 0.1\) per day, \((S, I, R)(0) = (9990, 10, 0)\). Exact peak \(I_{\max} = 3007.961\).
Lotka–Volterra (hares \(x\), lynx \(y\)): \(x' = ax - bxy\), \(y' = cxy - dy\) with \(a = 1\), \(b = 0.1\), \(c = 0.075\), \(d = 1.5\) per year, \((x, y)(0) = (10, 5)\).
Repressilator (Elowitz & Leibler 2000, dimensionless): \(m_i' = -m_i + \alpha/(1 + p_j^{\,n}) + \alpha_0\), \(p_i' = -\beta(p_i - m_i)\), \((i, j) = (1,3), (2,1), (3,2)\), with \(\alpha = 216\), \(\alpha_0 = 0.216\), \(\beta = 5\), \(n = 2\), start \(m = (1, 0, 0)\), \(p = (0, 0, 0)\).

