B5. (a) \(S\): \(10^{-6} + 10^{-3}\cdot 9990 \approx 10\) people. \(I\): \(10^{-6} + 10^{-3}\cdot 10 \approx 0.01\) people. \(R\): \(10^{-6}\) people (only atol, since \(R = 0\)). (b) In fractions, \(I\) is allowed \(10^{-6} + 10^{-3}\cdot 0.001 = 2\times10^{-6}\), i.e. \(0.02\) people: still fine, but now the atol term is as large as the rtol term. With a much smaller seed (one infected per million) it would not be. (c) \(10 + 0.01 \approx 10\) people: an error as large as \(I\) itself is allowed at the start. The solver may take big, inaccurate steps in the early exponential phase; the error in the seed is then amplified, and the whole epidemic is shifted (C6). (d) The allowed error \(10^{-6}\) M is 1000 times larger than the concentration: the solver may return anything for that species. Fixes: set atol far below the scale (e.g. \(10^{-15}\), one value per variable if the scales differ), or rescale the model to nM so that values are of order 1.
B6. (a) \(\frac{dV}{dt} = (c - \tfrac dx)\, x(a - by) + (b - \tfrac ay)\, y(cx - d) = (cx - d)(a - by) + (by - a)(cx - d) = 0.\) (b) \(V(10, 5) = 0.75 - 1.5\cdot2.3026 + 0.5 - 1.6094 = -3.8133\). Equilibrium \((20, 10)\): \(V = 1.5 - 1.5(\ln 2 + \ln 10) + 1 - \ln 10 = 2.5 - 4.4936 - 2.3026 = -4.2962\). (c) Larger \(V\) means an orbit further from the equilibrium, so Euler’s orbit spirals outwards: ever larger booms and deeper crashes. A reader would conclude that the populations are unstable and will go extinct, which the model does not say.
B7. (a) \(I' = \beta SI/N - \gamma I = (\beta S/N - \gamma) I = g I\), and \(I &gt; 0\), so $I’ $ has the sign of \(g\). At the peak \(g = 0\): \(S = N\gamma/\beta = 3333.3\). (b) \(g(35.16) = 0.3\cdot0.44261 - 0.1 = 0.0328\); \(g(42.04) = 0.3\cdot0.24045 - 0.1 = -0.0279\). Linear interpolation: \(t = 35.16 + 6.88\cdot 0.0328/(0.0328 + 0.0279) = 38.88\) days, half a day late. The step is long and \(g\) is curved, so a straight line through the two ends is not accurate. A solver interpolates with its dense output (a polynomial of degree 4 in Dormand–Prince) and finds the root of that with a root finder; with a cubic through the two steps we get \(38.37\) (figure on the events slide). (c) Yes: the excursion above 20 lasts 0.8 years, shorter than one step, so \(y - 20\) can be negative at both ends of a step (here at all of \(t = 0, 2.5, \dots, 15\)). Remedies: limit the step (hmax in deSolve, max_step in solve_ivp) to below the shortest excursion, or detect a quantity that changes sign only once per excursion (e.g. \(y' = 0\) at the maximum, then compare the maximum with 20).
