With atol = 10 the error is stuck at 5%, with atol = 1 at about 1%, however small rtol is; with atol = 1e-6 it falls in proportion to rtol. In the first days \(I \approx 10\), so with atol = 10 the allowed error in \(I\) is as large as \(I\) (B5c): the solver takes large steps with a large relative error in \(I\), and that error grows with the epidemic. Tightening only rtol does not reveal it; tightening atol does. (The same holds for the library solvers: solve_ivp with atol=10 gives a few percent error in \(I(60)\) as well.)
