Fast binding, slow catalysis


enzyme &lt;- function(t, y, p) {
  with(as.list(c(y, p)), {
    bind &lt;- kf * E * S - kr * C   # E + S &lt;-&gt; C
    turn &lt;- kcat * C              # C -&gt; E + P
    list(c(S = -bind, E = -bind + turn,
           C = bind - turn, P = turn))
  })
}

k_f = 100\ \mu\text{M}^{-1}\text{s}^{-1} (10^8 per M per s, close to diffusion-limited binding).
Binding settles in about 1 ms; the substrate is used up in about 10 s.





Four species, four mass-action ODEs. Two conservation laws hold exactly: E + C = 1 (total enzyme) and S + C + P = 10 (total substrate). The figure uses a log time axis so that both time scales are visible: in the first millisecond about 0.9 uM of enzyme binds substrate (C rises to 0.89) while S hardly changes; then for 10 s the complex slowly turns substrate into product. On a linear time axis the fast phase would be invisible: a vertical jump of C at t = 0. That invisible jump is the source of all the trouble.

Backward Euler with Newton in R
beuler &lt;- function(f, jac, y0, h, n, tol = 1e-10) {
  Y &lt;- matrix(NA, n + 1, length(y0)); Y[1, ] &lt;- y &lt;- y0
  I &lt;- diag(length(y0))
  for (i in 1:n) {
    yold &lt;- y                                     # Newton starts at y_n
    for (k in 1:20) {
      delta &lt;- solve(I - h * jac(y), -(y - yold - h * f(y)))
      y &lt;- y + delta
      if (sqrt(sum(delta^2)) &lt; tol) break
    }
    Y[i + 1, ] &lt;- y
  }
  Y
}

f(y) and jac(y) are the enzyme rates and their 4\times4 Jacobian (written out in the exercise).
The inner loop is newton_sys from Newton for systems of equations with \mathbf F = \mathbf G, J_G = I - hJ.


Fourteen lines. The outer loop is time stepping, the inner loop is Newton. For simplicity f does not depend on t (true for our kinetic models). Real codes do not iterate to 1e-10: they stop when the Newton correction is well below the time-stepping error, and they reuse the same Jacobian and its LU factorisation for many steps (“modified Newton”), because forming and factorising J is the expensive part. A production solver also checks whether Newton converges at all, and if not, retries with a smaller h.

Golden-section search in R
golden &lt;- function(f, a, b, tol = 1e-8) {
  g &lt;- (sqrt(5) - 1) / 2                      # 0.618...
  x1 &lt;- b - g * (b - a); f1 &lt;- f(x1)
  x2 &lt;- a + g * (b - a); f2 &lt;- f(x2)
  while (b - a &gt; tol) {
    if (f1 &lt; f2) { b &lt;- x2; x2 &lt;- x1; f2 &lt;- f1; x1 &lt;- b - g * (b - a); f1 &lt;- f(x1) }
    else         { a &lt;- x1; x1 &lt;- x2; f1 &lt;- f2; x2 &lt;- a + g * (b - a); f2 &lt;- f(x2) }
  }
  (a + b) / 2
}
golden(sse, 0, 1)
# [1] 0.3753873

One new f per pass through the loop. Width &lt; 10^{-8} after 38 passes.
Cannot fail on a unimodal function: no start value, no derivative, never leaves [a, b].


Nine lines. In each branch: shrink the interval, shift the surviving point into its new role, and evaluate one new point. The stopping rule is on the bracket width, an absolute tolerance; for a parameter of size 1000 you would want tol relative to |x|. On a function that is not unimodal it still returns a point, a local minimum (or a point near the edge); it gives no warning. We will see that at the end.

Background
To minimise a function \(f\) of one variable on an interval:

Minimum and root. At a smooth minimum \(x^*\), \(f'(x^*) = 0\) and \(f''(x^*) \ge 0\). A root of \(f'\) can also be a maximum (\(f'' &lt; 0\)). Maximising a log-likelihood \(\ell\) is minimising \(f = -\ell\).
Bracket. Three points \(a &lt; c &lt; b\) with \(f(c) &lt; f(a)\) and \(f(c) &lt; f(b)\) enclose a (local) minimum. \(f\) is unimodal on \([a, b]\) if it has a single minimum there and no other dips.
Golden section. With \(g = (\sqrt5 - 1)/2 = 0.6180\), evaluate \(x_1 = b - g(b - a)\) and \(x_2 = a + g(b - a)\). If \(f(x_1) &lt; f(x_2)\) keep \([a, x_2]\), otherwise keep \([x_1, b]\). One of the two points is reused, so each step costs one evaluation and shrinks the bracket by \(g\): after \(m \ge 2\) evaluations the width is \(g^{m-1}(b - a)\). Linear convergence.
Parabolic interpolation. The vertex of the parabola through \((a, f_a)\), \((b, f_b)\), \((c, f_c)\) is \[x_\text{new} = b - \frac12\,\frac{(b-a)^2(f_b - f_c) - (b-c)^2(f_b - f_a)}{(b-a)(f_b - f_c) - (b-c)(f_b - f_a)} .\] Superlinear (order about 1.32) near the minimum; can go anywhere far from it.
Brent’s method combines the two: parabolic steps while they behave, golden-section steps otherwise. R: optimize (default tol \(=\varepsilon^{1/4} \approx 1.2\times10^{-4}\), absolute). Python: scipy.optimize.minimize_scalar with bounds and method="bounded" (default xatol \(= 10^{-5}\)). Rust: argmin’s BrentOpt (default absolute part \(10^{-5}\)).
Newton for minimisation. \(x_{k+1} = x_k - f'(x_k)/f''(x_k)\): the vertex of the local parabola, which is Newton’s method on \(f'\). Quadratic near a minimum; if \(f''(x_k) &lt; 0\) the local parabola opens downwards and the step heads for a maximum.
The flat bottom. Near a minimum \(f(x^* + \delta) \approx f(x^*) + \tfrac12 f''(x^*)\delta^2\). The change is lost in rounding when \(\tfrac12 f''\delta^2 &lt; \varepsilon|f(x^*)|\), so a method that compares values of \(f\) can locate \(x^*\) only to about \(|\delta| \approx \sqrt{\varepsilon}\sqrt{2|f(x^*)|/f''(x^*)}\), with \(\sqrt\varepsilon = 1.49\times10^{-8}\). A root of \(f'\) can be found to about \(\varepsilon\).
Convexity. If \(f'' \ge 0\) on the whole interval, \(f\) is convex: a local minimum is the global minimum. Otherwise a local method can return a local minimum without any warning.

The running examples of the lecture:

mRNA decay. Fraction of an mRNA left after transcription stops: \(t = 0, 0.5, 1, 2, 4, 6, 8\) h, \(y = 1.000, 0.804, 0.684, 0.484, 0.217, 0.094, 0.083\). Model \(e^{-kt}\); the least-squares (Gaussian maximum-likelihood) rate minimises \(\text{SSE}(k) = \sum_i (y_i - e^{-kt_i})^2\); \(\hat k = 0.3753873095\) per hour.
Zero-truncated Poisson (from Newton and secant methods). UMI counts 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 in 52, 30, 12, 5, 1 cells; \(n = 100\), \(\sum x_i = 173\). Minus the log-likelihood is \(f(\lambda) = -173\log\lambda + 100\lambda + 100\log(1 - e^{-\lambda})\); the MLE is \(\hat\lambda = 1.2184695258\).
Circadian period. A simulated bioluminescence recording every 4 h for 6 days (36 values, given in C5). \(\text{RSS}(T)\) is the residual sum of squares of the best sine wave with period \(T\); the global minimum is at \(T = 24.63\) h.

