// rust/examples/optimisation_1d_background.rs  —  cargo run --example optimisation_1d_background
// Background: minimise the decay-rate SSE with Brent's method (argmin's BrentOpt,
// Rust's counterpart of R's optimize and scipy's minimize_scalar).
use argmin::core::{CostFunction, Error, Executor, State};
use argmin::solver::brent::BrentOpt;

const T: [f64; 7] = [0.0, 0.5, 1.0, 2.0, 4.0, 6.0, 8.0]; // hours
const Y: [f64; 7] = [1.000, 0.804, 0.684, 0.484, 0.217, 0.094, 0.083]; // fraction left

// argmin wants the objective as a type with a `cost` method
struct Sse;
impl CostFunction for Sse {
    type Param = f64;
    type Output = f64;
    fn cost(&self, k: &f64) -&gt; Result&lt;f64, Error&gt; {
        Ok(T.iter().zip(Y.iter()).map(|(t, y)| (y - (-k * t).exp()).powi(2)).sum())
    }
}

fn main() -&gt; Result&lt;(), Error&gt; {
    // BrentOpt stops when the bracket is below eps*|x| + t: here eps = sqrt(machine eps), t = 1e-10
    let solver = BrentOpt::new(0.0, 1.0).set_tolerance(f64::EPSILON.sqrt(), 1e-10);
    let res = Executor::new(Sse, solver).configure(|s| s.max_iters(100)).run()?;
    let k = *res.state().get_best_param().unwrap();
    println!("{:.7}", k);                          // decay rate (per hour)
    println!("{:.9}", res.state().get_best_cost()); // SSE at the minimum
    println!("{:.6}", 2f64.ln() / k);               // half-life (hours)
    Ok(())
}
// Output:
// 0.3753873
// 0.002042809
// 1.846485



Rust prints numbers in its own format (e.g. 1e-8 rather than 1e-08); the values are the same unless the text says otherwise. The minimisers do not all take the same steps, so the last digits of a result computed with a loose tolerance can differ between languages.

Solutions






Solution





B1. (a) \(f'(x) = x^2 - 1 = 0\) at \(x = \pm1\). \(f''(x) = 2x\): \(f''(1) = 2 &gt; 0\), a minimum with \(f(1) = -2/3\); \(f''(-1) = -2 &lt; 0\), a maximum with \(f(-1) = 2/3\). (b) Newton on \(f'\): \(x_{k+1} = x_k - (x_k^2 - 1)/(2x_k)\). From \(-0.5\): \(-0.5 - (-0.75)/(-1) = -1.25\), then \(-1.25 - 0.5625/(-2.5) = -1.025\): towards \(-1\), the maximum. From 2: \(2 - 3/4 = 1.25\), then \(1.25 - 0.5625/2.5 = 1.025\): towards the minimum. Root finding on \(f'\) cannot tell them apart. (c) \(f(0) = 0\), \(f(1) = -0.667\), \(f(3) = 6\): yes, a bracket. \(f(-3) = -6\), \(f(-1) = 0.667\), \(f(0) = 0\): the middle value is the largest, so this brackets a maximum, not a minimum. (d) \(f(-3) = -6 &lt; f(1) = -0.667\): the smallest value on \([-3, 3]\) is at the end point \(-3\), and \(f\) keeps falling beyond it. A local method near 1 returns 1, the only interior minimum. “Minimum” always means “on which interval?”.
B2. (a) \(x_1 = 4 - 0.618\cdot3 = 2.1459\), \(f(x_1) = 2.1459 - 2\ln 2.1459 = 0.6188\). \(x_2 = 1 + 0.618\cdot3 = 2.8541\), \(f(x_2) = 0.7566\). \(f(x_1) &lt; f(x_2)\): keep \([1, 2.8541]\). (b) The old \(x_1 = 2.1459\) becomes the new \(x_2\) (reused, \(f = 0.6188\)). New \(x_1 = 2.8541 - 0.618\cdot1.8541 = 1.7082\), \(f(1.7082) = 0.6373\). \(0.6373 &gt; 0.6188\): keep \([1.7082, 2.8541]\). (Step 3 would reuse 2.1459 as \(x_1\) and add \(x_2 = 2.4164\).) (c) Widths \(3\), \(1.8541\), \(1.1459\). Ratios \(1.8541/3 = 0.618\) and \(1.1459/1.8541 = 0.618\). After four evaluations the minimum 2 is inside \([1.7082, 2.8541]\). (d) \(3\cdot0.618^{m-1} \le 10^{-6}\) gives \(m - 1 \ge \ln(3.33\times10^{-7})/\ln 0.618 = 30.99\), so \(m = 32\) evaluations. Bisection on \(f'\): \(3\cdot0.5^{m} \le 10^{-6}\) gives \(m \ge 21.5\), so 22 evaluations of \(f'\). Golden section is about 1.44 times slower (\(\ln 2/\ln 1.618\)), the price for not needing \(f'\).
B3. (a) \(g(0) = 5\), \(g(1) = 2\), \(g(3) = 2\). With \(a = 0, b = 1, c = 3\): numerator \(1\cdot(2 - 2) - 4\cdot(2 - 5) = 12\), denominator \(1\cdot0 - (-2)(-3) = -6\); \(x_\text{new} = 1 - \tfrac12\cdot12/(-6) = 2\), exactly the minimum: the interpolating parabola is \(g\). (b) \(b - a = 0.5\), \(b - c = -1.5\), \(f_b - f_c = -0.11371\), \(f_b - f_a = -0.31093\). Numerator \(0.25(-0.11371) - 2.25(-0.31093) = 0.67117\); denominator \(0.5(-0.11371) - 1.5\cdot0.31093 = -0.52325\); \(x_\text{new} = 1.5 + 0.64134 = 2.1413\). Error 0.14 after three evaluations; golden section after four still had a bracket of width 1.15. The parabola uses the sizes of the values, golden section only their order. (c) \(h(1) = -1\), \(h(2) = 0\), \(h(3) = -1\): the parabola is \(h\) itself, which opens downwards; the formula returns its vertex 2, a maximum. Parabolic interpolation only makes sense when the middle value is the lowest (a bracket); Brent’s method checks that.
B4. (a) \(f'(x) = 1 - 2/x\), \(f''(x) = 2/x^2 &gt; 0\) for every \(x &gt; 0\): \(f\) is convex on its whole domain. (b) \(x - f'/f'' = x - (1 - 2/x)x^2/2 = x - (x^2 - 2x)/2 = 2x - x^2/2\). Then \(x_{k+1} - 2 = -\tfrac12(x_k - 2)^2\), so \(e_{k+1} = -e_k^2/2\): quadratic convergence. (c) \(1 \to 1.5 \to 1.875 \to 1.9921875\); errors \(0.5, 0.125, 0.0078\) (then \(3.1\times10^{-5}\)): correct digits roughly double each step. (d) From 3: \(e_0 = 1\), \(x_1 = 1.5\), then as in (c): converges. From 4: \(x_1 = 8 - 8 = 0\), where \(\ln 0 = -\infty\) and \(f = +\infty\). From 5: \(x_1 = -2.5\), outside the domain (\(\ln\) of a negative number is NaN). From \(e_{k+1} = -e_k^2/2\): converges if \(|e_0| &lt; 2\), i.e. \(0 &lt; x_0 &lt; 4\). Convexity guarantees that the step points downhill, not that it is not too long: \(f''\) is small for large \(x\), the local parabola is too flat, and the step overshoots. (e) “\(f(x_{k+1}) &lt; f(x_k)\), and finite”: \(f(0) = \infty\) and $f(-2.5) = $ NaN both fail it. The fix is to shorten the step until \(f\) decreases (a line search, Gradient descent, Newton and quasi-Newton).
B5. (a) \(f(1 + \delta) = 1 + \delta^2\) rounds to 1 when \(\delta^2 &lt; \varepsilon/2 = 1.1\times10^{-16}\), i.e. \(|\delta| &lt; 1.05\times10^{-8}\). (R: 1 + (1e-8)^2 == 1 is TRUE, 1 + (1e-7)^2 == 1 is FALSE.) (b) \(1 + 100\delta^2\): \(|\delta| &lt; 1.05\times10^{-9}\), ten times narrower: a larger curvature \(f''\) makes the bottom narrower. \(1000 + \delta^2\): half the gap near 1000 is \(5.7\times10^{-14}\), so \(|\delta| &lt; 2.4\times10^{-7}\), more than 20 times wider: a larger value \(|f(x^*)|\) makes it wider. Both are in the formula \(\sqrt{2\varepsilon|f|/f''}\). (c) \(\sqrt{2\cdot2.2\times10^{-16}\cdot52.61/56.92} = 2.0\times10^{-8}\); relative to 1.218 that is \(1.7\times10^{-8}\): about 8 significant digits. (d) Near \(\hat\lambda\), \(S\) changes linearly: \(|S(\hat\lambda + \delta)| \approx 56.9\,|\delta|\), which stays above the rounding error in \(S\) (about \(\varepsilon \cdot 173/\lambda \approx 3\times10^{-14}\)) down to \(\delta \approx 10^{-15}\). A root changes sign; a minimum only changes \(f\) quadratically, which disappears below \(\varepsilon|f|\) much sooner.
B6. (a) \((22, 26, 30)\): \(9.31 &gt; 4.45 &lt; 10.66\), a bracket. Also \((30, 34, 38)\): \(10.66 &gt; 10.40 &lt; 10.66\), a second, much shallower local minimum. Refine the first: its RSS is less than half of everything else. (b) Her lowest value is 10.45 at \(T = 40\), the end of the grid, and every value is close to the RSS of no rhythm at all (10.72). She would conclude that there is no clear rhythm, or a period of 40 h or more. The grid stepped over the dip. (c) \(10.311 &lt; 10.361\): keep \([21.46, 40]\), which still contains 24.6. Next it compares 28.54 with 32.92: \(10.249 &lt; 10.311\), so it keeps \([28.54, 40]\). Now 24.6 is outside the bracket and can never be found again. It converges to the local minimum at 32.4 h. The first two points both lay on the flat part, and their tiny difference (noise) decided. (d) The dip is 3 h wide; a spacing of 0.5–1 h puts several points inside it. We use 0.25 h in C5, 121 evaluations, which is cheap.
