The root of the score is found to all 16 digits; the minimum of \(-\ell\), even with tol = 1e-15, is off by about \(7\times10^{-10}\) (R, Python) or \(2\times10^{-9}\) (Rust). Asking for a smaller tolerance does not help.
Down to \(\delta = 10^{-6}\) the change in \(f\) is \(\approx \tfrac12\cdot56.92\,\delta^2\) (\(2.85\times10^{-7}\), \(2.85\times10^{-9}\), \(2.85\times10^{-11}\)). At \(10^{-7}\) it is already off by 8% (R) and below that it is rounding noise: whole multiples of \(7.1\times10^{-15}\), the gap between doubles near 52.6, sometimes 0, sometimes negative. The score keeps shrinking by a factor 10 per row all the way down. (Python’s values in the noisy rows differ from R’s and Rust’s: the sum inside nll is rounded slightly differently. That is exactly the point: those digits are noise.)
The prediction \(2.0\times10^{-8}\) matches where the change in \(f\) turns into noise (between \(10^{-7}\) and \(10^{-8}\)). The minimisers did better than the worst case (they land somewhere in the flat region, here by luck close to its centre); you cannot rely on that.

