Solution





B1. (a) \(p_i \ge 0\) for \(i = 1, \dots, 5\) and \(p_1 + \dots + p_5 = 1\): 4 free parameters (the fifth follows from the sum). (b) Irreversible: \(v \ge 0\). Reversible: \(-1000 \le v \le 1000\); the \(\pm 1000\) means “no real limit” (a direction-free reaction can also be split into two irreversible ones). (c) \(c \ge 0\) is active (\(c = 0\)). In \(A\) and \(k\) the gradient is 0, as at any minimum. In \(c\) it is \(+0.105\): the SSE would decrease if \(c\) decreased, but that is forbidden; increasing \(c\) increases the SSE. So it is a constrained minimum, with multiplier 0.105. A gradient of \(-0.105\) would mean that increasing \(c\) (allowed) lowers the SSE, so the optimiser stopped too early.
B2. (a) \(n_i/p_i = \lambda\) for each \(i\), so \(p_i = n_i/\lambda\). The constraint gives \(\sum n_i/\lambda = 1\), \(\lambda = N = 20\), and \(p = (12, 6, 2)/20 = (0.6, 0.3, 0.1)\). (b) \(\Delta f \approx \lambda \cdot 0.05 = 1.0\). Exactly: the new optimum is \(p = 1.05\,(0.6, 0.3, 0.1)\), and \(f\) increases by \(\sum n_i \log 1.05 = 20 \log 1.05 = 0.976\). The multiplier gives the first-order change.
B3. (a) Maize is the fraction that came out negative, so try maize \(= 0\), fish \(= 1 - p_1\): \(\delta^{13}\)C misfit: \(-26p_1 - 18(1 - p_1) + 24 = 6 - 8p_1\); \(\delta^{15}\)N misfit: \(2p_1 + 14(1 - p_1) - 9 = 5 - 12p_1\). Squared misfit \(h(p_1) = (6 - 8p_1)^2 + (5 - 12p_1)^2\). (b) \(h'(p_1) = -16(6 - 8p_1) - 24(5 - 12p_1) = 416 p_1 - 216 = 0\), so \(p_1 = 216/416 = 27/52 = 0.5192\). Diet: plants 0.519, maize 0, fish 0.481. Residuals \(\mathbf r = (6 - 8\cdot 0.5192,\ 5 - 12\cdot 0.5192) = (1.846, -1.231)\), misfit \(1.846^2 + 1.231^2 = 4.923\). (c) \(2A^\top\mathbf r = 2\,(-26\cdot1.846 + 2\cdot(-1.231),\ -10\cdot1.846 + 6\cdot(-1.231),\ -18\cdot1.846 + 14\cdot(-1.231)) = (-100.9,\ -51.7,\ -100.9)\). Plants and fish are free (not at a bound), so by the Lagrange condition their gradient components both equal the multiplier of \(\sum p_i = 1\): \(-100.9\). Moving \(\varepsilon\) from fish to maize changes the misfit by \(\varepsilon(-51.7 - (-100.9)) = +49.2\,\varepsilon &gt; 0\): worse. So maize \(= 0\) is optimal; \(49.2\) is the multiplier of the bound maize \(\ge 0\). The isotope signature of the consumer lies outside the mixing triangle, and the best feasible diet is the nearest point on its plants–fish edge.
B4. (a) Protein: \(2a + b \ge 8\) (above the line through \((4, 0)\) and \((0, 8)\)). Lipid: \(a + 3b \ge 9\) (above the line through \((9, 0)\) and \((0, 3)\)). The feasible region is unbounded (any large mix is allowed), but the cost has a minimum because it grows in every allowed direction. (b) Corners: \((0, 8)\), cost 40; the intersection \(2a + b = 8\), \(a + 3b = 9\): \(a = 3\), \(b = 2\), cost 25; \((9, 0)\), cost 45. Optimum: 3 kg of A and 2 kg of B, cost 25. (c) Protein 9: \(2a + b = 9\), \(a + 3b = 9\) gives \(a = 3.6\), \(b = 1.8\), cost 27: shadow price 2. Lipid 10: \(2a + b = 8\), \(a + 3b = 10\) gives \(a = 2.8\), \(b = 2.4\), cost 26: shadow price 1. Check: \((5, 5) = 2\,(2, 1) + 1\,(1, 3)\). (d) With B costing \(q\): \((3, 2)\) costs \(15 + 2q\), \((9, 0)\) costs 45. They tie at \(q = 15\); then the whole edge from \((3, 2)\) to \((9, 0)\) is optimal (a tie), and above 15 the optimum jumps to pure feed A.
B5. (a) \(x_1\) has the larger objective coefficient (6). Increasing \(x_1\) from 0 with \(x_2 = 0\): oxygen stops it at 1.5, proteome at 3, carbon at 6. Oxygen wins: corner \((1.5, 0)\), ATP 9. (b) Along the oxygen edge \(x_1 = 1.5\), \(x_2\) increases with rate \(+3\) per unit. Carbon stops it at \(x_2 = 4.5\), proteome at \(x_2 = 6\). Corner \((1.5, 4.5)\), ATP \(9 + 13.5 = 22.5\). (c) Edges leaving \((1.5, 4.5)\): back down the oxygen edge (rate \(-3\)), or along the carbon edge with \(x_1\) decreasing and \(x_2\) increasing by the same amount: rate \(-6 + 3 = -3\). No improving edge, so it is optimal. Active: oxygen and carbon. Inactive: proteome (\(6 + 4.5 = 10.5 &lt; 12\)). (d) \((6, 3) = y_C\,(1, 1) + y_O\,(1, 0)\): \(y_C = 3\), \(y_O = 3\); proteome is inactive, so \(y_P = 0\).
B6. (a) Tie: every point on the edge from \((4, 0)\) to \((0, 4)\) gives 4. (b) Unbounded: \(x_1\) can grow without limit (take \(x_2 = 1\)). (c) Infeasible: \(x_1 \ge 3\) and \(x_1 + x_2 \le 2\) with \(x_2 \ge 0\) contradict each other. (d) Optimum \((4, 0)\), value 8. Three constraints are active there (\(x_1 + x_2 = 4\), \(x_1 = 4\), \(x_2 = 0\)), one more than the 2 needed to define a corner: a degenerate corner. Its shadow prices are not unique, and a simplex step there can have length 0.
B7. (a) Columns R1…R5: \[S = \begin{pmatrix} 1 & -1 & -1 & -1 & 0\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 & -1 \\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1 & -2 \end{pmatrix}\begin{matrix}A\\B\\C\end{matrix}\] (b) \(v_1 = v_2 + v_3 + v_4\), \(v_2 = v_5\), \(v_3 + v_4 = 2v_5\). The three rows are independent (each has a pivot in a different column), so rank 3 and null space dimension \(5 - 3 = 2\). (c) From the balances \(v_1 = 3v_5\), so \(v_5 \le 10/3\). Optimum: \(v_1 = 10\), \(v_2 = v_5 = 10/3\), \(v_3 + v_4 = 20/3\). (d) \(v_3, v_4 \in [0, 20/3]\), with \(v_3 + v_4 = 20/3\). (e) R3 out: still \(10/3\) (R4 takes over). R2 out: 0 (no B). R3 and R4 out: 0 (no C): a synthetic lethal pair. (f) \(v_5 = v_1/3\), so one more unit of uptake gives \(1/3\) more growth: shadow price \(1/3\).
B8. (a) Glycolysis (either route) makes \(2g = 20\) P and 20 ATP. P balance: \(20 = \text{RES} + \text{BIO}\). ATP balance: \(20 + 11\,\text{RES} = 5\,\text{BIO}\). Substituting \(\text{RES} = 20 - \text{BIO}\): \(20 + 220 - 11\,\text{BIO} = 5\,\text{BIO}\), so BIO \(= 15\), RES \(= 5\), O2 uptake \(= 2 \cdot 5 = 10 &lt; 15\): the oxygen bound is inactive, its shadow price 0. For general \(g\) the same steps give BIO \(= 1.5g\): glucose shadow price 1.5. (b) P: \(20 = 2 + \text{FER} + \text{BIO}\); ATP: \(20 + 22 + \text{FER} = 5\,\text{BIO}\). From the first, FER \(= 18 - \text{BIO}\); then \(60 - \text{BIO} = 5\,\text{BIO}\): BIO \(= 10\), FER \(= 8\) (8 acetate secreted). (c) ATP minus P: \(11\,\text{RES} + \text{FER} - (\text{RES} + \text{FER} + \text{BIO}) = 5\,\text{BIO} - 2g\), i.e. FER \(= 2\,\text{BIO} - 6\,\text{RES}\). Put into the P balance: \(2g = \text{RES} + 2\,\text{BIO} - 6\,\text{RES} + \text{BIO}\), so \(\text{BIO} = (2g + 5\,\text{RES})/3\). Glucose price \(2/3 = 0.667\); oxygen: RES \(= \text{O}_2/2\), so \(5/6 = 0.833\) per unit oxygen. Valid while FER \(\ge 0\), i.e. up to O2 \(= 10\). (d) Double glucose (\(g = 20\), O2 \(= 4\)): BIO \(= (40 + 10)/3 = 16.7\). Double oxygen (\(g = 10\), O2 \(= 8\)): BIO \(= (20 + 20)/3 = 13.3\). Glucose wins, although oxygen has the higher shadow price: the price is per unit, and doubling glucose adds 10 units, doubling oxygen only 4. Both changes stay within the range where the prices hold (FER \(\ge 0\)).
