Background

Fick’s law and the diffusion equation. The flux (amount per unit area per second) is \(J = -D\,\partial c/\partial x\). A mass balance on a thin slice (rate in − rate out) gives \[\frac{\partial c}{\partial t} = D\,\frac{\partial^2 c}{\partial x^2}.\] Heat conduction is the same equation with temperature \(T\) and thermal diffusivity \(\alpha\) (water: \(\alpha \approx 1.4\cdot10^{-7}\) m²/s).
Initial and boundary conditions. A start profile \(c(x, 0)\), and at each end either a fixed value (a large reservoir, Dirichlet) or no flux, \(\partial c/\partial x = 0\) (a wall).
Time scale. Any diffusion time is a multiple of \(L^2/D\). Work in µm and s: \(10^{-9}\) m²/s \(= 1000\) µm²/s.
Grid. \(x_i = i\,\Delta x\), \(i = 0, \dots, N\), \(t_n = n\,\Delta t\), \(c_i^n \approx c(x_i, t_n)\). The second difference \((c_{i-1} - 2c_i + c_{i+1})/\Delta x^2\) from Numerical differentiation replaces \(\partial^2 c/\partial x^2\), with error \(\tfrac{\Delta x^2}{12}\,\partial^4 c/\partial x^4\).
FTCS (forward in time, centred in space) is Euler’s method (Initial value problems and Euler’s method) on the grid ODEs: \[c_i^{n+1} = c_i^n + r\,\bigl(c_{i-1}^n - 2c_i^n + c_{i+1}^n\bigr), \qquad r = \frac{D\,\Delta t}{\Delta x^2}.\] Stable only for \(r \le 1/2\). For \(r \le 1/2\) the new value is a weighted average of three old ones with non-negative weights, so no value can leave the range of the start and boundary values (maximum principle).
No-flux wall at \(x_N\): a ghost point \(c_{N+1} = c_{N-1}\) gives \(c_N^{n+1} = c_N^n + 2r\,(c_{N-1}^n - c_N^n)\).
Exact solution for the chamber below (empty at \(t = 0\), \(c = 1\) at \(x = 0\), \(c = 0\) at \(x = L\)): \[c(x, t) = 1 - \frac{x}{L} - \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \frac{2}{n\pi}\,\sin\frac{n\pi x}{L}\;e^{-n^2\pi^2 D t/L^2}.\]

The running example: a flow-free chemotaxis chamber. A straight channel of length \(L = 500\) µm joins a source reservoir (\(c = 1\), concentrations scaled by the source) to a sink reservoir (\(c = 0\)); there is no flow, so the attractant moves only by diffusion. Diffusion-based gradient devices of this kind are described in the reviews by Kim, Kim & Jeon (2010) [G1] and in Frontiers in Bioengineering and Biotechnology (2015) [G3]; our channel is a simplified version with typical, not device-specific, numbers. Small molecule: \(D = 1000\) µm²/s (\(10^{-9}\) m²/s); protein chemokine: \(D = 100\) µm²/s (\(10^{-10}\) m²/s). Grid: \(N = 50\) intervals of \(\Delta x = 10\) µm, \(r = 0.4\), so \(\Delta t = 0.04\) s.
FTCS is hand-written in all three languages: vectorised over space in R and numpy, a plain loop in Rust. The one trap: every point must be updated from the old time level. R and numpy evaluate the whole right-hand side before assigning, so this happens automatically; in Rust we copy the old level first.

