Solution — By hand





B1. (a) Left Riemann: \(1\cdot(0 + 1) = 1\). Midpoint: \(1\cdot(0.125 + 3.375) = 3.5\). Trapezoid: \(1\cdot(0 + 1 + 4) = 5\). Simpson: \(\tfrac13(0 + 4 + 8) = 4\). (b) Simpson is exact. It integrates parabolas exactly by construction, and the cubic term \((x - 1)^3\) is odd around the middle point, so it integrates to 0 and Simpson’s symmetric weights also give it 0. Simpson is exact for every cubic. (c) \(T(0.5) = 0.5\,(0 + 0.125 + 1 + 3.375 + 4) = 4.25\). The error went from 1 to 0.25, a factor 4 \(= 2^2\): order 2. (d) \((4 \cdot 4.25 - 5)/3 = 12/3 = 4\), exact. Richardson on two trapezoids with \(h\) and \(h/2\) is Simpson with \(h/2\); here it reproduces Simpson’s exact answer.
B2. (a) \(\tfrac12(0 + 6.87) = 3.435\); \(\tfrac12(6.87 + 7.16) = 7.015\); \(1\cdot(7.16 + 5.16) = 12.32\); \(2\,(5.16 + 2.33) = 14.98\); \(2\,(2.33 + 1.05) = 6.76\); \(6\,(1.05 + 0.09) = 6.84\). Total \(51.35\) mg·h/L. (b) \(2 \cdot 2.00/0.3276 = 12.21\); \(4 \cdot 2.83/0.7951 = 14.24\); \(4 \cdot 1.28/0.7971 = 6.42\); \(12 \cdot 0.96/2.4567 = 4.69\). With the two rising intervals: \(3.435 + 7.015 + 12.21 + 14.24 + 6.42 + 4.69 = 48.01\). (c) \(\lambda_z = 2.4567/12 = 0.2047\) /h (true 0.2), \(C_\text{last}/\lambda_z = 0.09/0.2047 = 0.44\). \(\mathrm{AUC}_{0-\infty} \approx 51.79\) (linear) or \(48.45\) (log-down); the extrapolated part is under 1 %. (d) The 12–24 h interval: linear 6.84 against the true 4.76, an error of \(+2.08\), almost all of the total error (\(51.35 - 49.53 = 1.82\)). The decline is exponential, strongly convex over a 12 h gap, and the straight line cuts far above it; the log-down rule assumes an exponential and gets 4.69. From 0 to 1 h both rules give 3.435 against 4.48: the absorption phase is concave and the chord lies below it. Remaining error of the log-down total (\(48.01 - 49.53 = -1.5\)) is mostly there. Add samples in the first 2 h (0.25, 0.5, 1.5 h) and one between 12 and 24 h.
B3. (a) Trapezoid: \(2.70/0.719 = 3.76\), \(0.719/0.183 = 3.93\), approaching \(4 = 2^2\): order 2. Simpson: \(0.664/0.0592 = 11.2\), \(0.0592/0.00411 = 14.4\), approaching \(16 = 2^4\): order 4. (b) Trapezoid: \(0.183\,(16/n)^2 = 10^{-6}\) gives \(n = 16\sqrt{1.83\times10^5} \approx 6800\) intervals. Simpson: \(0.00411\,(16/n)^4 = 10^{-6}\) gives \(n = 16 \cdot 4110^{1/4} \approx 16 \cdot 8.0 = 128\). (C1: Simpson with 128 intervals has error \(1.0\times10^{-6}\).)
B4. (a) \(f = 1\): \(1 + 1 = 2 = \int_{-1}^1 1\). \(f = x\): \(-\tfrac{1}{\sqrt3} + \tfrac1{\sqrt3} = 0\). \(f = x^2\): \(\tfrac13 + \tfrac13 = \tfrac23\). \(f = x^3\): \(0\) by symmetry. \(f = x^4\): \(\tfrac19 + \tfrac19 = \tfrac29 \ne \tfrac25\). Exact up to degree \(3 = 2n - 1\). (b) \((1 + 0.57735)^3 + (1 - 0.57735)^3 = 3.92450 + 0.07550 = 4.0000\): exact with 2 function values; Simpson needed 3.
B5. (a) \(10000 \cdot (1 - 0.99566) = 43.4\) and \(10000 \cdot (1 - 0.97391) = 260.9\). \(\text{dnorm}(43.4) = e^{-941.8}/2.507\), so \(\log_{10} \approx -0.4343 \cdot 941.8 - 0.4 \approx -409\). That is below the smallest double (\(\approx 10^{-323.3}\), How computers store numbers), so it is exactly 0. The other 20 points are even further out: all 21 values are 0. (b) On \([0, 20]\) the points are at \(10(1 - 0.99566) = 0.0434\), \(0.261\), … right where the density is large. (c) The closest point is at \(b \cdot 0.00217\); it is within 5 of 0 when \(b \le 5/0.00217 \approx 2300\). So the first pass sees the peak for \(b = 2000\) but not for \(b = 5000\): the closest point would be at 10.9, where dnorm is \(\approx 10^{-26}\). (C5: integrate returns 0.5 for 2000 and \(2.3\times10^{-6}\) for 5000.) The rule describes the first 21-point pass only; because QUADPACK bisects once when the first-pass estimate looks unreliable, the full routine actually works up to about \(b = 4300\) and fails from 4400 (Explore E5).
B6. (a) \(\int_0^1 \dfrac{e^{-t/(1-t)}}{(1-t)^2}\,dt\). At \(t = 0\): 1. At \(t = 0.5\) (\(x = 1\)): \(e^{-1}/0.25 = 1.47\). At \(t = 0.9\) (\(x = 9\)): \(e^{-9}/0.01 = 0.0123\). As \(t \to 1\) the exponential beats \(1/(1-t)^2\), so the integrand goes to 0: a smooth function on a finite interval. (b) \(\sqrt{x(1-x)} = \sqrt{\sin^2\theta\cos^2\theta} = \sin\theta\cos\theta\) and \(dx = 2\sin\theta\cos\theta\,d\theta\), so the integrand becomes \(2\,d\theta\) on \([0, \pi/2]\): \(2 \cdot \pi/2 = \pi\). (c) \(x = u^2\), \(dx = 2u\,du\), \(\sqrt{x} = u\): \(\int_0^1 2e^{-u^2}\,du\). Trapezoid, \(h = 0.5\): \(0.5\,(\tfrac12\cdot 2 + 2e^{-0.25} + \tfrac12\cdot 2e^{-1}) = 0.5\,(1 + 1.5576 + 0.3679) = 1.4627\), 2 % low. The original integrand is infinite at \(x = 0\), and the trapezoid rule needs \(f(0)\).









