The three answers disagree by orders of magnitude. The integrand is about \(10^{-23}\), far below abs.tol (\(1.2\times10^{-4}\) in R, \(1.5\times10^{-8}\) in SciPy), so the very first estimate passes the test, however wrong. The Rust crate, with target \(10^{-8}\), has the same problem. (b) Scaling to a maximum of 1 makes every routine agree: \(Z_g = 0.33221\), \(\log Z = -53.712\), posterior mean 1.2310 (slightly above \(\hat\lambda\), since the posterior is right-skewed). Scaling by the maximum is the log-sum-exp idea of How computers store numbers. (c) With 10 000 cells the posterior has width \(0.013\); on \((0, 10)\) and \((0, 100)\) every routine misses it, and only SciPy’s transform of \((0, \infty)\) happens to land on it (R and Rust do not). Integrating over \(\hat\lambda \pm 10\) SE gives \(0.033225\) in all three languages: \(\sqrt{100} = 10\) times smaller than \(Z_g\) in (b), as expected for a peak 10 times narrower with the same height. Give the routine the scale of the integrand: its maximum (values) and its width (axis).

A general SSA
Any network: a stoichiometry matrix S (one column per reaction) and a propensity function.
ssa &lt;- function(x, S, propensity, times) {   # S: species x reactions
  out &lt;- matrix(0, length(times), length(x)); t &lt;- 0; i &lt;- 1
  repeat {
    a &lt;- propensity(x); a0 &lt;- sum(a)
    t &lt;- t - log(runif(1)) / a0               # Inf if a0 = 0: nothing more happens
    while (i &lt;= length(times) && times[i] &lt; t) { out[i, ] &lt;- x; i &lt;- i + 1 }
    if (i &gt; length(times)) return(out)
    j &lt;- findInterval(runif(1) * a0, cumsum(a)) + 1   # reaction j w.p. a_j / a0
    x &lt;- x + S[, j]
  }
}

Records the state at fixed output times: the state before the jump is the state at every output time up to the jump.
findInterval on the cumulative propensities picks the reaction; a reaction with a_j = 0 is never chosen.


Walk through the while loop: the next event happens at time t; every output time before t sees the current state x, because nothing changes until then. When all output times are filled, return. If all propensities are zero (an absorbing state, such as an extinct population), a0 = 0, the waiting time is infinite, and all remaining output times get the final state. findInterval(v, cumsum(a)) counts how many cumulative sums are &lt;= v; adding 1 gives the reaction whose piece contains v. This is the “direct method”; the “next reaction method” (Gibson and Bruck 2000) is a faster variant for large networks.

Background
Facts used below:

Propensity. Reaction \(j\) fires in the next short time \(dt\) with probability \(a_j(\mathbf x)\,dt\), where \(\mathbf x\) is the vector of molecule counts. It changes \(\mathbf x\) by its stoichiometry (change) vector, column \(j\) of the matrix \(S\). Production at rate \(k\): \(a = k\); first-order decay of \(m\) molecules at rate \(\delta\) each: \(a = \delta m\). The ODE for the mean is \(\mathbf x' = S\,\mathbf a(\mathbf x)\); for propensities linear in \(\mathbf x\) it is exact for the mean.
Chemical master equation (CME). For the birth–death process (\(\varnothing \to M\) at rate \(k\), \(M \to \varnothing\) at rate \(\delta m\)), with \(P_n(t)\) the probability of \(n\) molecules: \[P_n' = k P_{n-1} + \delta (n+1) P_{n+1} - (k + \delta n) P_n.\] Its steady state is Poisson with mean \(k/\delta\).
Gillespie’s direct method (SSA). With \(a_0 = \sum_j a_j\) and two uniform numbers \(u_1, u_2\): \[\tau = -\frac{\log u_1}{a_0} \quad(\text{time to the next reaction}), \qquad j = \text{the reaction whose piece of } [0, a_0) \text{ contains } u_2 a_0.\] Then \(t \to t + \tau\), \(\mathbf x \to \mathbf x + S_{\cdot j}\), recompute the propensities, repeat. A \(u\) that falls exactly on a boundary goes to the next piece.
Telegraph promoter. Promoter state \(g \in \{0, 1\}\): off → on with propensity \(k_\text{on}(1 - g)\), on → off with \(k_\text{off} g\), transcription \(k_m g\). Steady-state mRNA mean and Fano factor: \[\bar m = \frac{k_m}{\delta_m}\,\frac{k_\text{on}}{k_\text{on} + k_\text{off}}, \qquad F_m = \frac{\text{var}}{\text{mean}} = 1 + \frac{k_m k_\text{off}}{(k_\text{on} + k_\text{off})(k_\text{on} + k_\text{off} + \delta_m)}.\] Constitutive transcription (always on) gives Poisson, \(F = 1\). Protein with constitutive mRNA: \(F_p = 1 + k_p/(\delta_m + \delta_p)\).
Tau-leaping. Fixed step \(\tau\): reaction \(j\) fires Poisson\((a_j \tau)\) times. Euler–Maruyama for the chemical Langevin equation \(dm = (k - \delta m)\,dt + \sqrt{k + \delta m}\,dW\): \(\;m_{n+1} = m_n + (k - \delta m_n) h + \sqrt{(k + \delta m_n) h}\; Z_n\), \(Z_n \sim N(0, 1)\).

Parameters used throughout (time in hours): constitutive gene \(k = 10\), \(\delta_m = 1\), translation \(k_p = 2\) per mRNA, protein decay \(\delta_p = 0.2\); telegraph gene \(k_\text{on} = 0.5\), \(k_\text{off} = 4.5\), \(k_m = 100\), same \(\delta_m, k_p, \delta_p\). Both have mean 10 mRNA and 100 protein molecules.
One SSA step from \(m = 10\), where both reactions have propensity 10 per hour. The three languages use different random number generators (R: Mersenne Twister; numpy: PCG64; Rust: ChaCha12), so the same seed gives different numbers: compare statistics between languages, never single trajectories. R’s runif never returns 0; numpy’s and Rust’s generators can, so Python and Rust use \(1 - u\) inside the log (also uniform).

