Solutions






Solution





B1. (a) Columns: transcription, mRNA decay, translation, protein decay. \[S = \begin{pmatrix} 1 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1 \end{pmatrix}.\] (b) \(a = (10,\ 1 \cdot 4,\ 2 \cdot 4,\ 0.2 \cdot 30) = (10, 4, 8, 6)\), \(a_0 = 28\) per hour. Probabilities \(10/28 = 0.357\), \(4/28 = 0.143\), \(8/28 = 0.286\), \(6/28 = 0.214\). Mean waiting time \(1/28\) h \(= 2.1\) min. (c) \(m' = 10 - m\), \(p' = 2m - 0.2p\). Steady state \(m = 10\), \(p = 2 \cdot 10/0.2 = 100\). (d) The promoter is off, so transcription has propensity \(100 \cdot 0 = 0\) and switching off has \(4.5 \cdot 0 = 0\). Possible: switching on (0.5), mRNA decay (4), translation (8), protein decay (6); \(a_0 = 18.5\) per hour.
B2. (a) \(a = (10, 2)\), \(a_0 = 12\). \(\tau = 0.693/12 = 0.0578\) h. \(u_2 a_0 = 3.6 &lt; 10\): transcription. \(t = 0.0578\) h, \(m = 3\). (b) \(a = (10, 3)\), \(a_0 = 13\). \(\tau = 1.609/13 = 0.1238\) h. \(u_2 a_0 = 11.7 \ge 10\): degradation. \(t = 0.1816\) h, \(m = 2\). (c) With \(m = 0\) the degradation propensity is 0, so the first reaction is certainly a transcription; \(a_0 = 10\), mean waiting time 0.1 h = 6 min. (d) Nothing: the step lengths are random waiting times set by the model. The SSA has no step-size error; its only error is the Monte Carlo error of averaging over a finite number of runs.
B3. (a) \(P_0' = \delta P_1 - k P_0 = P_1 - 2P_0\) (state 0 can only be entered from 1, and left by a transcription). \(P_1' = k P_0 + 2\delta P_2 - (k + \delta) P_1 = 2P_0 + 2P_2 - 3P_1\). (b) \(P_0' = 0 \Rightarrow P_1 = 2P_0\). \(P_1' = 0 \Rightarrow 2P_2 = 3P_1 - 2P_0 = 4P_0\), so \(P_2 = 2P_0\). In general \(P_n = \frac{2^n}{n!} P_0\); the sum is \(e^2 P_0 = 1\), so \(P_0 = e^{-2} = 0.135\), \(P_1 = 0.271\), \(P_2 = 0.271\), \(P_3 = 0.180\): Poisson(2), mean \(k/\delta = 2\). (c) \(200^3 = 8 \times 10^6\) equations (one per state). A simulation needs memory only for the current state; it samples the distribution instead of computing all of it.
B4. (a) \(g' = k_\text{on}(1 - g) - k_\text{off}\, g\) gives \(g^* = 0.5/5 = 0.1\). \(m' = k_m g - \delta_m m\) gives \(m^* = 100 \cdot 0.1 = 10\). \(p^* = 2 \cdot 10/0.2 = 100\). The same as the constitutive gene. (b) On for \(1/k_\text{off} = 0.22\) h (13 min), off for \(1/k_\text{on} = 2\) h on average. One burst makes on average \(k_m/k_\text{off} = 22.2\) mRNA. (Check: about \(1/2.22 = 0.45\) bursts per hour × 22.2 = 10 mRNA per hour, as for the constitutive gene.) (c) \(F_m = 1 + \frac{100 \cdot 4.5}{5 \cdot 6} = 1 + 15 = 16\). The constitutive gene has \(F = 1\): the same mean, 16 times the variance. (d) \(g^* = 1/10\), mean \(100 \cdot 0.1 = 10\) again; burst size \(100/9 = 11.1\); \(F_m = 1 + \frac{100 \cdot 9}{10 \cdot 11} = 9.18\). Smaller bursts, less noise; the Fano factor follows the burst size, roughly.
B5. (a) Gene A: mean 80/8 = 10; squared deviations 16, 4, 4, 16, 0, 9, 9, 0, sum 58; variance \(58/7 = 8.29\); \(F = 0.83\). Gene B: mean 80/8 = 10; squared deviations 100, 100, 441, 64, 100, 1225, 81, 81, sum 2192; variance \(313.1\); \(F = 31.3\). Gene A is consistent with Poisson (with 8 cells, \(F\) between about 0.3 and 2 is normal); gene B is far from it. (b) \(e^{-10} = 4.5 \times 10^{-5}\): in 8 cells we expect no zeros at all. Gene B has 3 cells with none: long silent periods between bursts. (c) Mean \(500\), variance \(50^2 \cdot 8.29 = 20\,700\), \(F = 41.4\). The Fano factor scales with the unit, so only molecule counts can be compared with the Poisson value 1. Fluorescence data need a calibration to molecule numbers first.
B6. (a) \(\delta m \tau = 1 \cdot 3 \cdot 1.5 = 4.5\), more than the 3 molecules there are. (b) Negative if \(N \ge 4\): \(1 - 0.342 = 0.658\). (c) Each SSA step fires one reaction, and a reaction with no molecules to act on has propensity 0 (\(\delta \cdot 0 = 0\)), so it is never chosen. (d) \(\delta \tau \le 0.1\), so \(\tau \le 0.1\) h: expected 0.3 degradations, and \(P(N \ge 4) = 2.7 \times 10^{-4}\). Not zero: with many leaps and many cells, some will still go negative. That is why practical tau-leaping codes treat species with few molecules with exact SSA steps.
B7. (a) \(\gamma/(\beta + \gamma) = 0.1/0.4 = 0.25\). (b) With probability 0.25 the first event is a recovery: extinct at once. With probability 0.75 it is an infection: now two independent chains, both of which must die out (\(q^2\)). So \(q = 0.25 + 0.75 q^2\), i.e. \(3q^2 - 4q + 1 = (3q - 1)(q - 1) = 0\), \(q = 1/3\) or \(q = 1\). The relevant root is the smaller, \(q = 1/3 = 1/R_0\). (c) \((1/3)^3 = 0.037\); \((1/3)^{10} = 1.7 \times 10^{-5}\): with 10 initial cases, the outbreak practically always takes off. (d) The ODE predicts a full epidemic (peak about 3000 infected) in every case: it has no extinction.
C1.

