B1. (a) \(\partial C/\partial C_0 = e^{-kt}\), \(\partial C/\partial k = -C_0\,t\,e^{-kt}\). (b) \(e^{-0.3} = 0.7408\), \(e^{-0.6} = 0.5488\), \(e^{-1.2} = 0.3012\): \[J = \begin{pmatrix} 0.7408 & -7.408\\ 0.5488 & -10.976\\ 0.3012 & -12.048\end{pmatrix}.\] (c) \(\frac{d}{dt}(t e^{-kt}) = (1 - kt)e^{-kt} = 0\) at \(t = 1/k = 3.3\) h. Samples near \(1/k\) carry the most information about \(k\): too early, nothing has decayed; too late, nothing is left. (The next unit makes this precise.) (d) A larger \(k_a\) moves the drug into plasma faster, so early concentrations rise; but it also brings the peak earlier, after which the drug is eliminated earlier, so late concentrations are lower.
B2. (a) As on the slide: \(\mathbf m = (7.408, 5.488, 3.012)\), \(J = (-7.408, -10.976, -12.048)\), \(\mathbf r = (-1.408, -1.788, -1.712)\), \(\sum J_ir_i = 50.68\), \(\sum J_i^2 = 320.51\), \(\delta = 0.1581\), \(k_1 = 0.4581\). (b) At \(k_1 = 0.4581\): \(\mathbf m = (6.325, 4.000, 1.600)\), \(J = (-6.325, -8.000, -6.400)\), \(\mathbf r = (-0.325, -0.300, -0.300)\), \(\sum J_ir_i = 6.374\), \(\sum J_i^2 = 144.97\), \(\delta = 0.0440\), \(k_2 = 0.5021\). RSS: \(8.111 \to 0.2854 \to 0.005870\) (minimum \(0.005236\)). (c) Errors \(0.204\), \(0.046\), \(0.0023\), then (by computer) \(4\times10^{-6}\) and \(3\times10^{-9}\): about 0, 1, 2, 5, 8 correct digits. The number of digits nearly doubles per step at the end: close to quadratic convergence, because the residuals at the minimum are small.
B3. (a) \(J = \begin{pmatrix} e^{-0.2t_1} & 1\\ \vdots & \vdots\\ e^{-0.2t_n} & 1\end{pmatrix}\): it does not depend on \((a, b)\). (b) The model is linear in \((a, b)\), so \(\mathbf m(\mathbf p + \boldsymbol\delta) = \mathbf m(\mathbf p) + J\boldsymbol\delta\) exactly. The Gauss–Newton subproblem is then the full problem, and its solution is the least-squares fit: it is linear least squares with design matrix \(J\) (Least squares: normal equations vs QR). In general, the more nearly linear the model, the faster Gauss–Newton.
B4. (a) \(J^TJ = \begin{pmatrix}3&3\\3&5\end{pmatrix}\), \(J^T\mathbf r = (2, 2)\). (b) \(\det = 6\); \(\delta_1 = (5\cdot2 - 3\cdot2)/6 = 2/3\), \(\delta_2 = (3\cdot2 - 3\cdot2)/6 = 0\). GN step \((0.667, 0)\). (c) \(\begin{pmatrix}4&3\\3&6\end{pmatrix}\), \(\det = 15\): \(\delta = (6/15, 2/15) = (0.400, 0.133)\). (d) \(\begin{pmatrix}103&3\\3&105\end{pmatrix}\), \(\det = 10806\): \(\delta = (204, 200)/10806 = (0.01888, 0.01851)\), close to \(J^T\mathbf r/100 = (0.02, 0.02)\). A large-\(\lambda\) step points along \(J^T\mathbf r\), which is minus half the gradient of the RSS: the steepest-descent direction. Its length is about \(1/\lambda\) times the gradient: short and safe. (e) Lengths \(0.667 &gt; 0.422 &gt; 0.026\). \(\lambda\) controls the step length and, with it, the direction: from the Gauss–Newton direction \((1, 0)\) to the gradient direction \((1, 1)\).
B5. (a) \(\partial m/\partial q = (\partial m/\partial k)(dk/dq) = k\,\partial m/\partial k\). (b) \(J_q = 0.3J = (-2.222, -3.293, -3.614)\); \(\sum J_{q,i} r_i = 0.3\cdot 50.68 = 15.21\); \(\sum J_{q,i}^2 = 0.09\cdot 320.51 = 28.85\); \(\delta q = 0.5271\); \(k_1 = 0.3\,e^{0.5271} = 0.5082\). Error \(0.004\), against \(0.046\) for the step in \(k\): here the log-step is better, because \(e^{-kt}\) depends on \(k\) through a product \(kt\) and the log scale fits that shape. (c) In \(k\): \(\sum J_ir_i = -8.409 - 3.189 - 0.126 = -11.72\), \(\sum J_i^2 = 4.977 + 0.992 + 0.0098 = 5.980\), \(\delta = -1.961\), \(k_1 = -0.461\): a negative elimination rate, a drug whose concentration grows by itself. In \(q\): \(J_q = 1.5J\), \(\delta q = \delta/1.5 = -1.307\), \(k_1 = 1.5\,e^{-1.307} = 0.406\), positive and on its way to \(0.504\) (next step \(0.5040\)). Log-steps are multiplicative: they can shrink \(k\) by a large factor but never through zero.
B6. (a) Write \(C = \frac{D}{V}\cdot\frac{k_a}{k_a - k_e}(e^{-k_et} - e^{-k_at})\). After the swap the fraction becomes \(\frac{k_e}{k_e - k_a}\) and the bracket \((e^{-k_at} - e^{-k_et})\); both change sign, so their product is \(\frac{k_e}{k_a - k_e}(e^{-k_et} - e^{-k_at})\). This equals the original when \(\frac{k_e}{V'} = \frac{k_a}{V}\), i.e. \(V' = V k_e/k_a\). (b) \((0.1906, 1.0151, 20.216\cdot0.1906/1.0151) = (0.1906, 1.0151, 3.796)\). (c) The amount in the gut (fast versus slow absorption), the elimination half-life (\(\ln 2/k_e\) = 3.6 h versus 0.68 h) and the volume. An IV dose has no absorption phase and measures \(k_e\) and \(V\) directly; plasma volume (about 3 L) makes \(V = 3.8\) L implausible for most drugs. Slow-release formulations do show the flip-flop (\(k_a &lt; k_e\)).
B7. (a) \(h = 10^{-8}\): \(\tfrac12 10^{-8} + 2\cdot10^{-6}/10^{-8} \approx 200\): useless (the true derivative is of order 1). \(h = 10^{-3}\): \(5\times10^{-4} + 2\times10^{-3} = 2.5\times10^{-3}\). (b) \(h^* = 2\sqrt{\varepsilon_\text{ode}} = 2\times10^{-3}\), error \(10^{-3} + 10^{-3} = 2\times10^{-3}\). For \(\varepsilon_\text{ode} = 10^{-10}\): \(h^* = 2\times10^{-5}\), error \(2\times10^{-5}\). A tight solver tolerance only helps if the step is chosen to match; the default \(h \approx 10^{-8}\) is right for model values accurate to \(10^{-16}\), not for an ODE solution. (c) \(5 \times 10 \times 36 = 1800\) solves, about 9 s. A model with 20 parameters, 100 starts and a 0.1 s solve takes hours, which is why the inner solver’s cost matters.
