With tol = 1e-2 the fitted \(k_a\) is wrong in the 3rd digit, with \(10^{-3}\) (scipy’s default rtol!) in the 4th. Worse, the reported RSS is wrong: at tol = 1e-3 R reports 0.06641, below the true minimum 0.06647; the optimiser partly fits the solver’s error. From tol = 1e-8 on, all digits shown agree with the closed-form fit. In Rust, tol = 1e-6 needs 17 iterations instead of 7: the noisy objective makes acceptance decisions erratic. (b) The solver error in the RSS is \(10^{-4}\) at tol = 1e-3, comparable with the RSS change from a 0.3 % change in \(k_a\) (\(0.0011 \times 0.3^2\)); at \(10^{-10}\) it is about \(10^{-10}\). Rule: set rtol/atol explicitly, 100–1000 times tighter than the precision you want, refit with a tighter tolerance, and keep the digits that agree. Different solvers (lsoda, RK45, Dopri5) give different numbers at loose tolerances and the same at tight ones.

