There are two minima with identical RSS (0.06646744 to all printed digits): \((1.015, 0.1906, 20.22)\) and its flip-flop mirror \((0.1906, 1.015, 3.796)\). Starts with \(k_a &gt; k_e\) mostly end in the first, starts with \(k_a &lt; k_e\) in the second. Some starts run off: \(k_a\) to \(10^{15}\) or \(10^{21}\), \(k_e\) or \(V\) to absurd values, with RSS 12–65 (64.54 = \(\sum y_i^2\) means the model predicts zero). Which starts fail differs between nls.lm (1 failure), least_squares (none) and our LM (3), because each takes different steps; always compare the RSS of the ends. The data cannot choose between the two minima; for an immediate-release tablet the usual assumption \(k_a &gt; k_e\) (and a plausible volume of 20 L rather than 3.8 L) points to the first, but that is prior knowledge, not a result of the fit.

Background
The enzyme model. One enzyme \(E\) binds substrate \(S\) to a complex \(C\), which either falls apart or turns the substrate into product \(P\): \[E + S \underset{k_r}{\overset{k_f}{\rightleftharpoons}} C \xrightarrow{k_{cat}} E + P\] With mass action and \(b = k_f E S - k_r C\) (net binding), \(c = k_{cat}C\) (catalysis): \[S' = -b, \quad E' = -b + c, \quad C' = b - c, \quad P' = c.\] We use \(k_f = 100\ \mu\text{M}^{-1}\text{s}^{-1}\), \(k_r = 100\ \text{s}^{-1}\), \(k_{cat} = 1\ \text{s}^{-1}\), \(S_0 = 10\ \mu\)M, \(E_0 = 1\ \mu\)M, \(C_0 = P_0 = 0\). Two totals are conserved: \(E + C = E_0\) and \(S + C + P = S_0\).
Facts used below:

Test equation. On \(y' = \lambda y\) a one-step method multiplies \(y\) by a fixed factor \(R(z)\) per step, with \(z = h\lambda\): Euler \(R = 1 + z\); RK4 \(R = 1 + z + z^2/2 + z^3/6 + z^4/24\); backward Euler \(R = 1/(1 - z)\). Errors do not grow if \(|R(z)| \le 1\); the set of such \(z\) is the stability region. On the negative real axis: Euler needs \(h|\lambda| \le 2\), RK4 \(h|\lambda| \le 2.785\), backward Euler nothing.
Eigenvalues set the time scales. Near a state, \(\mathbf y' = \mathbf f(\mathbf y)\) behaves like \(\mathbf y' = J\mathbf y\) with \(J\) the Jacobian (\(J_{ij} = \partial f_i / \partial y_j\), Newton for systems of equations). Each eigenvalue \(\lambda\) of \(J\) is a mode with time scale \(1/|\text{Re}\,\lambda|\). For a \(2\times2\) matrix, \(\lambda^2 - (\text{tr}\,J)\lambda + \det J = 0\); if one eigenvalue is much larger than the other, \(\lambda_\text{fast} \approx \text{tr}\,J\) and \(\lambda_\text{slow} \approx \det J / \text{tr}\,J\).
Stiff = the step size of an explicit method is limited by stability (by the fastest mode) rather than by the accuracy needed for the slow solution you care about. Typical sign: a ratio of \(10^3\) or more between the fastest and slowest time scale over the simulated time.
Backward Euler: \(\mathbf y_{n+1} = \mathbf y_n + h\,\mathbf f(\mathbf y_{n+1})\). Each step solves \(\mathbf G(\mathbf y) = \mathbf y - \mathbf y_n - h\mathbf f(\mathbf y) = \mathbf 0\) by Newton: solve \((I - hJ(\mathbf y))\,\boldsymbol\delta = -\mathbf G(\mathbf y)\), set \(\mathbf y \leftarrow \mathbf y + \boldsymbol\delta\), starting from \(\mathbf y = \mathbf y_n\). Order 1.
BDF2: \(\mathbf y_{n+1} - \tfrac43\mathbf y_n + \tfrac13\mathbf y_{n-1} = \tfrac23 h\,\mathbf f(\mathbf y_{n+1})\); Newton matrix \(I - \tfrac23 hJ\). Order 2, needs two start values.
Michaelis–Menten (QSSA): assume \(C' \approx 0\); then \(S' = -V_{\max}S/(K_m + S)\) with \(V_{\max} = k_{cat}E_0\), \(K_m = (k_r + k_{cat})/k_f\). Accurate when \(E_0 \ll S_0 + K_m\).
Library solvers. R: deSolve::ode with method = "lsoda" (default; switches between Adams and BDF), "bdf", "radau", "ode45" (explicit); step counts in attr(out, "istate") or diagnostics(out). Python: scipy.integrate.solve_ivp with method="LSODA", "BDF", "Radau", "RK45" (default, explicit); sol.t.size - 1 steps (when t_eval is not given), sol.nfev evaluations. Rust: the ode_solvers crate has only explicit methods (Dopri5, Dop853, Rk4); Dopri5 runs a stiffness test every 1000 steps and stops with an error (“the problem seems to become stiff”) when it fires repeatedly. The stiff side in Rust is hand-written backward Euler with Newton and nalgebra’s LU.

