Backward Euler is stable for all four steps, Euler only for \(h &lt; 2.5\). Only \(h = 0.5\) is accurate for either method (errors 0.07 and 0.04); at \(h = 2\) Euler even oscillates in sign. Backward Euler errs on the slow side: it always decays, but too slowly when \(h|\lambda|\) is large.

Euler: \(h \le 2/1201 = 1.67\) ms, so \(30/0.001665 = 18\,015\) steps. RK4: \(h \le 2.785/1201 = 2.32\) ms, 12 936 steps.
\(1/(1 + 120.1) = 0.0083\). The exact factor is \(e^{-120.1} \approx 10^{-52}\). Both make the fast mode vanish within a step or two, which is all we need: after the first millisecond nobody cares whether the fast transient is \(10^{-3}\) or \(10^{-52}\) of its starting size. The method is not accurate for that mode, but it is stable, and the slow mode (factor \(e^{-0.0083}\) per step) is resolved accurately.

B2. (a) \(C_{n+1} = C_n + h(2 - 0.8C_{n+1})\), so \(C_{n+1} = \dfrac{C_n + 2h}{1 + 0.8h}\).

Backward Euler: \(C_1 = 2/1.8 = 1.111\), \(C_2 = 3.111/1.8 = 1.728\). Euler: \(C_1 = 0 + 1\cdot 2 = 2\), \(C_2 = 2 + (2 - 1.6) = 2.4\). Exact: \(1.377\), \(1.995\). Both are off by similar amounts, in opposite directions (order 1).
Backward Euler: \(C_1 = 20/9 = 2.222\). Euler: \(C_1 = 0 + 10\cdot 2 = 20\). Exact \(2.499\). Euler is far off (and continuing would oscillate with factor \(1 - 8 = -7\)); backward Euler is already close to the steady state.
Put \(C_n = 2.5\): \((2.5 + 2h)/(1 + 0.8h) = 2.5\,(1 + 0.8h)/(1 + 0.8h) = 2.5\). Backward Euler finds the correct steady state for any step, and approaches it monotonically, because deviations from it shrink by \(1/(1 + 0.8h)\) per step.

B3. (a) \(y = M_0 + h(-ky^2)\), so \(G(y) = y + hky^2 - M_0 = y + y^2 - 1\), \(G'(y) = 1 + 2y\).

\(y = 1\): \(G = 1\), \(G' = 3\), \(y = 1 - 1/3 = 0.6667\). Then \(G = 0.6667 + 0.4444 - 1 = 0.1111\), \(G' = 2.3333\), \(y = 0.6667 - 0.0476 = 0.6190\).
\(y = (-1 + \sqrt{5})/2 = 0.61803\). Two iterations give 0.6190: two to three correct digits; a third iteration gives 0.618034 (quadratic convergence). The exact ODE solution is \(M(0.1) = 1/2 = 0.5\): the backward Euler step itself has a large error at this \(h\) (\(hk = 1\)), which Newton cannot fix. One Euler step gives \(1 - 0.1\cdot10\cdot1 = 0\): all monomers gone in 0.1 s.
\(G(y) = y + 2y^2 - 1 = 0\), so \(y = (-1 + \sqrt{1 + 8})/4 = 0.5\); exact \(M(0.2) = 1/3\). Euler: \(1 - 0.2\cdot10 = -1\), a negative concentration. Backward Euler stays positive (the positive root of the quadratic).
For small \(h\), \(M_{n+1}\) differs from \(M_n\) by \(O(h)\), so the start is close to the root and Newton converges quickly. A good start also matters for which root Newton finds: the quadratic has a second, negative root (\(-1.618\) in (c)).

B4. (a) \(J = \begin{pmatrix} -k_f(E_0 - C) & k_fS + k_r \\ k_f(E_0 - C) & -k_fS - k_r - k_{cat}\end{pmatrix}\). At \(S = 10\), \(C = 0\): \(J = \begin{pmatrix} -100 & 1100 \\ 100 & -1101 \end{pmatrix}\).

\(\text{tr} = -1201\), \(\det = 110\,100 - 110\,000 = 100\). \(\lambda = \tfrac12\big(-1201 \pm \sqrt{1201^2 - 400}\big) = \tfrac12(-1201 \pm 1200.834)\): \(\lambda_\text{fast} = -1200.917\), \(\lambda_\text{slow} = -0.0833\). Approximations: \(\text{tr} = -1201\) and \(\det/\text{tr} = -0.0833\). Time scales: \(1/1201 = 0.83\) ms (binding) and \(1/0.0833 = 12\) s (catalysis), ratio 14 400: stiff.
\(J = \begin{pmatrix} -100 & 100 \\ 100 & -101\end{pmatrix}\), \(\text{tr} = -201\), \(\det = 100\): \(\lambda \approx -200.5\) and \(-0.499\). Ratio about 400, still stiff over a 30 s run: Euler needs \(h &lt; 2/200.5 = 10\) ms although the solution is nearly constant.

B5. (a) \[J = \begin{pmatrix} -0.04 & 10^4y_3 & 10^4y_2 \\ 0.04 & -10^4y_3 - 6\times10^7y_2 & -10^4y_2 \\ 0 & 6\times10^7y_2 & 0\end{pmatrix}\]

At \((1, 0, 0)\) only the first column is nonzero: \(J = \begin{pmatrix}-0.04 & 0 & 0\\ 0.04 & 0 & 0\\ 0 & 0 & 0\end{pmatrix}\), eigenvalues \(-0.04, 0, 0\). Not stiff at \(t = 0\); it becomes stiff as soon as \(y_2\) and \(y_3\) grow (within about \(10^{-3}\) time units).
\(10^4y_3 = 2842\), \(10^4y_2 = 0.0919\), \(6\times10^7y_2 = 551.4\): \[J \approx \begin{pmatrix} -0.04 & 2842 & 0.0919 \\ 0.04 & -3393.4 & -0.0919 \\ 0 & 551.4 & 0\end{pmatrix}.\] Columns summing to 0 mean \((y_1 + y_2 + y_3)' = 0\) for all \(\mathbf y\), and \((1,1,1)J = \mathbf 0\): \(J\) is singular, one eigenvalue is 0. The trace \(-3393.4\) is the sum of the eigenvalues, so \(\lambda_\text{fast} \approx -3393.4 + 0.02 \approx -3393\) (the exact value is \(-3392.8\)).
\(h \lesssim 3.3/3393 \approx 10^{-3}\), so about \(40/10^{-3} = 40\,000\) steps (more early on, when the fast eigenvalue is larger). C6 finds 34 500 to 59 000.
\(I - J = \begin{pmatrix} 1.04 & -2842 & -0.0919 \\ -0.04 & 3394.4 & 0.0919 \\ 0 & -551.4 & 1\end{pmatrix}\). Its large entries are no problem for an LU solve; it is far from singular.

B6. (a) \(C_{n+1}\,(1 + \tfrac23\cdot0.8h) = \tfrac43 C_n - \tfrac13 C_{n-1}\), so with \(h = 1\): \(C_{n+1} = (\tfrac43 C_n - \tfrac13 C_{n-1})/1.5333\).

\(C_2 = (\tfrac43\cdot4.4933 - \tfrac13\cdot10)/1.5333 = (5.9911 - 3.3333)/1.5333 = 1.733\).
Backward Euler: \(C_1 = 10/1.8 = 5.556\), \(C_2 = 5.556/1.8 = 3.086\). Exact \(C_2 = 2.019\): error 1.07 for backward Euler, 0.29 for BDF2 (with an exact start value).

B7. (a) \(\omega h = 0.2618\), factor \(1/\sqrt{1.06854} = 0.9674\) per hour, \(0.9674^{24} = 0.451\) per day, \(0.451^{10} = 3.5\times10^{-4}\) after 10 days. The model oscillates for ever; backward Euler says the clock stops within a week.











