Background
To minimise a smooth function \(f(\mathbf x)\) of \(n\) variables:

Gradient \(\nabla f\): the vector of partial derivatives \(\partial f/\partial x_j\) (the Jacobian of a scalar function, written as a column). It points uphill, perpendicular to the contour lines. At a minimum, \(\nabla f = \mathbf 0\).
Hessian \(H\): the matrix of second derivatives \(H_{ij} = \partial^2 f/\partial x_i\partial x_j\), the Jacobian of the gradient. Symmetric. Near \(\mathbf x\), \(f(\mathbf x + \mathbf s) \approx f(\mathbf x) + \nabla f^\mathsf T \mathbf s + \tfrac12 \mathbf s^\mathsf T H \mathbf s\). A point with \(\nabla f = \mathbf 0\) is a minimum if \(H\) is positive definite (all eigenvalues \(&gt; 0\); for \(2\times2\): \(\det H &gt; 0\) and \(\operatorname{tr} H &gt; 0\)), a maximum if all eigenvalues are negative, a saddle if they have both signs.
Gradient of a sum of squares. If \(\mathrm{SSE}(\mathbf p) = \sum_i (y_i - m(c_i;\mathbf p))^2\), then \(\nabla\mathrm{SSE} = -2J^\mathsf T\mathbf r\), with residuals \(r_i = y_i - m(c_i)\) and the model Jacobian \(J_{ij} = \partial m(c_i)/\partial p_j\).
Steepest descent: \(\mathbf x_{k+1} = \mathbf x_k - \alpha_k \nabla f(\mathbf x_k)\). Backtracking (Armijo): start with \(\alpha = 1\) and halve until \(f(\mathbf x - \alpha\mathbf g) \le f(\mathbf x) - c\,\alpha\,\|\mathbf g\|^2\), \(c = 10^{-4}\). On a quadratic with exact line search the error shrinks by about \((\kappa - 1)/(\kappa + 1)\) per step, where \(\kappa = \lambda_{\max}/\lambda_{\min}\) is the condition number of \(H\).
Newton: solve \(H\mathbf s = -\nabla f\), set \(\mathbf x \leftarrow \mathbf x + \alpha\mathbf s\). Quadratic convergence near a minimum. \(\mathbf s\) is a descent direction only if \(\nabla f^\mathsf T\mathbf s &lt; 0\), which is guaranteed when \(H\) is positive definite.
BFGS keeps \(B \approx H^{-1}\), moves along \(-B\nabla f\), and after each step updates \(B\) from \(\mathbf s = \mathbf x_{k+1} - \mathbf x_k\) and \(\mathbf y = \nabla f_{k+1} - \nabla f_k\) so that \(B\mathbf y = \mathbf s\) (secant condition): \(B \leftarrow V^\mathsf T B V + \rho\,\mathbf s\mathbf s^\mathsf T\), \(V = I - \rho\,\mathbf y\mathbf s^\mathsf T\), \(\rho = 1/\mathbf y^\mathsf T\mathbf s\). L-BFGS stores only the last few \((\mathbf s, \mathbf y)\) pairs.
Nelder–Mead keeps \(n+1\) points (a simplex) and replaces the worst by reflecting it through the centroid \(\bar{\mathbf x}\) of the others: \(\mathbf x_r = \bar{\mathbf x} + (\bar{\mathbf x} - \mathbf x_\text{worst})\). If \(\mathbf x_r\) beats the best point, try the expansion \(\bar{\mathbf x} + 2(\bar{\mathbf x} - \mathbf x_\text{worst})\); if it is no better than the second-worst, contract towards \(\bar{\mathbf x}\).
Libraries. R: optim (default "Nelder-Mead"; "BFGS", "CG", "L-BFGS-B"; option gr for the gradient, control = list(parscale = ...) for scaling). Python: scipy.optimize.minimize (default "BFGS", jac= for the gradient; no parscale, so rescale by hand). Rust: the argmin crate (NelderMead, BFGS, LBFGS, NonlinearConjugateGradient). Gradient checks: R numDeriv::grad, jacobian and hessian; Python scipy.optimize.approx_fprime; Rust: hand-written differences.

The running example. A reporter assay: 9 doses of an agonist from 0.01 to 100 µM (half-log steps), 2 wells each, response in % of the maximum. The 4-parameter logistic model is
\[m(c) = b + \frac{t - b}{1 + (\mathrm{EC50}/c)^{h}}, \qquad \mathbf p = (b, t, \mathrm{EC50}, h) = (\text{bottom}, \text{top}, \mathrm{EC50}, \text{hill}).\]
With \(u = (\mathrm{EC50}/c)^h\) and \(D = 1 + u\) the model derivatives are \(\partial m/\partial b = u/D\), \(\partial m/\partial t = 1/D\), \(\partial m/\partial \mathrm{EC50} = -(t-b)\,h\,u/(\mathrm{EC50}\,D^2)\), \(\partial m/\partial h = -(t-b)\,u\log(\mathrm{EC50}/c)/D^2\). The best fit is \(\mathbf p^* = (5.3932,\ 96.1737,\ 1.27098,\ 1.23955)\) with \(\mathrm{SSE} = 314.92396\); the start used throughout is \(\mathbf p_0 = (0, 100, 1, 1)\).
The data, the SSE and its gradient in each language (every task below uses these definitions):

