Solutions






Solution





B1. (a) \(\nabla f = (2x_1 - 2x_2,\ -2x_1 + 4x_2 - 2)\). At \((0,0)\): \((0, -2)\). At \((3, 1)\): \((4, -4)\). (b) \(H = \begin{pmatrix} 2 & -2 \\ -2 & 4\end{pmatrix}\), constant (\(f\) is quadratic). (c) \(2x_1 - 2x_2 = 0 \Rightarrow x_1 = x_2\); then \(-2x_2 + 4x_2 = 2 \Rightarrow x_2 = 1\). The stationary point is \((1, 1)\). (d) \(\det H = 8 - 4 = 4 &gt; 0\), \(\operatorname{tr} H = 6 &gt; 0\): positive definite, a minimum. Eigenvalues \(\tfrac12(6 \pm \sqrt{36 - 16}) = 3 \pm \sqrt5 = 5.236\) and \(0.764\); \(\kappa = 5.236/0.764 = 6.85\). (e) \(H^{-1} = \tfrac14\begin{pmatrix}4 & 2\\ 2 & 2\end{pmatrix}\), \(\mathbf s = -H^{-1}(0, -2)^\mathsf T = -\tfrac14(-4, -4) = (1, 1)\). One step lands exactly on the minimum \((1,1)\), because the quadratic model of a quadratic function is the function itself.
B2. (a) \(\nabla f = (3x^2 - 3,\ 2y) = \mathbf 0\) at \((1, 0)\) and \((-1, 0)\). (b) \(H = \begin{pmatrix} 6x & 0 \\ 0 & 2\end{pmatrix}\). At \((1, 0)\): eigenvalues 6 and 2, a minimum. At \((-1, 0)\): \(-6\) and 2, a saddle. (c) At \((-0.5, 1)\): \(\nabla f = (-2.25, 2)\), \(H = \operatorname{diag}(-3, 2)\). \(\mathbf s = (-(-2.25)/(-3),\ -2/2) = (-0.75, -1)\), new point \((-1.25, 0)\): it moves away from the minimum at \(x = 1\), towards the saddle at \(x = -1\). \(\nabla f^\mathsf T\mathbf s = (-2.25)(-0.75) + 2(-1) = 1.6875 - 2 = -0.3125 &lt; 0\), so it is a descent direction here (the \(y\) part dominates), and a line search would accept a step; but along \(x\) it climbs towards the saddle. Negative curvature means the Newton step cannot be trusted, even when it happens to go downhill.
B3. (a) \(H = \operatorname{diag}(1, 9)\), \(\kappa = 9\). (b) \(\mathbf g_0 = (9, 9)\), \(\alpha_0 = (81 + 81)/(81 + 9\cdot81) = 162/810 = 0.2\), \(\mathbf x_1 = (9 - 1.8,\ 1 - 1.8) = (7.2, -0.8)\). \(\mathbf g_1 = (7.2, -7.2)\), \(\alpha_1 = 2\cdot 51.84/(51.84 + 466.56) = 0.2\), \(\mathbf x_2 = (7.2 - 1.44,\ -0.8 + 1.44) = (5.76, 0.64)\). (c) \(\mathbf g_0^\mathsf T\mathbf g_1 = 9\cdot7.2 - 9\cdot7.2 = 0\). With exact line search each new gradient is perpendicular to the previous direction: the zig-zag. (d) \(\|\mathbf x_0\| = \sqrt{82} = 9.055\), \(\|\mathbf x_1\| = \sqrt{52.48} = 7.244\), \(\|\mathbf x_2\| = \sqrt{33.59} = 5.795\): factor \(0.8\) each step, exactly \((\kappa - 1)/(\kappa + 1) = 8/10\) (this start is the worst case). (e) \(0.8^k = 10^{-6} \Rightarrow k = 6/\log_{10}(1.25) = 61.9\), so 62 steps. Newton: one step, \(\mathbf s = -H^{-1}\mathbf g_0 = (-9, -1)\), straight to \((0, 0)\).
B4. \(f(\mathbf x_0) = \tfrac12(81 + 9) = 45\), \(\|\mathbf g\|^2 = 162\). \(\alpha = 1\): \(\mathbf x = (0, -8)\), \(f = 288 &gt; 45 - 0.0162\), reject. \(\alpha = \tfrac12\): \((4.5, -3.5)\), \(f = \tfrac12(20.25 + 110.25) = 65.25\), reject. \(\alpha = \tfrac14\): \((6.75, -1.25)\), \(f = \tfrac12(45.5625 + 14.0625) = 29.81 \le 45 - 0.00405\), accept. The exact step (\(\alpha = 0.2\)) gives \(f(7.2, -0.8) = \tfrac12(51.84 + 5.76) = 28.8\). Backtracking is a little worse per step but needs only function values.
B5. (a) \(\nabla f = (x_1, 9x_2)\), so \(x_1 - \alpha x_1 = (1-\alpha)x_1\) and \(x_2 - 9\alpha x_2 = (1 - 9\alpha)x_2\). (b) Need \(|1 - \alpha| &lt; 1\) and \(|1 - 9\alpha| &lt; 1\): \(0 &lt; \alpha &lt; 2/9\), i.e. \(\alpha &lt; 2/\lambda_{\max}\). This is the stability condition of Euler’s method for \(y' = -\lambda y\) (Initial value problems and Euler’s method): steepest descent with a fixed step is Euler’s method on \(\mathbf x' = -\nabla f\). (c) Balance \(1 - \alpha = -(1 - 9\alpha) \Rightarrow \alpha = 2/10 = 0.2\); factor \(0.8 = (\kappa-1)/(\kappa+1)\) again. No fixed step does better than the condition number allows.
B6. (a) \(\mathbf x_1 = (7.2, -0.8)\): \(\nabla f(\mathbf x_1) = (7.2, -7.2)\), \(\nabla f(\mathbf x_0) = (9, 9)\), \(\mathbf y = (-1.8, -16.2)\) (\(= H\mathbf s\), as for any quadratic). \(\mathbf y^\mathsf T\mathbf s = 3.24 + 29.16 = 32.4 &gt; 0\). (b) \(V\mathbf y = \mathbf y - \rho\,\mathbf y\,(\mathbf s^\mathsf T\mathbf y) = \mathbf y - \mathbf y = \mathbf 0\). So \(B_1\mathbf y = V^\mathsf TB_0(V\mathbf y) + \rho\,\mathbf s(\mathbf s^\mathsf T\mathbf y) = \mathbf 0 + \mathbf s = \mathbf s\). (c) \(B_1 = s/y = (x_1 - x_0)/(f'(x_1) - f'(x_0))\): the inverse of the slope of \(f'\) between two points. The step \(-B_1 f'(x_1)\) is a secant step for the equation \(f'(x) = 0\).
B7. (a) \(\mathrm{EC50} = q/1000\), so \(\partial\,\mathrm{SSE}/\partial q = \tfrac1{1000}\,\partial\,\mathrm{SSE}/\partial\mathrm{EC50}\) and \(\partial^2\mathrm{SSE}/\partial q^2 = 10^{-6}\,\partial^2\mathrm{SSE}/\partial\mathrm{EC50}^2\). (b) µM: \(3701.6/14.4 \approx 257\). nM: the EC50 entry becomes \(0.0036\), so \(3701.6/0.0036 \approx 10^6\). The rough guesses have the right order of magnitude (550 and \(1.9\times10^6\)). (c) EC50: \(10^{-3}/1271 = 8\times10^{-7}\) relative; hill: \(10^{-3}/1.24 = 8\times10^{-4}\) relative. The same absolute step means very different relative steps: another reason to scale. (d) \(\partial^2\mathrm{SSE}/\partial\ell^2 \approx (\ln 10 \cdot \mathrm{EC50})^2\,\partial^2\mathrm{SSE}/\partial\mathrm{EC50}^2\) at the minimum (where the gradient term vanishes). In µM: \((2.303 \cdot 1.271)^2 \cdot 3611.5 \approx 3.1\times10^4\), and the same in nM, because \(\ell\) only shifts by 3 when the unit changes. The log parameter does not care about units.
B8. (a) \(f(1,1) = 3\), \(f(2,1) = 6\), \(f(1,2) = 9\): worst \((1, 2)\). (b) Centroid of \((1,1)\) and \((2,1)\): \((1.5, 1)\). Reflection: \((1.5, 1) + ((1.5,1) - (1,2)) = (2, 0)\), \(f = 4\). Better than the second-worst (6) but not better than the best (3): accept the reflection. New simplex \((1,1), (2,1), (2,0)\) with values 3, 6, 4. (c) Worst now \((2, 1)\); centroid of \((1,1)\) and \((2,0)\) is \((1.5, 0.5)\); reflection \((1, 0)\), \(f = 1\): better than the best (3), so try the expansion \((1.5, 0.5) + 2(-0.5, -0.5) = (0.5, -0.5)\), \(f = 0.25 + 0.5 = 0.75 &lt; 1\): accept the expansion. New simplex \((1,1), (2,0), (0.5,-0.5)\). Only comparisons of \(f\) values were needed.
