In R, Nelder–Mead needs 335 calls; BFGS and L-BFGS-B about 40 plus their finite-difference gradients; CG stops at maxit with SSE 321.2 and says so (conv 1). scipy finds the minimum with all four but reports success = False for BFGS and CG (“precision loss”: its stopping tolerance is too tight for a finite-difference gradient). In Rust, argmin’s CG stops at its iteration limit with SSE 432.5; the call counts there include the finite-difference gradient calls. (b) With EC50 = 1 nM as a start, every method ends far away (R and Rust: a flat line at the mean, hill \(-13.8\) or \(-32\), SSE 25807; scipy: a step, hill 42, SSE 9864), and R says conv 0. (argmin’s own BFGS never returned from these nM starts, because its line searches have no limit on the number of trial steps; the Rust tab therefore uses the BFGS of C5.) From EC50 = 1000 nM, R’s BFGS stops early (SSE 314.9246, 109 calls): \(\kappa = 1.9\times10^6\), and the gradient step \(10^{-3}\) is tiny for EC50. Rescaling (parscale, or optimising \(\mathbf p/\text{scale}\)) brings R back to 36 calls and the exact minimum; our Rust BFGS needs 142 instead of 196 calls. scipy’s BFGS reaches the minimum from EC50 = 1000 in every version, but with many calls and success = False. (c) \(\log_{10}\mathrm{EC50} = 3.1041\) (EC50 = 1271 nM) in 31 calls: the log takes care of units and keeps EC50 positive.

