R: Nelder–Mead reaches 314.92 from 10 of the 15 starts; of the 5 failures only one reports convergence = 1. BFGS with the analytic gradient reaches 314.92 from all 15. The other languages fail differently: scipy’s Nelder–Mead (other default tolerances and initial simplex) ends above 314.92 from 7 starts while reporting success for 6 of them, and scipy’s BFGS returns nan from every start with EC50 \(\ge 10\), because its line search tries a step with a negative EC50, where \((\mathrm{EC50}/c)^h\) is nan, and does not recover. argmin’s Nelder–Mead (with a cost that returns \(+\infty\) instead of NaN) reaches 314.92 from all 15 starts, as does our BFGS in Rust: the same method in different libraries differs in the details (initial simplex, tolerances, how NaN is handled), and those details decide the failures. Multistart reveals all of these: when the answers disagree, look closer. Fitting \(\log\mathrm{EC50}\) avoids the nan.

Background
Facts used below. Grid \(x_i = i\,\Delta x\), \(i = 0, \dots, N\); \(c_i^n \approx c(x_i, t_n)\); units µm and s. This is the node grid of the previous unit: boundary values sit on the end nodes, the \(N - 1\) interior nodes are the unknowns. The embryo (B5, C5) and the PCR wall (C6) use the same kind of nodes, but an end with no flux or heat loss is an unknown too (ghost point, fact 8), and the variable is a temperature in °C; mesh ratio \(r = D\,\Delta t/\Delta x^2\); \(A\) = tridiagonal matrix with \(-2\) on the diagonal and \(1\) beside it.

The chamber (from The diffusion (heat) equation by finite differences): a channel of length \(L = 500\) µm, small molecule \(D = 1000\) µm²/s, \(N = 50\) (\(\Delta x = 10\) µm), source \(c_0 = 1\), sink \(c_N = 0\), empty at \(t = 0\). exact(x, t, L, D) is its Fourier-series solution.
Backward Euler: \(-r\,c_{i-1}^{n+1} + (1 + 2r)\,c_i^{n+1} - r\,c_{i+1}^{n+1} = c_i^n\), i.e. \((I - rA)\,\mathbf c^{n+1} = \mathbf c^n + \mathbf b\); the boundary values enter \(\mathbf b\) as \(r c_0\) (first row) and \(r c_N\) (last row).
Crank–Nicolson: \(-\tfrac r2 c_{i-1}^{n+1} + (1 + r)\,c_i^{n+1} - \tfrac r2 c_{i+1}^{n+1} = c_i^n + \tfrac r2\bigl(c_{i-1}^n - 2c_i^n + c_{i+1}^n\bigr)\), plus \(\tfrac r2 c_0\) and \(\tfrac r2 c_N\) in the first and last rows.
Thomas thomas(a, b, c, d) from Structure: symmetric, banded, sparse: sub-diagonal (first entry unused), diagonal, super-diagonal (last entry unused), right-hand side; about \(8N\) operations.
Growth factors for a mode with \(z = 4r\sin^2(\theta/2)\) (\(\theta = \pi\): the zigzag, \(z = 4r\)): exact \(e^{-z}\), FTCS \(1 - z\), backward Euler \(1/(1 + z)\), Crank–Nicolson \((1 - z/2)/(1 + z/2)\).
Exact sine mode: with \(c = 0\) at both ends, \(c(x, 0) = \sin(\pi x/L)\) gives \(c(x, t) = e^{-D(\pi/L)^2 t}\sin(\pi x/L)\).
Error at a fixed time \(\approx C_t\,\Delta t^p + C_x\,\Delta x^2\), \(p = 1\) (backward Euler), \(p = 2\) (Crank–Nicolson). Halving a step divides its part by \(2^p\); \(\log_2\) of the error ratio is the observed order.
No-flux end (ghost point \(c_{N+1} = c_{N-1}\)): the last row becomes \(-2r\,c_{N-1} + (1 + 2r)\,c_N\) for backward Euler. Heat loss at the end, \(-k\,\partial T/\partial z = h\,(T - T_\text{air})\): ghost point \(T_{N+1} = T_{N-1} - 2\Delta z\,(h/k)\,(T_N - T_\text{air})\).
Heat obeys the same equation with the thermal diffusivity \(D = k/(\rho c_p)\) (\(k\) conductivity, \(\rho\) density, \(c_p\) heat capacity). Water: \(D \approx 1.4\times10^{-7}\) m²/s \(= 1.4\times10^5\) µm²/s.

The basic tools in each language: Thomas, one backward Euler step, one Crank–Nicolson step, and 15 steps of 4 s on the chamber. Python users can replace thomas by scipy.linalg.solve_banded; Rust has no tridiagonal solver in the allowed crates, so it is hand-written.

