Backward Euler: order 1 all the way. Crank–Nicolson on the fine grid: order 2 down to about 0.4 s, then the ratios go wild: the error reaches the space floor (about \(1.5\times10^{-6}\) for \(N = 400\)), with a dip at \(\Delta t = 0.19\) s. On the coarse grid (\(N = 50\)) the floor is \(1.2\times10^{-4}\) and the dip is at 1.5 s (\(1.3\times10^{-5}\)). The dip is cancellation: Crank–Nicolson decays the sine slightly too fast, the grid slightly too slowly, and at one step size the two errors almost cancel. A single run there looks ten times more accurate than the method is. In space: order 2 exactly (the sine is an eigenvector of \(A\), so only its decay rate is in error, by a factor \(\propto\Delta x^2\)).
