Background
The one-compartment model with first-order absorption from the gut (rate \(k_a\)), first-order elimination (rate \(k_e\)), volume \(V\), dose \(D\) and bioavailability \(F\):
\[C(t) = \frac{F\,D\,k_a}{V\,(k_a - k_e)}\left(e^{-k_e t} - e^{-k_a t}\right)\]
Facts used below:

Identifiable means: different parameter values give different model outputs. Structural non-identifiability is a property of the model (no data can fix it); practical non-identifiability is a property of the data (too few, too noisy, badly placed samples).
Jacobian. \(J_{ij} = \partial C(t_i)/\partial\theta_j\) (\(n\) samples × \(p\) parameters). In log-parameters, \(\partial C/\partial\log\theta_j = \theta_j\,\partial C/\partial\theta_j\): multiply column \(j\) by \(\theta_j\). An exact linear dependence between columns (a zero singular value) is structural non-identifiability.
Hessian. For \(S(\theta) = \sum_i r_i^2\) with \(r_i = y_i - C(t_i)\): \(H = 2J^TJ - 2\sum_i r_i\nabla^2C(t_i) \approx 2J^TJ\) (Gauss–Newton).
Covariance. With independent noise of variance \(\sigma^2\): \(\operatorname{Cov}(\hat\theta) \approx \sigma^2(J^TJ)^{-1} = 2\sigma^2 H^{-1}\), with \(\sigma^2\) estimated by \(s^2 = S(\hat\theta)/(n-p)\) if not known. Standard errors are the square roots of the diagonal; the correlation is \(r_{jk} = \operatorname{Cov}_{jk}/(\mathrm{SE}_j\,\mathrm{SE}_k)\). The Fisher information is \(\mathcal I = J^TJ/\sigma^2\).
SVD of \(J\). With \(J = U\Sigma V^T\), the uncertainty along the right singular vector \(v_i\) is \(\sigma/\sigma_i\): small singular values are badly determined directions. The eigenvalues of \(J^TJ\) are \(\sigma_i^2\).
Profile likelihood. \(\text{PL}(\theta_j) = \min\) of \(S\) over all other parameters, with \(\theta_j\) fixed. A 95 % interval is where \((\text{PL}(\theta_j) - S(\hat\theta))/s^2 \le 3.84\) (\(= 1.96^2\), the 95 % point of \(\chi^2_1\)). For a linear model this equals the Wald interval \(\hat\theta_j \pm 1.96\,\mathrm{SE}_j\).
Prediction uncertainty (delta method): for any derived quantity \(g(\theta)\), \(\operatorname{Var}g(\hat\theta) \approx \nabla g^T\operatorname{Cov}(\hat\theta)\nabla g\).
A prior \(\log\theta_j \sim N(\mu, \tau^2)\) adds the residual \((\log\theta_j - \mu)/\tau\) to the data residuals \(r_i/\sigma\). This is ridge regularisation, and the maximum a posteriori estimate.

