B1. (a) \(S/(K_m+S) = 1/3, 1/2, 5/7 = 0.333, 0.5, 0.714\). \(\partial v/\partial k_\text{cat} = E_0S/(K_m+S) = (0.0333, 0.05, 0.0714)\); \(\partial v/\partial E_0 = k_\text{cat}S/(K_m+S) = (3.33, 5, 7.14)\); \(\partial v/\partial K_m = -k_\text{cat}E_0S/(K_m+S)^2 = -(1/9, 2/16, 5/49) = (-0.111, -0.125, -0.102)\). (b) Column 2 = \((k_\text{cat}/E_0)\,\times\) column 1 = \(100\,\times\) column 1 in every row. So \(J\) has rank 2, not 3, and \(J^TJ\) (\(3\times3\)) is singular: \((J^TJ)^{-1}\) does not exist, and nls stops with “singular gradient”. (c) Both become \(k_\text{cat}\,\partial v/\partial k_\text{cat} = E_0\,\partial v/\partial E_0 = v = (0.333, 0.5, 0.714)\): identical columns. The null direction is \(v = (1, -1, 0)/\sqrt2\): raise \(\log k_\text{cat}\) and lower \(\log E_0\) by the same amount. (d) \(V_\text{max} = k_\text{cat}E_0\) and \(K_m\) are identifiable; \(k_\text{cat}\) and \(E_0\) separately are not. An independent measurement of \(E_0\) (protein concentration, active-site titration) separates them: then \(k_\text{cat} = V_\text{max}/E_0\). This is the same structure as \(F/V\) in the PK model.
B2. (a) Swapped: \(\dfrac{FDk_e}{V'(k_e - k_a)}(e^{-k_at} - e^{-k_et}) = \dfrac{FDk_e}{V'(k_a - k_e)}(e^{-k_et} - e^{-k_at})\). This equals the original for all \(t\) if \(k_e/V' = k_a/V\), i.e. \(V' = Vk_e/k_a\). (b) \((k_a, k_e, V) = (0.2, 1, 4)\). Original: prefactor \(100\cdot1/(20\cdot0.8) = 6.25\), bracket \(0.6703 - 0.1353 = 0.5350\), \(C(2) = 3.344\) mg/L. Flip-flop: prefactor \(100\cdot0.2/(4\cdot(-0.8)) = -6.25\), bracket \(e^{-2} - e^{-0.4} = -0.5350\), \(C(2) = 3.344\) mg/L. Identical. (c) After an IV bolus there is no absorption phase, and \(k_e\) alone sets the decay: half-life \(\ln2/k_e = 3.47\) h for the first set, \(0.69\) h for the second. One IV time course fixes \(k_e\), and then the oral curve fixes \(k_a\). (d) Yes: each minimum is isolated, with a well-defined bowl around it (the profile in the lecture has two separate wells). Globally the data cannot choose. A multistart lands in both minima, with exactly the same SSE: a symmetry, not a convergence failure.
