Solution





B1. (a) Divide by \(D/h^2 = 0.008\): \(R_0h^2/D = 0.25/0.008 = 31.25\). \[2c_1 - c_2 = 200 - 31.25 = 168.75,\quad -c_1 + 2c_2 - c_3 = -31.25,\quad -c_2 + 2c_3 = 168.75.\] With \(c_1 = c_3\): \(-2c_1 + 2c_2 = -31.25\), add to twice the first: \(c_2 = 337.5 - 200 = 137.5\); then \(c_1 = (168.75 + 137.5)/2 = 153.125\). So \(\mathbf c = (153.125,\ 137.5,\ 153.125)\) µM. (b) \(c(500) = 200 - 0.25\cdot500\cdot1500/4000 = 153.125\), \(c(1000) = 200 - 0.25\cdot10^6/4000 = 137.5\). Exact, because the solution is a parabola and the second difference of a parabola is exactly its second derivative (the Taylor error term contains \(c''''= 0\)). (c) The centre value is \(c_0 - R_0L^2/(8D)\); it is 0 for \(R_0 = 8Dc_0/L^2 = 8\cdot2000\cdot200/(4\times10^6) = 0.8\) µM/s, i.e. \(1.6\times10^5\) cells/cm². Above it the model gives negative oxygen in the middle: it assumes cells consume at the full rate whatever the oxygen level, which is impossible once oxygen runs out. Michaelis–Menten uptake fixes this (C6).
B2. (a) With \(s = D/h^2 = 3.84\): \(s(-c_0 + 2c_1 - c_2) = -0.6\) and \(s(-c_1 + 2c_2 - c_3) = s(-2c_1 + 2c_2) = -0.6\). (b) Divide by \(s\): \(0.6/3.84 = 0.15625\). Second equation: \(c_2 = c_1 - 0.078125\). First: \(2c_1 - c_1 + 0.078125 = c_0 - 0.15625\), so \(c_1 = c_0 - 0.234375\) and \(c_2 = c_0 - 0.3125\) mM. (c) \(V_{\max}/D = 0.001\) mM/µm². \(x = 12.5\): \(0.001\,(312.5 - 78.125) = 0.234375\). \(x = 25\): \(0.001\,(625 - 312.5) = 0.3125\). Exact again: a parabola, and the ghost point is also exact for it (the parabola is symmetric about \(x = L\)). (d) The closed end runs out when \(c_0 &lt; V_{\max}L^2/(2D) = 0.3125\) mM. That is \(0.3125\times10^{-3}\) mol/L \(\times\) 180 g/mol \(= 0.056\) g/L \(= 0.0056\) % glucose, 36 times below 0.2 %. In rich medium the drop along the trench is only 3 %. (e) \(L_{\max} = \sqrt{2Dc_0/V_{\max}} = \sqrt{2\cdot600\cdot11.1/0.6} = 149\) µm, six times the trench length.
B3. (a) \((2, 3)\): \(k = 2 + 2\cdot4 = 10\). \((3, 1)\): \(k = 3\). (b) \(k = 7\): \(j = \lfloor 6/4\rfloor + 1 = 2\), \(i = 6 \bmod 4 + 1 = 3\), so \((3, 2)\). General: \(i = (k-1) \bmod n_x + 1\), \(j = \lfloor (k-1)/n_x\rfloor + 1\). (c) 0-based \((1, 2)\), row-major position \(1\cdot n_y + 2 = 5\) (0-based), i.e. the 6th entry. In the \(x\)-fastest numbering the 6th entry is \(k = 6\), the point \((2, 2)\). So the matrix treats the oxygen at \((2,3)\) as if it were at \((2,2)\); every value is moved, and nothing complains. (d) \(x\) fastest: \(x\)-neighbours \(\pm1\), \(y\)-neighbours \(\pm n_x = \pm4\). \(y\) fastest: \(x\)-neighbours \(\pm n_y = \pm3\), \(y\)-neighbours \(\pm1\).
B4. (a) \[A = \begin{pmatrix} 4 & -1 & 0 & -1 & 0 & 0\\ -1 & 4 & -1 & 0 & -1 & 0\\ 0 & -1 & 4 & 0 & 0 & -1\\ -1 & 0 & 0 & 4 & -1 & 0\\ 0 & -1 & 0 & -1 & 4 & -1\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & 0 & -1 & 4\end{pmatrix}\] (b) The two \(3\times3\) diagonal blocks are \(T_3 + 2I = \text{tridiag}(-1, 4, -1)\) (one \(y\)-row each, coupled in \(x\)); the two off-diagonal blocks are \(-I_3\) (the coupling to the same \(x\) in the other row). (c) Corner points (\(k = 1, 3, 4, 6\)) have two edge neighbours, the middle points (\(k = 2, 5\)) one: \(\mathbf b = (2, 1, 2, 2, 1, 2)\). (d) Row sums of \(A\): \(4 - 2 = 2\) for corners, \(4 - 3 = 1\) for middle points, exactly \(\mathbf b\). Physically: with the same concentration on every edge and no sources or sinks, nothing flows, so the steady state is that constant. A solver that does not return a constant here has a bug in \(A\) or \(\mathbf b\) (C4(a)).
B5. (a) \[I_2\otimes T_2 = \begin{pmatrix} 2&-1&0&0\\-1&2&0&0\\0&0&2&-1\\0&0&-1&2\end{pmatrix},\quad T_2\otimes I_2 = \begin{pmatrix} 2&0&-1&0\\0&2&0&-1\\-1&0&2&0\\0&-1&0&2\end{pmatrix},\quad \text{sum} = \begin{pmatrix} 4&-1&-1&0\\-1&4&0&-1\\-1&0&4&-1\\0&-1&-1&4\end{pmatrix}.\] (b) Each point has two interior neighbours and two edge neighbours. With all four equal: \(\frac{D}{h^2}(4c - 2c) = \frac{D}{h^2}\,2c_0 - R_0\), so \(c = c_0 - R_0h^2/(2D) = 200 - 0.5\cdot250\,000/4000 = 168.75\) µM. (c) For \(n_x = n_y\) the two orders give the same matrix (both terms are present, only their order in the sum changes), so it cannot matter. For \(3 \times 2\), \(I_2\otimes T_3 + T_2\otimes I_3\) and \(T_3\otimes I_2 + I_3\otimes T_2\) are different \(6\times6\) matrices (C5).
B6. (a) \(F'(c) = 0.004 + 1/(1+c)^2\). (b) \(c = 200\): \(F = 0 + 200/201 = 0.99502\), \(F' = 0.004 + 1/201^2 = 0.0040248\), so \(c_1 = 200 - 247.23 = -47.23\). Then \(F(-47.23) = 0.004\cdot(-247.23) + (-47.23)/(-46.23) = -0.98891 + 1.02164 = 0.03273\), \(F' = 0.004 + 1/46.23^2 = 0.004468\), \(c_2 = -47.23 - 7.33 = -54.55\). (Then \(-54.659\).) (c) \(c = 0\): \(F = -0.8\), \(F' = 1.004\), \(c_1 = 0.7968\). \(F(0.7968) = -0.79681 + 0.44345 = -0.35336\), \(F' = 0.004 + 1/1.7968^2 = 0.31374\), \(c_2 = 0.7968 + 1.1263 = 1.9231\). (Then 3.034, 3.580, 3.658, 3.659.) (d) \(F(3.659) = 0.004\cdot(-196.341) + 3.659/4.659 = -0.78536 + 0.78536 \approx 0\); \(F(-54.659) = -1.01864 + 54.659/53.659 = -1.01864 + 1.01864 \approx 0\). The oxygen level is 3.66 µM: positive, and below the hypoxia threshold of 10 µM, because the uptake (\(\approx 0.79\) µM/s) almost matches what diffusion can deliver. The negative root exists because the formula \(c/(K_m + c)\) is defined for \(c &lt; -K_m\) too, where it is positive: “negative oxygen” is consumed. The first Newton step from 200 sees almost no dependence of uptake on \(c\) (\(F'\) is nearly the constant 0.004), so it jumps to the constant-uptake answer \(-50\), near the spurious root. Exactly what happens in 2D (lecture, C6).
B7. (a) With \(s = D/h^2\) and \(d_k = R_0K_m/(K_m + c_k)^2\): \[J = \begin{pmatrix} 4s + d_1 & -s & -s & 0\\ -s & 4s + d_2 & 0 & -s\\ -s & 0 & 4s + d_3 & -s\\ 0 & -s & -s & 4s + d_4\end{pmatrix}.\] (b) \(\partial r(c_k)/\partial c_m = 0\) for \(m \ne k\): uptake at a point depends only on the oxygen at that point. If uptake also depended on the neighbours’ oxygen, the entries \((k, m)\) for neighbours \(m\) would change, which are already non-zero, so the sparsity pattern would still be that of \(A\). (c) \(J = A + \operatorname{diag}(d)\) with \(d_k &gt; 0\). Symmetric because both terms are. For any \(\mathbf v \ne \mathbf 0\), \(\mathbf v^TJ\mathbf v = \mathbf v^TA\mathbf v + \sum_k d_kv_k^2 &gt; 0\), since the first term is already positive. So Cholesky (solve on a symmetric sparse matrix) and CG both work at every Newton step.
B8. (a) \(h = 50\): \(N = 3081\), \(n_x = 79\), nnz(\(L\)) \(\approx 243\,000\) (table: 240 317). \(h = 12.5\): \(N = 50\,721\), \(n_x = 319\), nnz(\(L\)) \(\approx 1.62\times10^7\) (table: 16 129 277). About 130 MB of values. (b) \(50\,721 \times 319^2 \approx 5.2\times10^9\) operations: a few seconds. With reordering the lecture’s solve took 0.16 s. (c) \(N = 10^6\), bandwidth \(n_xn_y = 10^4\), nnz(\(L\)) \(\approx 10^{10}\), 80 GB; cost \(N(n_xn_y)^2 = 10^{14}\) operations. Reordering helps (to roughly \(N^{4/3}\) non-zeros in 3D) but 3D problems of this size are usually solved iteratively (Iterative linear solvers in depth).
