(Rust prints the closed-end values in scientific notation; the digits agree.) In rich medium (0.2 % glucose) the concentration at the closed end is 10.79 mM, 97 % of the channel value, and Newton converges in one step because uptake is practically constant at concentrations 2000 times \(K_m\). The closed end only starves when the channel concentration drops to about 0.1 mM, near the B2(d) threshold of 0.31 mM (at 0.3 mM the end is at 0.021 mM, still above \(K_m\), because Michaelis–Menten uptake slows down as \(c\) falls). At 0.1 mM the deepest 11.5 µm of the trench are below \(K_m\). With clipping, Newton needs at most 7 steps; without it, the dilute cases can converge to negative concentrations as in B6.
