// D = 600 um^2/s: closed end 10.788 mM, Lmax = 149 um
// D =  60 um^2/s: closed end 7.977 mM, Lmax = 47 um
// D =   6 um^2/s: closed end 0.000 mM, Lmax = 15 um




Even with \(D\) reduced tenfold, the closed end still has 8 mM. Only a hundredfold reduction (6 µm²/s) starves it, and \(L_{\max}\) shows why: it falls below the 25 µm trench length only then.
Consistent: the model says glucose diffusion along a 25 µm trench cannot limit growth in rich medium unless diffusion through the packed cells were about 100 times slower than in water, or the trench several times longer. That matches the conclusion of [B1] that nutrient supply was not the limiting factor. (We have read only the abstract of [B1]; check the paper for what exactly was measured.)
The model answers: “can diffusion of a nutrient with these parameters supply the closed end?” It cannot answer “why do the cells grow slowly?”, because it contains no mechanics (pressure, cells pushing against each other and the walls), no other nutrients or waste products, and no feedback from growth on uptake. A model can rule out an explanation within its assumptions; it cannot point to an explanation that it does not contain. Rule-of-thumb check before building any model: write down the alternatives it cannot represent.

