With the mixed order the check error is of order 30–80 (relative!) instead of \(2\times10^{-3}\), and the “oxygen” reaches 1500–2300 µM, far above the 200 µM on the edges. That is impossible with pure uptake (maximum principle). R’s mixed version and the Python/Rust version are mirror images of each other (matrix wrong vs vectors wrong), so their numbers differ; both are equally wrong. No language gives a warning: every size matches.
On a square grid the two orders give the same matrix (B5(c)), so the “bug” changes nothing: check and answer are fine. The bug sleeps until someone changes the chamber to a rectangle or uses different \(h\) in \(x\) and \(y\). Lesson: test on a rectangle, with a field that differs in \(x\) and \(y\), and check bounds on the solution.

