Plain Newton converges (12 steps, residual below \(10^{-8}\)) to a field with \(\min c = -27.6\) µM: a root of the equations, not a possible oxygen level (B6). (c) With clipping: \(10^5\) cells/cm² is well oxygenated (min 87 µM, 3 steps); at \(2\times10^5\) the centre drops to 0.56 µM and 10 % of the chamber is hypoxic; at \(3\times10^5\), 25 %. The three languages agree. Rust uses CG for every Newton step (110 to 200 CG iterations each; --release makes it fast), R and Python a sparse direct solve. In R, forceSymmetric makes solve use sparse Cholesky; in Python spsolve uses sparse LU (SuperLU) with a fill-reducing column ordering.

Background
The model. A hydrogel slab, \(L = 1000\) µm thick, sits on a glass bottom with cells embedded in it. A drug in the medium above diffuses in (\(D = 100\) µm²/s) and the cells take it up (\(k = 0.04\) s⁻¹). The steady concentration (relative to the bath) solves \[-(D\,u')' + k\,u = f, \qquad u(0) = 1, \qquad u'(L) = 0,\] with \(f = 0\). The exact solution is \(u(x) = \cosh\big((L-x)/\lambda\big)/\cosh(L/\lambda)\), \(\lambda = \sqrt{D/k} = 50\) µm.
Facts used below:

Hat functions. On nodes \(x_0 &lt; x_1 &lt; \dots &lt; x_N\) (any spacing), \(\varphi_j\) is 1 at \(x_j\), 0 at all other nodes and linear in between. \(u_h(x) = \sum_j u_j\varphi_j(x)\) is the piecewise linear function through the points \((x_j, u_j)\); its integral is exactly the trapezoid sum.
Weak form. Multiply by \(\varphi_i\), integrate, integrate by parts. The boundary term is the flux and vanishes at a no-flux end (natural boundary condition: nothing to do). At \(x = 0\) the value is known, so there is no equation for \(u_0\). Result: \(K\mathbf u = \mathbf b\) with \(K_{ij} = \int D\varphi_i'\varphi_j' + k\varphi_i\varphi_j\,dx\), \(b_i = \int f\varphi_i\,dx\).
Element matrices for an element of length \(h_e\) (constant \(D\), \(k\), \(f\) on it): \[\frac{D}{h_e}\begin{pmatrix} 1 & -1\\ -1 & 1\end{pmatrix} + \frac{k h_e}{6}\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 1\\ 1 & 2\end{pmatrix}, \qquad \frac{f h_e}{2}\begin{pmatrix}1\\1\end{pmatrix}.\] Lumped version: replace the uptake block by \(\frac{k h_e}{2}\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\0&1\end{pmatrix}\).
Assembly. Add the block of element \(e\) into rows and columns \(e, e+1\). Then move the column of the known \(u_0\) to the right-hand side and drop row 0. The result is tridiagonal and symmetric: solve with the Thomas algorithm (from Structure: symmetric, banded, sparse).
Graded mesh. \(x_i = L\,(i/N)^2\) puts short elements near \(x = 0\) (first one \(L/N^2\)) and long ones at depth.

The solver in each language. R and Rust use the Thomas algorithm; Python uses scipy.linalg.solve_banded, which takes the three diagonals in a 3-row array. D, k and f may be single numbers or one value per element.

