\((1, 2, 2, 2, 2, 1)\) and \(-1\): the B2(a) pattern. (b) The lumped matrix (diagonal 6, off-diagonal \(-1\); last row 3) is exactly the finite-difference matrix times \(h\). The consistent matrix has diagonal \(2 + 2kh/3 = 4.67\) and off-diagonal \(-1 + kh/6 = -0.33\) (in units of \(D/h = 1\)): part of the uptake sits on the neighbours. (c) On this coarse mesh the lumped (finite-difference) version is more accurate (0.036 against 0.064). Neither is good: both meshes are too coarse for a 50 µm layer, and the “better” matrix is not a substitute for more nodes where they are needed.










