Both meshes are second order (observed \(p = 2.006\) and \(2.000\)); the graded mesh is about 4.5 times more accurate at every \(N\), so the graded mesh with 10 elements (0.0139) beats the uniform one with 20 (0.0169). With 10 uniform elements the amount of drug in the gel is 57.7 instead of 50 (15 % too high: the straight line from 1 to 0.07 over the first 100 µm lies far above the true curve); on the graded mesh it is 50.8. Grading costs nothing: the same number of unknowns and the same tridiagonal solve. This is the 1D version of what mesh generators and adaptive refinement do in 2D and 3D.









