The consistent matrix gives negative minima for \(h/\lambda = 10, 5, 3.3, 2.5\), all above \(\sqrt6 \approx 2.45\) (B3(d)); at \(h/\lambda = 2\) every value is positive. The lumped (finite-difference) matrix stays positive at every \(h\). The check that finds this in your own work: look at the minimum and maximum of the solution (a concentration must stay between its boundary values here, the maximum principle) and refine.
The exact interface value is \(1/3\): the coating is a straight-line drop from 1 to \(u(100)\), and the gel below takes up the drug as if it were an infinitely deep slab. With a node at the interface the error falls by 4 per doubling (second order: \(1.5\cdot10^{-3}\), \(3.7\cdot10^{-4}\), \(9.3\cdot10^{-5}\)). Without one, it only halves (first order: \(7.5\cdot10^{-3}\), \(4.1\cdot10^{-3}\), \(2.1\cdot10^{-3}\)), because the element that straddles the interface has the wrong \(k\) on part of its length, and \(u'\) has a kink inside it that a straight line cannot follow. Rule: put mesh nodes (in 2D, mesh edges) on material interfaces. Mesh generators do this when the interface is part of the geometry you draw.

Background
With a flow of speed \(u\) the flux of a solute is \(J = u\,c - D\,\partial c/\partial x\), and the mass balance gives the advection–diffusion equation
\[\frac{\partial c}{\partial t} + u\,\frac{\partial c}{\partial x} = D\,\frac{\partial^2 c}{\partial x^2}.\]
We work in µm and s: \(D = 1000\) µm²/s is \(10^{-9}\) m²/s (a small molecule), \(D = 100\) µm²/s a protein.
Facts used below:

Péclet number \(\text{Pe} = uL/D\): advection against diffusion over a length \(L\). Across a channel of width \(w\) at mean speed \(\bar u\), streams mix after a mixing length of order \(\bar u w^2/D = \text{Pe}\cdot w\). Call the streams mixed to 1 % when every \(|c - \tfrac12| \le 0.01\). For two half-streams in plug flow this happens at \(x \approx 0.42\,\bar u w^2/D\) (from the slowest Fourier mode); with the Poiseuille profile of Fact 7 it happens somewhat earlier (about \(0.31\,\bar u w^2/D\) for the T-sensor, Explore E5), so \(0.42\,\bar u w^2/D\) is a slightly cautious design estimate.
Back-diffusion test problem. Steady \(u\,c' = D\,c''\) on \([0, L]\) with \(c(0) = 0\), \(c(L) = 1\): \(c(x) = \dfrac{e^{\text{Pe}\,x/L} - 1}{e^{\text{Pe}} - 1}\), a boundary layer of thickness \(D/u\) at \(x = L\). We use \(L = 500\) µm, \(u = 100\) µm/s, \(D = 1000\) µm²/s, so Pe = 50.
Central differences, with the cell Péclet number \(P = u\Delta x/D\): \(\left(1 + \tfrac P2\right)c_{i-1} - 2c_i + \left(1 - \tfrac P2\right)c_{i+1} = 0\). Second order; wiggles if \(P &gt; 2\).
Upwind differences (\(u &gt; 0\)): \((1 + P)\,c_{i-1} - (2 + P)\,c_i + c_{i+1} = 0\). First order, never wiggles, and exactly the central scheme with \(D\) replaced by \(D + u\Delta x/2\) (numerical diffusion).
Explicit upwind in time: \(c_i^{n+1} = (C + r)\,c_{i-1}^n + (1 - C - 2r)\,c_i^n + r\,c_{i+1}^n\) with Courant number \(C = u\Delta t/\Delta x\) and \(r = D\Delta t/\Delta x^2\). Stable (no new extremes) if \(C + 2r \le 1\); for \(D = 0\) this is the CFL condition \(C \le 1\). Numerical diffusion \(u\Delta x(1 - C)/2\).
Marching. Steady flow along a channel, profile \(u(y)\), Pe ≫ 1: drop diffusion along \(x\) and solve \(u(y)\,\partial c/\partial x = D\,\partial^2 c/\partial y^2\) as if \(x\) were time. Grid across the channel: \(M\) cells of width \(\Delta y\), centres \(y_j = (j - \tfrac12)\Delta y\). This cell-centred grid differs on purpose from the node grid \(x_i = i\Delta x\) of The diffusion (heat) equation by finite differences and of Steady state in two dimensions: no unknown sits on a wall where \(u = 0\) (the “time step” \(\Delta x/u_j\) would be infinite there), and the no-flux wall is a mirror halfway between the last cell and its ghost cell (\(c_0 = c_1\)). The 1D problems along a channel (Facts 2–5) keep the node grid \(x_i = i\Delta x\). Backward Euler in \(x\) with \(a_j = D\Delta x/(u_j\Delta y^2)\): \(-a_j c_{j-1}^{k+1} + (1 + 2a_j)\,c_j^{k+1} - a_j c_{j+1}^{k+1} = c_j^k\), with \(1 + a_j\) on the diagonal in the two wall rows (no flux). One tridiagonal solve per step.
Given flow profile (Poiseuille): \(u(y) = 6\bar u\,(y/w)(1 - y/w)\), zero at the walls, \(1.5\bar u\) in the middle. The solute flux \(\sum_j u_j c_j\) is the same at every \(x\).

The basic tool: the steady 1D problem with both schemes. The tridiagonal system is solved with solve (Python: numpy.linalg.solve; the Rust version uses the hand-written Thomas algorithm thomas(a, b, c, d) from Implicit schemes and the method of lines). The exact solution uses expm1 (Python: np.expm1, Rust: f64::exp_m1).

