\(D = 398\) µm²/s against the true 400: the noise allows a few µm²/s either way. Every evaluation of the objective runs the PDE solver (40 tridiagonal solves); Brent needs 19 of them. (b) The grid matters more than the noise: a single 1 mm step gives 505 (27 % above the converged value), \(\Delta x = 250\) µm gives 419, and the estimate settles near 396 only for \(\Delta x \le 25\) µm. Backward Euler damps a mode with rate \(\lambda\) by \(1/(1 + \lambda\Delta x)\) per step, less than the exact \(e^{-\lambda\Delta x}\): a coarse step spreads the interface too little, and the fit compensates with a larger \(D\). The solver’s error is absorbed into the parameter. Refine until the fitted parameter stops changing. (c) \(D = 318.56 = 0.8 \times 398.20\): the data fix only \(D/\bar u\). A 20 % error in the flow rate is a 20 % error in \(D\), whatever the quality of the profile.

Background
Facts used below:

Synthesis–diffusion–degradation (units µm and min): \(\partial c/\partial t = D\,\partial^2 c/\partial x^2 - k\,c\) on \(0 &lt; x &lt; L\), influx \(j\) at \(x = 0\) (\(-D\,\partial c/\partial x = j\)), wall at \(x = L\) (\(\partial c/\partial x = 0\)). We use \(D = 100\) µm²/min, \(k = 0.01\) per min, \(L = 500\) µm, \(j = D/\lambda = 1\).
Steady state: \(c(x) = \dfrac{j\lambda}{D}\,\dfrac{\cosh\bigl((L - x)/\lambda\bigr)}{\sinh(L/\lambda)}\) with \(\lambda = \sqrt{D/k}\); in a long embryo simply \(c(x) = \tfrac{j\lambda}{D}e^{-x/\lambda}\). Mass balance: \(k\int_0^L c\,dx = j\).
Grid: nodes \(x_i = i\,\Delta x\), \(i = 0, \dots, N\). Ghost points: \(c_{-1} = c_1 + 2\Delta x\,j/D\) and \(c_{N+1} = c_{N-1}\). With \(r = D/\Delta x^2\), the operator \(-D\,c''\) becomes a tridiagonal matrix \(K_D\) with diagonal \(2r\), off-diagonals \(-r\), and \(-2r\) in the first row (above the diagonal) and in the last row (below the diagonal); the influx gives the right-hand side \(2j/\Delta x\) in row 0.
IMEX Euler: \((I + \Delta t\,K_D)\,\mathbf c^{n+1} = \mathbf c^n + \Delta t\,\bigl(\mathbf f(\mathbf c^n) + \mathbf s\bigr)\). On one Fourier mode with \(f = -kc\) it multiplies by \(G = (1 - k\Delta t)/(1 + Dq^2\Delta t)\).
Splitting: Lie does \(\Delta t\) of diffusion, then \(\Delta t\) of reaction; Strang does \(\Delta t/2\) reaction, \(\Delta t\) diffusion, \(\Delta t/2\) reaction. With exact sub-steps the error comes from \(\mathcal{AB} - \mathcal{BA}\): Lie is order 1, Strang order 2.
Schnakenberg model (dimensionless): \(u_t = a - u + u^2 v + \nabla^2 u\), \(v_t = b - u^2 v + d\,\nabla^2 v\), with \(a = 0.1\), \(b = 0.9\). A perturbation \(\boldsymbol\delta\cos(qx)\) of the uniform state grows or decays with the eigenvalues of \(J - q^2\,\mathrm{diag}(1, d)\), \(J\) = Jacobian of the reaction.
Periodic 2D grid: the five-point Laplacian has eigenvalues \(-\kappa_{kl}\) with \(\kappa_{kl} = \frac{4}{\Delta x^2}\bigl(\sin^2\frac{\pi k}{N} + \sin^2\frac{\pi l}{N}\bigr)\) on the 2D Fourier modes, so an implicit diffusion step is: FFT, divide by \(1 + \Delta t\,D\,\kappa\), inverse FFT.
Euler stability for a reaction eigenvalue \(\lambda\): \(|1 + \Delta t\,\lambda| \le 1\).

The set-up in each language. thomas() is the function from Structure: symmetric, banded, sparse (Python: it copies b and d). The variables lo, di, up (diagonals of \(K_D\)), src, cs (steady state) and the function imex() are used again in C2 and C3.

