The numerical accumulation times agree with \((1 + x/\lambda)/(2k)\) to 0.4 %; the small differences come from the finite embryo (the formula is for an infinitely long one) and from the time step. Far from the source the gradient takes three times as long as at the source.
The IMEX fixed point satisfies \(\mathbf c = (I + \Delta t K_D)^{-1}(\mathbf c + \Delta t(-k\mathbf c + \mathbf s))\), i.e. \(K_D\mathbf c + k\mathbf c = \mathbf s\): \(\Delta t\) cancels. Any step gives the right steady state, which is convenient, and also means that a converged steady state says nothing about whether the transient was accurate.

