Solutions






Solution





B1. (a) \(A = \begin{pmatrix}4&-1&-1&0\\-1&4&0&-1\\-1&0&4&-1\\0&-1&-1&4\end{pmatrix}\). Each row: \(4(0.5) - 0.5 - 0.5 = 1\). ✓ (b) By symmetry all four values stay equal, \(x \leftarrow (1 + 2x)/4\): \(0 \to 0.25 \to 0.375 \to 0.4375\). Errors \(0.5, 0.25, 0.125, 0.0625\): the factor is \(\tfrac12\) per step. (c) Sweep 1: \(x_1 = 1/4 = 0.25\), \(x_2 = (1 + 0.25 + 0)/4 = 0.3125\), \(x_3 = 0.3125\), \(x_4 = (1 + 0.3125 + 0.3125)/4 = 0.40625\). Sweep 2: \(x_1 = (1 + 0.625)/4 = 0.40625\), \(x_2 = x_3 = (1 + 0.40625 + 0.40625)/4 = 0.453125\), \(x_4 = (1 + 0.90625)/4 = 0.4765625\). Errors of point 4: \(0.09375\), then \(0.0234375\): ratio \(\tfrac14\). (d) \(\cos(\pi/3) = 0.5 = \rho_J\) and \(\cos^2(\pi/3) = 0.25 = \rho_{GS}\): one Gauss–Seidel sweep is worth two Jacobi steps.
B2. (a) \(D = 2I\), so \(T_J = \begin{pmatrix}0&\frac12&0\\\frac12&0&\frac12\\0&\frac12&0\end{pmatrix}\). \(T_J(1, \sqrt2, 1)^T = (\tfrac{\sqrt2}{2}, 1, \tfrac{\sqrt2}{2})^T = \tfrac{1}{\sqrt2}(1, \sqrt2, 1)^T\): eigenvalue \(1/\sqrt2\). \((1, 0, -1)\) gives 0, \((1, -\sqrt2, 1)\) gives \(-1/\sqrt2\). Sum 0 = trace. ✓ (b) \(\rho = 1/\sqrt2 = 0.7071 = \cos(\pi/4)\). Steps per digit: \(\ln 10 / (-\ln 0.7071) = 2.3026 / 0.3466 = 6.6\). (c) \(-\ln(0.998795) = 0.0012057\), so \(2.3026/0.0012057 = 1910\) sweeps per digit, about \(11\,460\) for six (the measured count is 11 302). Gauss–Seidel: \(\rho^2\), half as many: about 955 per digit, \(5730\) for six (measured 5652). (d) \(\begin{pmatrix}1&2\\2&1\end{pmatrix}\): \(T_J = \begin{pmatrix}0&-2\\-2&0\end{pmatrix}\), eigenvalues \(\pm2\), \(\rho = 2 &gt; 1\): the error doubles each step. \(\begin{pmatrix}2&1\\1&2\end{pmatrix}\): \(T_J = \begin{pmatrix}0&-\frac12\\-\frac12&0\end{pmatrix}\), \(\rho = \tfrac12\). The second matrix is diagonally dominant, the first is not.
B3. (a) \(m = 127\): CG \(\approx 200\), Jacobi \(\approx 45\,200\). \(m = 255\): CG \(\approx 400\), Jacobi \(\approx 181\,000\). (Measured: CG 203 and 409; Jacobi 45 193 at \(m = 127\).) (b) \(10n = 6.5\times10^5\) operations per iteration. CG: \(400 \times 6.5\times10^5 \approx 2.6\times10^8\) (a fraction of a second compiled). Jacobi: \(181\,000 \times 6.5\times10^5 \approx 1.2\times10^{11}\), about 500 times more. (c) \(h = 1/101\), between the 2D runs at \(m = 63\) and \(127\): about 200 iterations (a run in R gives 203). Work: \(200 \times 2 \times 7\times10^6 \approx 3\times10^9\) operations, seconds. Memory: \(7\times10^6 \times 12\) bytes \(= 84\) MB for \(A\), plus \(4 \times 8\) MB for vectors: about 120 MB. The Cholesky factor: \(6\times10^8 \times 12 \approx 7\) GB, roughly 60 times more.
B4. (a) \(10^{-6} \times 0.9988 / 0.0012 \approx 8.3\times10^{-4}\): the iterate is still about \(10^{-3}\) away, 830 times more than the step. (The lecture’s run: error \(8.29\times10^{-4}\).) (b) \(\ln 1000 / (-\ln 0.9988) = 6.908/0.0012007 \approx 5750\) more sweeps. (c) The residual measures how well the equations are satisfied, independently of how fast the method moves; a slow method has small steps but not a small residual. Still, relative error \(\le \kappa(A) \times\) relative residual (Pivoting, norms and conditioning), and here \(\kappa \approx 1659\), so a residual of \(10^{-6}\) guarantees only about \(2\times10^{-3}\) relative error in the worst case.
B5. (a) Trace 101, determinant 99: \(\lambda = (101 \pm \sqrt{101^2 - 4\cdot99})/2 = (101 \pm 99.02)/2\), so \(100.01\) and \(0.990\), \(\kappa \approx 101\). \(D^{-1/2}AD^{-1/2} = \begin{pmatrix}1&0.1\\0.1&1\end{pmatrix}\), eigenvalues \(1.1\) and \(0.9\), \(\kappa = 1.22\). The diagonal scaling removes the bad scaling of the first unknown. (b) \(D^{-1/2}AD^{-1/2} = A/4\): same eigenvectors, same \(\kappa\). Nothing changes; CG takes exactly the same steps (C4 confirms).
